window.LIVE_BOOKS={"speakup-a2-b1":{"title":"SpeakUp English A2 → B1","lessons":[{"number":1,"title":"Getting Acquainted","content":"LESSON 1 Getting Acquainted\nCAN-DO GOAL: Introduce yourself and answer simple personal questions.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your name?\n2. Where are you from?\n3. What do you do?\n4. What do you enjoy doing?\n5. Tell us one interesting fact about yourself.\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ likes coffee\n☐ has two brothers or sisters\n☐ enjoys football\n☐ speaks another language\n☐ loves travelling\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Getting Acquainted\nWORD EXAMPLE\nhobby My hobby is reading.\nhometown I come from Hargeisa.\noccupation I'm a teacher.\nmarried I'm married.\nsingle I'm single.\noutgoing She's very outgoing and easy to talk to.\nUseful Expressions\n• Nice to meet you.\n• What do you do?\n• Could you tell me about yourself?\n• Really?\n• That's interesting.\n• How about you?\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\noccupation • engineer • business • photographer • hometown\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nSarah's Introduction\nREAD\nHi! My name is Sarah. I'm twenty-eight years old. I live in London, but my hometown is \nManchester. I work as a nurse. In my free time I enjoy hiking and reading mystery books. I also \nlove travelling because I enjoy meeting new people.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Where does Sarah live?\n2. What is her job?\n3. What are her hobbies?\n4. Why does she enjoy travelling?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Simple — talking about yourself\nUse the present simple to talk about facts, routines, and things that are generally true about you: \nwork, habits, and preferences.\nExamples\n• I work as a nurse.\n• I live in London.\n• I enjoy hiking.\n• She comes from Hargeisa.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ in ______.\n2. My job ______.\n3. I enjoy ______.\n4. I usually ______ at the weekend.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE\nYou are attending an international conference. Introduce yourself to five classmates.\nTry to cover:\n• their job\n• hobbies\n• family\n• favourite food\n• dream destination\nDo NOT write answers. Remember them.\nChallenge\nIntroduce one classmate to everyone else.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new words\n☐ how to introduce myself\n☐ how to ask follow-up questions\n☐ how to keep a conversation going\nHOMEWORK\nRecord a 2-minute self-introduction. OR Interview one family member in English.\n\n"},{"number":2,"title":"Work & Careers","content":"LESSON 2 Work & Careers\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about your job, your daily routine, and what you're doing these days.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your job, or what job would you like?\n2. What time do you usually start work?\n3. What are you working on these days?\n4. Do you prefer working alone or in a team?\n5. What's the best part of your job?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ works in an office\n☐ is studying for a new job\n☐ works at the weekend\n☐ wants to change jobs\n☐ works with computers\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Work & Careers\nWORD EXAMPLE\ncolleague My colleague helps me with reports.\ndeadline The deadline is Friday.\napply for I applied for a new job.\nshift I work the morning shift.\npromotion She got a promotion last year.\ncurrently I'm currently training a new employee.\nUseful Expressions\n• I work as a...\n• I'm currently...\n• At the moment, I'm...\n• My job involves...\n• I usually..., but this week I'm...\n• It's a nine-to-five job.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ncolleague • deadline • promotion • currently • employee\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA Normal Week, A Busy Week\nREAD\nAmina works as an accountant. She usually starts at nine and finishes at five. This week is \ndifferent: she's preparing a big report, so she's staying late every day. Her colleague is helping \nher, and they're meeting the deadline on Friday. Next week, things go back to normal.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What is Amina's job?\n2. What does she usually do?\n3. What is she doing this week, and why?\n4. When does the deadline finish?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Simple vs Present Continuous\nUse the present simple for routines and permanent facts. Use the present continuous (am/is/are + -\ning) for actions happening now or around this time.\nExamples\n• I work as a teacher. (routine)\n• I'm preparing a report this week. (temporary)\n• She usually finishes at five.\n• They're meeting the deadline on Friday.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I usually ______ (start) work at ______.\n2. This week I ______ (work) on a new project.\n3. She ______ (not/normally/work) at weekends.\n4. Right now, they ______ (finish) the report.\nMake It Personal — write two sentences about your usual routine and two about what's different this \nweek.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nA DAY IN YOUR JOB\nInterview a partner about their work or studies — their usual routine and what's different this \nweek.\nTry to cover:\n• what they usually do\n• what they're doing today or this week\n• something they enjoy about it\n• something they find difficult\n• a change they'd like to make\nAsk at least two follow-up questions.\nChallenge\nGuess your partner's job from three clues before they tell you.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new work words\n☐ present simple for routines\n☐ present continuous for now\n☐ how to talk about my job\nHOMEWORK\nWrite six sentences about your working week: three routine, three happening now.\n\n"},{"number":3,"title":"Travel & Adventure","content":"LESSON 3 Travel & Adventure\nCAN-DO GOAL: Tell a simple story about a past trip.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Where did you last go on a trip?\n2. How did you travel — by car, plane, or bus?\n3. What did you pack?\n4. Did anything go wrong?\n5. What's a place you'd love to visit?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has been on a plane\n☐ has travelled alone\n☐ has visited another country\n☐ has camped outside\n☐ got lost on a trip once\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Travel & Adventure\nWORD EXAMPLE\nluggage I lost my luggage at the airport.\nbook (a ticket) We booked our tickets online.\ndelay Our flight had a two-hour delay.\nsouvenir I bought a souvenir for my sister.\naccommodation The accommodation was cheap but clean.\narrive We arrived late at night.\nUseful Expressions\n• We set off early.\n• We finally arrived...\n• On the way, we...\n• It took us...\n• We stayed at...\n• The best part was...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nluggage • accommodation • souvenir • delay • arrive\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA Trip to Zeila\nREAD\nLast summer, Hodan and her cousins travelled to Zeila by car. They left early in the morning \nand stopped twice for tea. They arrived at midday and swam in the sea all afternoon. In the \nevening, they bought souvenirs at the small market. Hodan says it was the best trip of her life.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. How did Hodan travel to Zeila?\n2. What did they do on the way?\n3. What did they do in the afternoon?\n4. What did Hodan buy?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPast Simple — regular and irregular verbs\nUse the past simple to talk about finished actions in the past. Regular verbs add -ed (arrived, \nstopped); many common verbs are irregular (went, bought, saw).\nExamples\n• They travelled to Zeila by car.\n• They arrived at midday.\n• She bought souvenirs at the market.\n• We saw the sea for the first time.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. We ______ (travel) to the coast last year.\n2. I ______ (buy) a gift for my mother.\n3. They ______ (not/stay) very long.\n4. ______ you ______ (enjoy) the trip?\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about a real trip you took.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nMY BEST TRIP\nTell your group about a real (or imagined) trip, from start to finish.\nTry to cover:\n• how you travelled\n• where you stayed\n• one thing that went well\n• one thing that went wrong\n• the best moment\nListen and ask your partner one question about their trip.\nChallenge\nVote on the most exciting trip in your group.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new travel words\n☐ past simple regular verbs\n☐ past simple irregular verbs\n☐ how to tell a simple story\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short paragraph (6–8 sentences) about a memorable trip.\n\n"},{"number":4,"title":"Technology & Social Media","content":"LESSON 4 Technology & Social Media\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about how often you use technology and social media.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How often do you check your phone?\n2. Which app do you use the most?\n3. Do you ever take a break from social media?\n4. What do you usually post online?\n5. Is technology more helpful or more distracting for you?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ checks their phone every hour\n☐ has more than 3 social media apps\n☐ watches videos before bed\n☐ has deleted an app on purpose\n☐ prefers calling to texting\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Technology & Social Media\nWORD EXAMPLE\napp I downloaded a new app.\nupdate My phone needs an update.\nscreen time My screen time is too high.\nscroll I scroll through my feed every morning.\nnotification I turned off my notifications.\nconnection The wifi connection is slow.\nUseful Expressions\n• I'm always on my phone.\n• I hardly ever...\n• I check it first thing in the morning.\n• I try to cut down on...\n• It's become a habit.\n• I can't live without it.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nnotification • connection • application • usually • occasionally\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nYusuf's Phone Habits\nREAD\nYusuf always checks his phone as soon as he wakes up. He usually scrolls through the news for \nten minutes. He often messages his friends during the day, but he rarely posts photos. In the \nevening, he sometimes watches videos, but he never uses his phone after ten o'clock — he says \nit helps him sleep better.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What does Yusuf do as soon as he wakes up?\n2. How often does he post photos?\n3. What does he sometimes do in the evening?\n4. Why doesn't he use his phone after ten?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nAdverbs of Frequency + Present Simple\nAdverbs of frequency (always, usually, often, sometimes, rarely, never) go before the main verb, but \nafter the verb 'be'. Use them with the present simple to say how often something happens.\nExamples\n• He always checks his phone.\n• She is rarely late.\n• I often message my friends.\n• We never use phones in class.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (always/check) my messages in the morning.\n2. She ______ (rarely/post) photos online.\n3. They ______ (never/be) without their phones.\n4. We ______ (sometimes/watch) videos at lunch.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about your own technology habits using different adverbs.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nA DAY WITHOUT YOUR PHONE\nDiscuss with your group: could you survive a full day without your phone?\nTry to cover:\n• how often you actually use your phone\n• what you would miss the most\n• what you wouldn't miss at all\n• one habit you'd like to change\n• a rule your family has about technology\nUse at least three adverbs of frequency.\nChallenge\nAs a group, agree on three rules for healthy phone use.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new technology words\n☐ adverbs of frequency\n☐ word order with adverbs\n☐ how to talk about habits\nHOMEWORK\nTrack your screen time for one day and write five sentences about it.\n\n"},{"number":5,"title":"Health & Wellbeing","content":"LESSON 5 Health & Wellbeing\nCAN-DO GOAL: Give simple advice about health and lifestyle.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How many hours do you usually sleep?\n2. Do you think you eat healthily?\n3. How do you relax after a stressful day?\n4. Do you exercise regularly?\n5. What's one habit you'd like to improve?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ drinks 8 glasses of water a day\n☐ exercises 3 times a week\n☐ sleeps less than 6 hours\n☐ never skips breakfast\n☐ knows a good home remedy\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Health & Wellbeing\nWORD EXAMPLE\nexhausted I feel exhausted after work.\nstressed She's stressed about the exam.\nsymptom A headache is a common symptom.\nrest You need to rest.\ndiet He has a healthy diet.\nhabit Sleeping late is a bad habit.\nUseful Expressions\n• You should really...\n• You shouldn't...\n• Have you tried...?\n• It might help if you...\n• I always feel better when I...\n• Take it easy.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nexhausted • symptom • habit • stressed • remedy\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nFeeling Tired All the Time\nREAD\nAhmed feels exhausted every afternoon. He sleeps only five hours a night and drinks a lot of \ncoffee. He rarely exercises and often eats fast food for lunch. His doctor says he should sleep \nmore, drink more water, and take short walks during the day. Ahmed knows he shouldn't \nignore this advice much longer.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Why does Ahmed feel exhausted?\n2. How much does he sleep?\n3. What does the doctor advise?\n4. What does Ahmed know he shouldn't do?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nShould / Shouldn't — giving advice\nUse should + base verb to give advice or say something is a good idea. Use shouldn't to say \nsomething is a bad idea.\nExamples\n• You should drink more water.\n• You shouldn't skip breakfast.\n• He should see a doctor.\n• We shouldn't stay up too late.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. You ______ (sleep) more if you're tired.\n2. She ______ (not/skip) meals.\n3. ______ I ______ (see) a doctor about this?\n4. They ______ (exercise) more often.\nMake It Personal — write four pieces of health advice for yourself.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE HEALTH CLINIC\nTake turns: one person describes how they've been feeling, the other gives advice.\nTry to cover:\n• how you've been sleeping\n• how you've been eating\n• your stress levels\n• your exercise habits\n• one change you should make\nGive at least three pieces of advice using should/shouldn't.\nChallenge\nGive your partner one piece of advice they haven't heard before.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new health words\n☐ should for advice\n☐ shouldn't for warnings\n☐ how to talk about wellbeing\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five pieces of advice for a friend who feels stressed.\n\n"},{"number":6,"title":"Food & Culture","content":"LESSON 6 Food & Culture\nCAN-DO GOAL: Compare food, dishes, and eating habits.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your favourite dish from home?\n2. Is home cooking better than restaurant food?\n3. What's the spiciest food you've eaten?\n4. Do you prefer sweet or savoury food?\n5. What food do you never eat?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ cooks every day\n☐ doesn't like spicy food\n☐ has tried food from 3 countries\n☐ prefers tea to coffee\n☐ can't cook at all\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Food & Culture\nWORD EXAMPLE\nflavour This soup has a great flavour.\ningredient Rice is the main ingredient.\nspicy This sauce is very spicy.\nfresh The vegetables are always fresh.\nrecipe She shared her recipe with me.\nportion The portions here are huge.\nUseful Expressions\n• It tastes amazing.\n• It's a bit too spicy for me.\n• This is healthier than that.\n• It's the best dish I've tried.\n• I could eat this every day.\n• It's an acquired taste.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ningredient • flavour • recipe • delicious • portion\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nTwo Restaurants, Two Styles\nREAD\nThe new café in town is more expensive than the old market stall, but the food is fresher. The \nmarket stall is busier and noisier than the café, and the portions are bigger. Most people say the \ncafé's coffee is the best in town, but the market stall's sambusas are the tastiest snack around.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Which place is more expensive?\n2. Which place is busier?\n3. Where can you find the biggest portions?\n4. What is the tastiest snack, according to the text?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nComparatives and Superlatives\nUse comparatives (+er / more) to compare two things, and superlatives (the +est / the most) to \ncompare three or more things.\nExamples\n• The café is more expensive than the stall.\n• The market is noisier than the café.\n• This is the best dish on the menu.\n• That's the spiciest sauce I've tried.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. This restaurant is ______ (cheap) than that one.\n2. It's the ______ (good) coffee in town.\n3. Home cooking is ______ (healthy) than fast food.\n4. This is the ______ (spicy) dish I've ever eaten.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences comparing two dishes or restaurants you know.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE FOOD DEBATE\nIn pairs, compare two restaurants, dishes, or cooking styles you both know.\nTry to cover:\n• which is cheaper / more expensive\n• which is tastier\n• which is healthier\n• which has better service\n• which you'd recommend, and why\nUse at least three comparatives or superlatives.\nChallenge\nAgree together on “the best dish in town” and be ready to defend your choice.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new food words\n☐ comparative adjectives\n☐ superlative adjectives\n☐ how to compare things\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short review comparing two meals you've had recently.\n\n"},{"number":7,"title":"Education & Learning","content":"LESSON 7 Education & Learning\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about a memory from your school days.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What was your favourite subject at school?\n2. Who was your favourite teacher, and why?\n3. Did anything funny ever happen in class?\n4. What did you find difficult to learn?\n5. What's one thing school didn't teach you, but should have?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ studied a language at school\n☐ had a strict teacher\n☐ once got in trouble in class\n☐ enjoyed maths\n☐ learned something outside school that changed them\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Education & Learning\nWORD EXAMPLE\nsubject Maths was my favourite subject.\nstrict Our teacher was very strict.\nexam I was nervous before the exam.\nclassmate My classmate helped me study.\nmemorise We had to memorise the poem.\nskill Reading is an important skill.\nUseful Expressions\n• I'll never forget when...\n• I used to struggle with...\n• Looking back, I...\n• It was such a funny moment.\n• I was terrified of...\n• That taught me a lot.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nsubject • classmate • memorise • strict • exam\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Exam Day\nREAD\nLayla was studying for her final exam when the lights suddenly went out. While she was \nreading her notes by candlelight, her sister was cooking dinner in the next room. Layla was still \npractising formulas when her father arrived home. In the end, she passed the exam — and \nnever forgot that strange evening.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What was Layla doing when the lights went out?\n2. What was her sister doing at the same time?\n3. What was Layla doing when her father arrived?\n4. What happened in the end?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPast Simple vs Past Continuous\nUse the past continuous (was/were + -ing) for an action in progress, and the past simple for the \nshorter action that interrupted it, or for a sequence of finished actions.\nExamples\n• She was studying when the lights went out.\n• While he was reading, his phone rang.\n• They were talking when the teacher walked in.\n• I finished my homework and went to bed.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (study) when my friend ______ (call).\n2. While she ______ (write) the exam, the fire alarm ______ (ring).\n3. We ______ (walk) home when it ______ (start) to rain.\n4. He ______ (not/listen) when the teacher explained it.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about something that happened while you were doing something\nelse.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nSCHOOL DAYS STORY\nTell your group a true (or almost true) story from your school days.\nTry to cover:\n• what you were doing\n• what suddenly happened\n• how you felt\n• what happened next\n• what you learned from it\nUse the past continuous at least twice.\nChallenge\nVote on the most memorable story in your group.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new education words\n☐ past continuous form\n☐ interrupted past actions\n☐ how to tell a story with two timelines\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short story (6–8 sentences) about a memorable school day.\n\n"},{"number":8,"title":"Money & Business","content":"LESSON 8 Money & Business\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about future plans for money and business ideas.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Do you have a plan for saving money?\n2. Would you like to start your own business one day?\n3. What's a business idea you think could work here?\n4. Do you prefer saving or spending?\n5. What's the best investment you've ever made?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ is saving for something specific\n☐ has a business idea\n☐ sells something online\n☐ has a budget\n☐ wants to invest in property\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Money & Business\nWORD EXAMPLE\nbudget I'm trying to stick to a budget.\ninvest She wants to invest in a small shop.\nprofit The business made a good profit.\nloan He took out a loan to start the shop.\ncustomer The customer was very happy.\nafford I can't afford a new phone right now.\nUseful Expressions\n• I'm planning to...\n• I'm going to save up for...\n• It's a risky investment.\n• It should pay off eventually.\n• I can't really afford it.\n• It's worth the money.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nbudget • invest • customer • afford • profit\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA New Small Business\nREAD\nFaisal is going to open a small phone-repair shop next month. He's going to rent a small space \nnear the market and buy basic tools first. He isn't going to hire anyone yet — he's going to run \nit alone for the first few months. If it goes well, he's going to save money and open a second shop\nnext year.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What is Faisal going to open?\n2. Where is he going to rent space?\n3. Is he going to hire anyone at first?\n4. What is his plan if the business goes well?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nFuture Plans with Going to\nUse going to + base verb to talk about future plans and intentions — decisions already made before \nthe moment of speaking.\nExamples\n• I'm going to save some money this month.\n• She's going to open a shop.\n• We aren't going to spend a lot.\n• Are you going to invest in that?\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (save) money for a new phone.\n2. They ______ (not/open) the shop this year.\n3. ______ you ______ (start) your own business one day?\n4. He ______ (buy) new equipment next month.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about your own money or business plans.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nPITCH YOUR BUSINESS\nIn pairs, invent a small business idea and pitch it to your partner.\nTry to cover:\n• what the business is going to sell\n• who your customers are going to be\n• where you're going to open it\n• how you're going to get money to start\n• your plan for the first year\nUse going to at least four times.\nChallenge\nYour partner decides whether they'd invest — and explains why.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new money words\n☐ going to for plans\n☐ how to make predictions\n☐ how to pitch an idea\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five sentences about your real financial plans for this year.\n\n"},{"number":9,"title":"Environment & Climate","content":"LESSON 9 Environment & Climate\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about rules and responsibilities for protecting the environment.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Do you recycle at home?\n2. What environmental problem worries you the most?\n3. Is it hot or dry where you live at the moment?\n4. Do you use plastic bags often?\n5. What's one small change that could help the environment?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ recycles regularly\n☐ has planted a tree\n☐ avoids plastic bags\n☐ walks instead of driving sometimes\n☐ has picked up litter in public\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Environment & Climate\nWORD EXAMPLE\nrecycle We recycle paper and plastic.\npollution Air pollution is a big problem.\nwaste Try not to waste water.\nrubbish Don't drop rubbish in the street.\nresponsibility It's everyone's responsibility.\nreduce We should reduce plastic use.\nUseful Expressions\n• We have to...\n• We don't have to..., but it helps.\n• It's everyone's responsibility to...\n• Small changes make a big difference.\n• It's against the rules to...\n• We're required to...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\npollution • recycle • responsibility • reduce • rubbish\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nRules at Green School\nREAD\nAt Green School, students have to recycle paper and plastic every day. They don't have to bring \ntheir own water bottles, but most students do. Teachers have to switch off lights when they \nleave a room, and everyone has to help keep the schoolyard clean. Students don't have to plant \ntrees, but the whole school plants one together every year.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What do students have to do every day?\n2. Do students have to bring water bottles?\n3. What do teachers have to do?\n4. What does the whole school do every year?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nHave to / Don't have to — obligation\nUse have to for rules and obligations (it's necessary). Use don't have to when something is not \nnecessary — there's a choice.\nExamples\n• We have to recycle at school.\n• You don't have to bring a bottle, but it helps.\n• She has to switch off the lights.\n• They don't have to plant trees every week.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. We ______ (have to/recycle) our rubbish.\n2. You ______ (not/have to/bring) your own bag.\n3. ______ he ______ (have to/walk) to school?\n4. Everyone ______ (have to/help) keep it clean.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about rules you have to or don't have to follow.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nOUR GREEN RULES\nIn groups, design five environmental rules for your school, workplace, or neighbourhood.\nTry to cover:\n• one rule everyone has to follow\n• one thing people don't have to do, but should\n• who is responsible for each rule\n• how to encourage people to follow them\n• one consequence for breaking a rule\nUse have to / don't have to for every rule.\n\nChallenge\nPresent your five rules to another group and vote on the best one.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new environment words\n☐ have to for obligation\n☐ don't have to for choice\n☐ how to talk about rules\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five environmental rules for your own home.\n\n"},{"number":10,"title":"Relationships & Family","content":"LESSON 10 Relationships & Family\nCAN-DO GOAL: Describe people and relationships in your family.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How many people are in your family?\n2. Who are you closest to in your family?\n3. Who do you look like the most?\n4. What's a family tradition you enjoy?\n5. Who taught you the most important lesson in your life?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has more than 4 siblings\n☐ lives with grandparents\n☐ is the eldest child\n☐ has a family business\n☐ has a twin\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Relationships & Family\nWORD EXAMPLE\nsibling I have three siblings.\nrelative She's a distant relative of mine.\nraise My grandparents helped raise me.\nclose (to someone) I'm very close to my aunt.\nin-law My mother-in-law is very kind.\nresemble I resemble my father a lot.\nUseful Expressions\n• We get along really well.\n• We don't see each other often.\n• She's the one who...\n• He's like a second father to me.\n• We're very close.\n• I take after my mother.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nsibling • relative • resemble • raise • in-law\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Person Who Raised Me\nREAD\nMy grandmother is the person who raised me. She's the woman who taught me to cook and the \nperson who I go to for advice. My cousin, who lives next door, is the friend that I trust the most. \nMy family is not big, but it's the family that I wouldn't trade for anything.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Who is the person who raised the writer?\n2. What did the grandmother teach?\n3. Who does the writer trust the most?\n4. How does the writer feel about their family?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nRelative Clauses — who, which, that\nUse who for people, which for things, and that for either. A relative clause adds extra information \nabout the noun right before it, without starting a new sentence.\nExamples\n• She's the woman who raised me.\n• That's the book that changed my life.\n• He's the cousin who lives next door.\n• This is the tradition which my family loves.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. She's the aunt ______ taught me to cook.\n2. That's the house ______ I grew up in.\n3. He's the friend ______ I trust the most.\n4. This is a tradition ______ my family keeps every year.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about people in your family using who/which/that.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nFAMILY PORTRAIT\nDescribe three important people in your life to your partner, using relative clauses.\nTry to cover:\n• who they are to you\n• one thing they taught you\n• a memory that shows what they're like\n• someone you resemble\n• a tradition your family keeps\nUse who, which, or that at least four times.\nChallenge\nYour partner draws a simple family tree from what you say.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new family words\n☐ relative clauses with who\n☐ relative clauses with which/that\n☐ how to describe people\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short paragraph describing three people in your family.\n\n"},{"number":11,"title":"Media & News","content":"LESSON 11 Media & News\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about abilities now and in the past.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How do you usually get the news?\n2. Could you name three news topics from this week?\n3. Do you trust social media news?\n4. Could you live without checking the news for a week?\n5. What language could you follow the news in as a child?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ reads news in more than one language\n☐ can spot fake news easily\n☐ watches news every day\n☐ could recite the news headlines today\n☐ prefers newspapers to phones\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Media & News\nWORD EXAMPLE\nheadline The headline caught my attention.\nsource Always check the source.\nreliable Is this a reliable website?\nreport She reported live from the scene.\nbreaking news It was breaking news last night.\nfake news Fake news spreads quickly online.\nUseful Expressions\n• Did you hear about...?\n• I read somewhere that...\n• According to the news...\n• I can't believe everything I read.\n• That story is all over the news.\n• I couldn't find a reliable source.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nheadline • reliable • source • report • breaking\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Young Reporter\nREAD\nWhen Nasra was ten, she couldn't read the newspaper alone — her father read it to her every \nmorning. Now she can read three newspapers a day and can spot a fake headline in seconds. She\ncould always tell a good story, and now she can write one, too. Next year, she'll be able to report \nthe news herself.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What couldn't Nasra do when she was ten?\n2. What can she do now?\n3. What could she always do well?\n4. What will she be able to do next year?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nCan / Could — ability now and in the past\nUse can for ability in the present, and could for ability in the past. Use be able to when you need \nother tenses.\nExamples\n• She can read three newspapers a day.\n• She couldn't read alone as a child.\n• I can spot fake news quickly.\n• He could always tell a good story.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (can/read) the news in two languages.\n2. She ______ (not/can/write) well as a child.\n3. ______ you ______ (can/name) today's top story?\n4. We ______ (can/check) the source before sharing.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about things you can do now and couldn't do before.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHEN AND NOW\nInterview your partner about abilities they had as a child and abilities they have now.\nTry to cover:\n• something they could do as a child\n• something they couldn't do then\n• something they can do now\n• how they check if news is reliable\n• one skill they'd like to have\nUse can and could at least three times each.\nChallenge\nShare the most surprising “then and now” fact you heard.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new media words\n☐ can for present ability\n☐ could for past ability\n☐ how to talk about skills\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five sentences comparing what you could do at age 10 and what you can do now.\n\n"},{"number":12,"title":"Sports & Fitness","content":"LESSON 12 Sports & Fitness\nCAN-DO GOAL: Compare sports and describe how people play them.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your favourite sport to watch or play?\n2. Do you exercise as often as you'd like?\n3. Who is the fastest runner you know?\n4. Do you play any sport as well as a professional?\n5. What sport looks easy but is actually hard?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ plays a team sport\n☐ runs regularly\n☐ watches football every week\n☐ has competed in a competition\n☐ prefers watching sport to playing it\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Sports & Fitness\nWORD EXAMPLE\nopponent She beat her opponent easily.\nskilful He's a skilful player.\nfitness Fitness is important to her.\ntrain They train every morning.\ncompete We compete every weekend.\neffort She always plays with great effort.\nUseful Expressions\n• He plays really well.\n• She's as good as any professional.\n• They train harder than us.\n• It takes a lot of effort.\n• He runs incredibly fast.\n• They lost, but they tried hard.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nopponent • skilful • compete • fitness • effort\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nTwo Runners\nREAD\nIdil runs as fast as her brother, but she trains far more seriously than he does. She wakes up \nearly and runs carefully and patiently every morning, while her brother trains occasionally, \nwhenever he feels like it. Idil doesn't run as often as a professional athlete, but she competes as \nconfidently as one — and she always plays fairly.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. How does Idil's speed compare to her brother's?\n2. How often does her brother train?\n3. How does Idil run each morning?\n4. How does Idil compete?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nAs...as + Adverbs of Manner\nUse as + adjective/adverb + as to say two things are equal. Adverbs of manner (usually adjective + -\nly) describe how an action is done.\nExamples\n• She runs as fast as her brother.\n• He trains as seriously as a professional.\n• They played carefully and calmly.\n• She competes confidently.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. He plays ______ (good) as his coach.\n2. She runs ______ (quick).\n3. They don't train ______ (hard) as we do.\n4. He answered the question ______ (calm).\nMake It Personal — write four sentences comparing yourself to someone else using as...as, or describing \nhow you do something.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nSPORTS COMMENTATOR\nTake turns commentating on an imaginary match, comparing two players or teams.\nTry to cover:\n• how fast/skilful each player is\n• who trains harder\n• who plays more fairly\n• a comparison using as...as\n• your prediction for the result\nUse at least two adverbs of manner and one as...as comparison.\nChallenge\nThe listeners guess which team you support from your commentary.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new sports words\n☐ as...as comparisons\n☐ adverbs of manner\n☐ how to describe how people do things\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five sentences comparing your fitness habits to a friend's.\n\n"},{"number":13,"title":"City vs Countryside","content":"LESSON 13 City vs Countryside\nCAN-DO GOAL: Compare life in the city and the countryside.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Do you live in a city, a town, or the countryside?\n2. How much traffic is there where you live?\n3. Would you like to live somewhere quieter, or busier?\n4. How many parks are near your home?\n5. What do you miss most about the countryside, or the city?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has lived in both a city and a village\n☐ prefers quiet places\n☐ spends a lot of time in traffic\n☐ has grown vegetables\n☐ would move to the countryside tomorrow\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About City vs Countryside\nWORD EXAMPLE\ncrowded The streets are always crowded.\npeaceful The village is very peaceful.\ntraffic There's a lot of traffic at 5pm.\nfacility The city has more facilities.\nlandscape The countryside has a beautiful landscape.\ncommunity It's a small, friendly community.\nUseful Expressions\n• It's much quieter here.\n• There isn't much to do.\n• There's so much traffic.\n• There aren't many shops nearby.\n• It's a close community.\n• I wouldn't trade it for the city.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ncrowded • peaceful • facility • community • landscape\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nTwo Different Lives\nREAD\nIn the city, there's a lot of traffic and not much peace and quiet. There are many shops and \nfacilities, but there isn't much green space. In the countryside, there isn't much traffic, and \nthere's a lot of fresh air. There aren't many shops nearby, but there's a strong sense of \ncommunity. Both places have a little of everything — just not the same things.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What is there a lot of in the city?\n2. What isn't there much of in the city?\n3. What is there a lot of in the countryside?\n4. What does the countryside have a strong sense of?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nQuantifiers — much, many, a lot of, a few, a little\nUse much and a little with uncountable nouns (traffic, space); use many and a few with countable \nnouns (shops, parks). A lot of works with both.\nExamples\n• There isn't much traffic here.\n• There are many shops in the city.\n• There's a little green space.\n• There are a few parks nearby.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. There isn't ______ traffic in the village.\n2. There are ______ facilities in this town.\n3. We have ______ time before the bus arrives.\n4. There are only ______ shops open on Sunday.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about your area using different quantifiers.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nCITY MOUSE, COUNTRY MOUSE\nIn pairs, one person argues for city life, the other for countryside life.\nTry to cover:\n• traffic and transport\n• facilities and shops\n• peace and quiet\n• community and neighbours\n• which one you'd choose, and why\nUse at least four quantifiers (much/many/a lot of/a few/a little).\nChallenge\nLet a third classmate decide who made the stronger argument.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new place words\n☐ quantifiers with countable nouns\n☐ quantifiers with uncountable nouns\n☐ how to compare places\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five sentences comparing your area to somewhere very different.\n\n"},{"number":14,"title":"Dreams & Ambitions","content":"LESSON 14 Dreams & Ambitions\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about your dreams and ambitions for the future.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What did you want to be when you were young?\n2. What would you like to achieve in the next five years?\n3. Is there a skill you want to learn?\n4. Who inspires your ambitions?\n5. What's stopping you from starting today?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ wants to start a business\n☐ wants to live abroad someday\n☐ wants to learn a new language\n☐ has a five-year plan\n☐ wanted a different job as a child\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Dreams & Ambitions\nWORD EXAMPLE\nambition Her ambition is to become a doctor.\nachieve I want to achieve my goals.\ngoal My main goal this year is...\ninspire My mother inspires me.\npursue He decided to pursue his dream.\ndetermined She's very determined.\nUseful Expressions\n• I'd like to...\n• One day, I want to...\n• My biggest ambition is...\n• I'm determined to...\n• It's always been my dream to...\n• I hope to achieve...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nambition • achieve • determined • pursue • inspire\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nAmran's Big Dream\nREAD\nAmran wants to become an engineer one day. She'd like to study abroad and then come home to\nbuild schools in small villages. She wants to help children who don't have easy access to \neducation. Her father inspires her the most — he never finished school, but he always \nencouraged her to dream big. She's determined to make him proud.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What does Amran want to become?\n2. What would she like to do abroad?\n3. Who does she want to help?\n4. Who inspires her the most?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nWould like to / Want to + infinitive\nUse want to + verb for things you desire. Use would like to + verb for the same idea, but more polite \nand often used to talk about future wishes.\nExamples\n• I want to become an engineer.\n• She'd like to study abroad.\n• We want to open a school.\n• They'd like to travel more.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (want to/learn) a new skill this year.\n2. She ______ (would like to/live) abroad one day.\n3. We ______ (not/want to/wait) any longer.\n4. ______ you ______ (would like to/change) your job?\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about your own ambitions.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nFIVE YEARS FROM NOW\nDescribe your life five years from now to your group, as if it has already come true.\nTry to cover:\n• what you want to achieve\n• what you'd like your daily life to look like\n• who inspires your ambition\n• one step you're taking this year\n• one challenge you expect\nUse want to and would like to at least twice each.\nChallenge\nGroup members guess whose dream is the most ambitious.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new ambition words\n☐ want to + infinitive\n☐ would like to + infinitive\n☐ how to talk about future goals\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short paragraph about your ambitions for the next five years.\n\n"},{"number":15,"title":"Crime & Justice","content":"LESSON 15 Crime & Justice\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about life experiences using the present perfect.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Have you ever witnessed a small accident?\n2. Have you ever lost something valuable?\n3. Has anyone in your family ever reported a problem to the police?\n4. Have you ever returned money you found?\n5. Have you already learned about the law in this country?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has witnessed a small crime\n☐ has lost their phone or keys\n☐ has already spoken to a police officer\n☐ has never been to a police station\n☐ has just heard some interesting news today\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Crime & Justice\nWORD EXAMPLE\nwitness She was a witness to the accident.\nevidence The police gathered enough evidence.\ntheft There was a theft at the shop.\npunishment The punishment didn't fit the crime.\nreport (a crime) He reported the theft immediately.\nprevent Better lighting could prevent crime.\nUseful Expressions\n• Have you ever...?\n• I've never...\n• I've just heard that...\n• I've already told the police.\n• She hasn't reported it yet.\n• It's happened to me before.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nwitness • evidence • punishment • already • prevent\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nHave You Ever Reported a Crime?\nREAD\nGuled has never witnessed a serious crime, but he's already reported a small theft once. \nSomeone stole his neighbour's bicycle last year, and he's just remembered a detail he forgot to \nmention to the police. His neighbour has never got the bicycle back, but she's already bought a \nstronger lock for the new one.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Has Guled ever witnessed a serious crime?\n2. What has he already done once?\n3. What has he just remembered?\n4. What has his neighbour already done?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Perfect — ever, never, just, already\nUse the present perfect (have/has + past participle) to talk about life experience, without saying \nexactly when. Ever asks about experience; never means ‘not at any time’; just means ‘a very short \ntime ago’; already shows something happened sooner than expected. This is the moment learners \nusually meet this tense properly — take it slowly.\nExamples\n• Have you ever reported a crime?\n• I've never lost anything valuable.\n• She's just called the police.\n• We've already spoken to a witness.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. ______ you ______ (ever/witness) a crime?\n2. I ______ (never/lose) my passport.\n3. He ______ (just/report) the theft.\n4. They ______ (already/find) the evidence.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about your own life using ever, never, just, or already.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nHAVE YOU EVER...?\nWalk around and ask classmates 'Have you ever...?' questions about everyday experiences — \nkeep it light, not just about crime.\nTry to cover:\n• something you've never done\n• something you've just done today\n• something you've already done this week\n• something you've done more than once\n• something you'd like to do one day\nFind at least one classmate who has done something you never have.\nChallenge\nReport back: 'X has never... but Y has already...'\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new justice words\n☐ present perfect form\n☐ ever / never / just / already\n☐ how to talk about life experience\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five 'Have you ever...?' questions and answer them about yourself.\n\n"},{"number":16,"title":"Science & Innovation","content":"LESSON 16 Science & Innovation\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about recent changes and events in science and technology, choosing the right past tense.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Have you heard about any new inventions recently?\n2. When did you last use a new piece of technology?\n3. Has a new app changed your daily life?\n4. Did you use the internet ten years ago?\n5. Have scientists found a solution to a problem you care about?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has used an app that didn't exist five years ago\n☐ learned to use a computer as a child\n☐ has read about a new invention this month\n☐ remembers life before smartphones\n☐ has already tried an AI tool\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Science & Innovation\nWORD EXAMPLE\ninvention The wheel was an important invention.\ndiscover Scientists discovered a new treatment.\nbreakthrough It was a major breakthrough.\ndevelop They developed a new app.\nresearch The research took five years.\nrelease The company released a new update.\nUseful Expressions\n• Scientists have recently found...\n• It was invented in...\n• They've just released...\n• Ten years ago, we didn't have...\n• This has completely changed how we...\n• It hasn't always been this way.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ninvention • discover • breakthrough • research • develop\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nFrom No Internet to Everywhere\nREAD\nTwenty years ago, most people in this region didn't have internet access at home. Mobile money\ndidn't exist, and people paid for everything with cash. Since then, technology has changed \nenormously: companies have developed mobile money apps, and millions of people have already\nstarted using them every day. Life was very different before — and it has kept changing ever \nsince.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Did most people have internet access twenty years ago?\n2. How did people pay for things back then?\n3. What have companies developed since then?\n4. How does the text describe life today compared to before?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Perfect vs Past Simple\nThis is the change that most confuses A2 learners moving to B1. Use the past simple for a finished \naction at a specific, named time (yesterday, in 2019, ten years ago). Use the present perfect for a \nchange up to now, or an experience with no specific time given.\nExamples\n• Technology has changed a lot. (up to now, no specific time)\n• Mobile money didn't exist ten years ago. (specific time -> past simple)\n• They've developed a new app. (recent change, still relevant)\n• The app was released last year. (specific time -> past simple)\nComplete the Sentences\n1. Technology ______ (change) a lot in the last ten years.\n2. The internet ______ (arrive) here in 2010.\n3. I ______ (never/use) this app before today.\n4. She ______ (buy) her first smartphone last year.\nMake It Personal — write two sentences with the past simple (with a specific time) and two with the \npresent perfect (no specific time) about technology in your life.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHEN AND NOW: TECHNOLOGY\nCompare life before and after a piece of technology arrived, choosing your tense carefully.\nTry to cover:\n• something that has changed in the last ten years (present perfect)\n• a specific moment when you first used it (past simple)\n• something you've never used\n• something that hasn't changed\n• a prediction for the next ten years\nUse present perfect for changes-up-to-now and past simple for named moments — your partner should be \nable to tell which is which.\nChallenge\nYour partner points out one sentence and says whether you used the right tense.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new science words\n☐ present perfect for change/experience\n☐ past simple for a specific time\n☐ how to choose between the two\nHOMEWORK\nWrite six sentences about technology in your life: three with present perfect, three with past \nsimple.\n\n"},{"number":17,"title":"Arts & Entertainment","content":"LESSON 17 Arts & Entertainment\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about real possibilities in the arts and entertainment world.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What kind of music, film, or art do you enjoy?\n2. If you have free time this weekend, what will you watch?\n3. If a famous singer visits your city, will you go to the concert?\n4. What will you do if your favourite show ends?\n5. If you learn an instrument, which one will you choose?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ will watch a film this week if they have time\n☐ plays an instrument\n☐ will go to a concert if tickets are available\n☐ prefers live music to recorded music\n☐ will try a new hobby this year if possible\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Arts & Entertainment\nWORD EXAMPLE\nperformance It was an amazing performance.\ntalented She's a talented singer.\naudience The audience loved the show.\nrelease (a film/song) They'll release the film in June.\nticket I'll buy a ticket if it's not too expensive.\nvenue The venue holds 500 people.\nUseful Expressions\n• If I have time, I'll...\n• If it's not too expensive, I will...\n• I won't go if...\n• What will you do if...?\n• If tickets are available, I'll book them.\n• It depends — if...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nperformance • talented • audience • venue • release\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Concert Decision\nREAD\nIf the tickets aren't too expensive, Nimo will go to the concert this weekend. If she goes, she'll \ntake her sister, because they both love the singer. If it rains, the venue will move the show \nindoors. If Nimo can't get a ticket in time, she'll watch the performance online instead — she \ndoesn't want to miss it either way.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. When will Nimo go to the concert?\n2. Who will she take if she goes?\n3. What will happen if it rains?\n4. What will Nimo do if she can't get a ticket?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nFirst Conditional\nUse if + present simple, ...will + base verb to talk about a real, likely possibility in the future and its \nresult.\nExamples\n• If it's not expensive, I'll go.\n• If she has time, she'll watch it.\n• If it rains, they'll move indoors.\n• I won't go if the tickets are sold out.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. If I ______ (have) time, I ______ (watch) the show.\n2. If the tickets ______ (be) cheap, we ______ (buy) two.\n3. She ______ (not/come) if it ______ (rain).\n4. If you ______ (miss) it, you ______ (can/watch) it online.\nMake It Personal — write four real if-sentences about your own weekend plans.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nWEEKEND PLANS\nPlan a realistic weekend of entertainment with a partner, using real conditions.\nTry to cover:\n• what you'll do if the weather is good\n• what you'll do if you have no money\n• what you'll do if a friend invites you out\n• a backup plan if your first idea falls through\n• one thing you definitely won't do\nUse the first conditional at least four times.\nChallenge\nYour partner has to guess which of your plans is the most likely to actually happen.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new arts words\n☐ first conditional form\n☐ if + present, will + base verb\n☐ how to talk about real future possibilities\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five real if-sentences about your plans for this week.\n\n"},{"number":18,"title":"Global Issues","content":"LESSON 18 Global Issues\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about global problems and how they are addressed, using the passive voice.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What global issue do you think about most?\n2. Is plastic used a lot where you live?\n3. How is rubbish collected in your area?\n4. Are trees planted in your city?\n5. What is being done about climate change near you?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has seen plastic pollution up close\n☐ knows how recycling works locally\n☐ has taken part in a clean-up\n☐ thinks climate change is discussed enough\n☐ believes small actions make a difference\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Global Issues\nWORD EXAMPLE\nresource Water is a limited resource.\nproduce A lot of plastic is produced every year.\naffect Climate change affects everyone.\nsolution We need a real solution.\nawareness The campaign raised awareness.\nreduce Plastic use should be reduced.\nUseful Expressions\n• It's caused by...\n• It's affected by...\n• A lot is being done about it.\n• Not enough is being done.\n• It's used for...\n• It's produced in large amounts.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nresource • awareness • affect • solution • produce\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nWhat Happens to Plastic?\nREAD\nMillions of tonnes of plastic are produced every year. Most of it is used only once and then \nthrown away. In some places, plastic is recycled and turned into new products. In other places, \nit is burned or buried, and the environment is damaged. Awareness is being raised around the \nworld, but real change is still needed.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. How much plastic is produced every year?\n2. How is most plastic used?\n3. What is done with plastic in some places?\n4. What is being raised around the world?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Simple Passive\nUse the passive (is/are + past participle) when the action matters more than who does it. Compare: \n'They produce plastic' (active, focus on 'they') vs 'Plastic is produced' (passive, focus on the plastic).\nExamples\n• Plastic is produced in huge amounts.\n• Trees are planted every year here.\n• The rubbish is collected on Fridays.\n• This issue isn't discussed enough.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. A lot of plastic ______ (produce) every year.\n2. Trees ______ (plant) in this park every spring.\n3. Rubbish ______ (not/collect) on Sundays.\n4. ______ recycling ______ (do) in your area?\nMake It Personal — write four passive sentences about what is or isn't done in your area.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nSTATE OF OUR CITY\nIn groups, report on how a global issue is handled in your own area, like a short news report.\nTry to cover:\n• what is done well\n• what isn't done enough\n• how rubbish/recycling is handled\n• how awareness is raised\n• one change you'd like to see\nUse the passive voice at least four times.\nChallenge\nPresent your 'report' to the class as if on the evening news.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new global-issue words\n☐ present simple passive form\n☐ when to use active vs passive\n☐ how to talk about global problems\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five passive sentences about how something is handled in your city.\n\n"},{"number":19,"title":"Free Time & Hobbies","content":"LESSON 19 Free Time & Hobbies\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about your free time and hobbies, choosing correctly between -ing and to + verb.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What do you enjoy doing in your free time?\n2. Is there a hobby you'd love to try?\n3. Do you prefer relaxing at home or going out?\n4. What did you use to enjoy doing as a child?\n5. Do you find it hard to find time for hobbies?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ enjoys reading\n☐ would love to try painting\n☐ hates cleaning\n☐ hopes to learn an instrument\n☐ avoids watching the news before bed\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Free Time & Hobbies\nWORD EXAMPLE\nhobby Photography is my hobby.\nrelax I like to relax by drawing.\navoid I avoid checking my phone at night.\nenjoy She enjoys painting.\nmanage (to) I managed to finish my project.\nspare time I don't have much spare time.\nUseful Expressions\n\n• I really enjoy...\n• I'd love to try...\n• I hope to learn...\n• I can't stand...\n• I don't mind...\n• I always avoid...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nhobby • avoid • manage • spare • photography\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nMaking Time for Hobbies\nREAD\nFaduma enjoys painting, but she doesn't always manage to find time for it. She'd love to try \npottery one day, and she hopes to join a class next year. She avoids spending all her free time on \nher phone, and she's decided to start drawing again instead. She's learned to relax by doing \nsomething with her hands.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What does Faduma enjoy doing?\n2. What would she love to try?\n3. What does she avoid doing?\n4. What has she learned to do?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nGerunds vs Infinitives\nSome verbs are followed by -ing (enjoy, avoid, don't mind, finish), and some are followed by to + \nverb (want, hope, decide, manage, would love). There's no simple rule — learn them with the verb.\nExamples\n• She enjoys painting. (enjoy + -ing)\n• She'd love to try pottery. (would love + to)\n• He avoids checking his phone. (avoid + -ing)\n• They decided to join a class. (decide + to)\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I enjoy ______ (read) in my free time.\n2. She'd love ______ (learn) to paint.\n3. He avoids ______ (watch) TV before bed.\n4. We've decided ______ (start) a new hobby.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about your free time using enjoy, avoid, would love to, and hope \nto.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nHOBBY SWAP\nInterview a partner about their free time and try to convince them to try one of your hobbies.\nTry to cover:\n• what they enjoy doing\n• what they avoid doing in their free time\n• a hobby they'd love to try\n• a hobby they hope to keep doing forever\n• one hobby you could teach each other\nUse at least three -ing verbs and three to + verb structures.\nChallenge\nBy the end, agree on one hobby to try together.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new hobby words\n☐ verbs followed by -ing\n☐ verbs followed by to + verb\n☐ how to talk about free time\nHOMEWORK\nWrite six sentences about your free time using enjoy, avoid, decide, hope, want, and would love.\n\n"},{"number":20,"title":"Cultural Identity","content":"LESSON 20 Cultural Identity\nCAN-DO GOAL: Report what other people said about culture and identity.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's something you're proud of in your culture?\n2. Has anyone ever asked you about your traditions?\n3. What did an older relative once tell you about your culture?\n4. Is there a tradition that is changing?\n5. What would you like people from other cultures to know about yours?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ speaks more than 2 languages\n☐ has explained a tradition to a foreigner\n☐ has been asked a surprising question about their culture\n☐ wears traditional clothing sometimes\n☐ has a favourite proverb\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Cultural Identity\nWORD EXAMPLE\ntradition This is an old tradition.\nidentity Language is part of my identity.\nproverb My grandmother taught me a proverb.\ncustom It's a local custom.\nheritage She's proud of her heritage.\ngeneration It's passed down through generations.\nUseful Expressions\n• She told me that...\n• He said (that)...\n• They explained that...\n• My grandmother always said...\n• According to my father,...\n• I was told that...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ntradition • heritage • generation • identity • custom\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nWhat My Grandmother Said\nREAD\nMy grandmother told me that traditions connect us to the people who came before us. She said \nthat she had learned the same proverbs from her own grandmother. She explained that our \nlanguage carries stories that books cannot hold. She also said that the world was changing fast, \nbut that some things should never be forgotten.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did the grandmother say traditions do?\n2. What did she say she had learned from her own grandmother?\n3. What did she explain about the language?\n4. What did she say about the changing world?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nReported Speech — statements\nTo report what someone said, use said/told + that, and move the verb one step back into the past \n(present simple -> past simple; present perfect -> past perfect).\nExamples\n• “Traditions matter.” -> She said that traditions mattered.\n• “I learned this from my mother.” -> He said he had learned it from his mother.\n• “The world is changing.” -> She said the world was changing.\n• “I'm proud of my culture.” -> He told me he was proud of his culture.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. “I love this tradition.” -> She said (that) she ______ (love) that tradition.\n2. “We speak three languages.” -> They told me (that) they ______ (speak) three languages.\n3. “My culture is changing.” -> He said (that) his culture ______ (change).\n4. “I learned this as a child.” -> She said (that) she ______ (learn) it as a child.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences reporting something a family member once told you.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nWORDS OF WISDOM\nShare something a family member or elder once told you, then report a partner's story to a third\nperson.\nTry to cover:\n• who told you something, and when\n• what they said (in your own words)\n• why it stayed with you\n• your partner's story, reported back\n• one tradition you'd like to pass on\nUse reported speech at least three times.\nChallenge\nCompare: whose story was reported most accurately?\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new culture words\n☐ reported speech with said/told\n☐ backshifting verbs into the past\n☐ how to report what someone said\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five sentences reporting things people in your life have told you.\n\n"},{"number":21,"title":"Making Decisions","content":"LESSON 21 Making Decisions\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about imaginary situations and decisions.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Is it hard for you to make decisions?\n2. What would you do with an unexpected day off?\n3. If you could change one decision from your past, would you?\n4. Who do you usually ask for advice?\n5. If money weren't a problem, what would you do differently?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ finds big decisions stressful\n☐ makes lists before deciding\n☐ would move abroad if they could\n☐ always asks for a second opinion\n☐ trusts their instincts\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Making Decisions\nWORD EXAMPLE\ndecision It was a difficult decision.\noption We have two options.\ninstinct I trusted my instinct.\nconsider I considered both sides.\nregret I don't regret my choice.\nimaginary It's just an imaginary situation.\nUseful Expressions\n• If I were you, I'd...\n• What would you do if...?\n• I'd probably...\n• It depends on the situation.\n• I wouldn't know where to start.\n• I'd have to think about it.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ndecision • instinct • consider • regret • imaginary\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nIf I Had More Time\nREAD\nIf Khadija had more free time, she would learn to paint. If she weren't so busy with work, she \nwould travel more often, too. If money weren't a problem, she would open a small café by the \nsea. She knows these are just imaginary situations for now, but she says that if she stopped \ndreaming, she wouldn't have any goals to work towards.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What would Khadija learn if she had more time?\n2. What would she do if she weren't so busy?\n3. What would she do if money weren't a problem?\n4. Why does she keep imagining these situations?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nSecond Conditional\nUse if + past simple, ...would + base verb to talk about unreal or unlikely present situations — \nimaginary, not real, right now.\nExamples\n• If I had more time, I would learn to paint.\n• If she weren't busy, she'd travel more.\n• What would you do if you won the lottery?\n• I wouldn't worry if I were you.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. If I ______ (have) more money, I ______ (travel) more.\n2. If she ______ (not/be) so busy, she ______ (relax) more.\n3. What ______ you ______ (do) if you ______ (can) live anywhere?\n4. If he ______ (know) the answer, he ______ (tell) us.\nMake It Personal — write four imaginary sentences about your own life using the second conditional.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nIF I COULD CHANGE ONE THING\nDiscuss imaginary situations and decisions with your group.\nTry to cover:\n• what you'd do with an extra free day\n• what you'd do if money weren't a problem\n• how your life would be different if you lived somewhere else\n• what you'd tell your younger self\n• one imaginary decision you'd love to make real\nUse the second conditional at least four times.\nChallenge\nAs a group, decide whose imaginary life sounds the most interesting.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new decision words\n☐ second conditional form\n☐ if + past simple, would + base verb\n☐ how to talk about imaginary situations\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five second conditional sentences about your imaginary life.\n\n"},{"number":22,"title":"Looking Back, Looking Forward","content":"LESSON 22 Looking Back, Looking Forward\nCAN-DO GOAL: Review and combine everything you've learned this term.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's the most useful thing you've learned this term?\n2. Have you noticed your English improving?\n3. What was difficult at the start that feels easier now?\n4. If you kept studying, where would you be in a year?\n5. What are you going to focus on next?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has used present perfect correctly this week\n☐ has tried a first conditional sentence outside class\n☐ has noticed real improvement\n☐ still finds one structure tricky\n☐ would recommend this course to a friend\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Looking Back, Looking Forward\nWORD EXAMPLE\nimprove My speaking has improved a lot.\nprogress I've made good progress.\nconfident I feel more confident now.\nfluent I want to become fluent.\nchallenge The conditionals were a real challenge.\nachievement Finishing this course is an achievement.\nUseful Expressions\n• I've come a long way.\n• I still find ... a bit tricky.\n• I'm much more confident now.\n• If I keep practising, I'll...\n• I've never spoken this much English before.\n• This term has changed how I...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nimprove • progress • confident • fluent • achievement\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nTwenty-Two Lessons Later\nREAD\nAt the start of this course, Sagal could barely introduce herself in English. Since then, she has \nlearned to talk about her routines, her past, her plans, and her opinions. She has already used \nthe present perfect in a real conversation, and if she keeps practising, she'll be speaking fluently\nwithin a year. She's never felt this confident before — and she isn't going to stop now.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What could Sagal barely do at the start of the course?\n2. What has she learned to talk about since then?\n3. What has she already used in a real conversation?\n4. What will happen if she keeps practising?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nMixed Review — Tenses, Conditionals, Perfect Aspect\nThis lesson mixes everything from the term: present/past simple and continuous, present perfect, \nfirst and second conditionals, passive, gerunds/infinitives, and reported speech. The goal is choosing\nthe right tool for each sentence, not learning something new.\nExamples\n• I've learned a lot this term. (present perfect)\n• If I practise every day, I'll improve. (first conditional)\n• If I had more time, I'd study more. (second conditional)\n• My teacher said I had improved. (reported speech)\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (learn) a lot since September.\n2. If I ______ (practise) more, I ______ (improve) faster.\n3. If I ______ (have) more time now, I ______ (study) every day.\n4. My teacher told me I ______ (make) great progress.\nMake It Personal — write four sentences about your own progress this term, using four different \nstructures from the course.\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nMY LANGUAGE JOURNEY\nTell your group the story of your term: where you started, what changed, and where you're \ngoing next.\nTry to cover:\n• one thing you couldn't do at the start (past simple)\n• one thing that has changed since then (present perfect)\n• a real plan for practising more (first conditional)\n• an imaginary ‘dream level’ of English (second conditional)\n• something a teacher or classmate once told you (reported speech)\nTry to use at least five different structures from the term.\nChallenge\nVote on the classmate with the most inspiring language journey.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ every structure from the course, in review\n☐ how to choose the right tense for the sentence\n☐ how confident I feel using English now\n☐ one structure I still want to practise\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short paragraph (8–10 sentences) about your English journey this term, using as many \ndifferent structures as you can."}]},"speakup-b2":{"title":"SpeakUp English B2","lessons":[{"number":1,"title":"Getting Acquainted","content":"LESSON 1 Getting Acquainted\nCAN-DO GOAL: Introduce yourself and keep a conversation going with follow-up questions.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your name?\n2. Where are you from?\n3. What do you do?\n4. What do you enjoy doing?\n5. Tell us one interesting fact about yourself.\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ likes coffee\n☐ has two brothers\n☐ enjoys football\n☐ speaks another language\n☐ loves travelling\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Getting Acquainted\nWORD EXAMPLE\nhobby My hobby is reading.\nhometown I come from Hargeisa.\noccupation I'm a teacher.\nmarried I'm married.\nsingle I'm single.\noutgoing She's very outgoing and easy to talk to.\nUseful Expressions\n• Nice to meet you.\n• What do you do?\n• Could you tell me about yourself?\n• Really?\n• That's interesting.\n• How about you?\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\noccupation • engineer • business • photographer • entrepreneur\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nSarah's Introduction\nREAD\nHi! My name is Sarah. I'm twenty-eight years old. I live in London, but my hometown is \nManchester. I work as a nurse. In my free time I enjoy hiking and reading mystery books. I also \nlove travelling because I enjoy meeting new people.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Where does Sarah live?\n2. What is her job?\n3. What are her hobbies?\n4. Why does she enjoy travelling?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Simple — talking about yourself\nUse the present simple to talk about facts, routines, and things that are generally true about you: work, \nhabits, and preferences.\nExamples\n• I work as a nurse.\n• I live in London.\n• I enjoy hiking.\n• She comes from Hargeisa.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ in ______.\n2. My job ______.\n3. I enjoy ______.\n4. I usually ______ at the weekend.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE\nYou are attending an international conference. Introduce yourself to five classmates.\nTry to cover:\n• their job\n• hobbies\n• family\n• favourite food\n• dream destination\nDo NOT write answers. Remember them.\nChallenge\nIntroduce one classmate to everyone else.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ 10 new words\n☐ how to introduce myself\n☐ how to ask follow-up questions\n☐ how to keep a conversation going\nHOMEWORK\nRecord a 2-minute self-introduction. OR Interview one family member in English.\n\n"},{"number":2,"title":"Work & Careers","content":"LESSON 2 Work & Careers\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about your work history and describe career achievements.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your dream job?\n2. Have you ever changed careers?\n3. What was your first job?\n4. What skills are most important in your field?\n5. Would you rather work alone or in a team?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has had more than three jobs\n☐ works from home\n☐ wants to start a business\n☐ has worked abroad\n☐ enjoys their current job\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Work & Careers\nWORD EXAMPLE\npromotion She got a promotion after two years.\ncolleague My colleague helped me finish the report.\ndeadline We have a tight deadline this week.\nresign He decided to resign from his position.\nqualification A degree is a common qualification for this role.\nworkload The workload gets heavier at the end of the month.\nUseful Expressions\n• What line of work are you in?\n• I'm currently working on...\n• I used to work as...\n• It's a steep learning curve.\n• I'm responsible for...\n• That sounds demanding.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ncareer • qualification • colleague • responsibility • achievement\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nFrom Intern to Manager\nREAD\nAmina joined the company as an intern five years ago. Since then, she has completed two major \nprojects and has been promoted twice. Last year, she managed a team of six people for the first \ntime. She has also started mentoring new employees, something she never expected to enjoy as \nmuch as she does now.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. How long has Amina worked at the company?\n2. What did she do last year?\n3. What does she do now that surprised her?\n4. How many times has she been promoted?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Perfect vs Past Simple\nUse the present perfect for experiences and changes connected to now (no finished time given). Use the \npast simple for finished actions at a specific time in the past.\nExamples\n• I have worked here for three years. (still true)\n• I started this job in 2021. (specific time)\n• She has never managed a team before.\n• He resigned last month.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (work) here since 2022.\n2. She ______ (start) her business last year.\n3. Have you ever ______ (change) careers?\n4. We ______ (finish) the project on Friday.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE CAREER PANEL\nYou are on a panel discussing career paths for young people. Discuss with your group.\nTry to cover:\n• your career journey so far\n• one mistake you learned from\n• advice for someone starting out\n• a skill you want to develop\n• your five-year plan\nSpeak for at least one minute without stopping.\nChallenge\nVote on the most inspiring career story in your group.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ work-related vocabulary\n☐ present perfect vs past simple\n☐ how to describe achievements\n☐ how to give career advice\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five sentences about your work history using the present perfect. OR Interview someone \nabout their career.\n\n"},{"number":3,"title":"Travel & Adventure","content":"LESSON 3 Travel & Adventure\nCAN-DO GOAL: Tell a travel story using a sequence of past events.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's the most memorable trip you've taken?\n2. Do you prefer planned trips or spontaneous ones?\n3. What's on your travel bucket list?\n4. Have you ever been lost while travelling?\n5. What's the best way to experience a new culture?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has been to more than five countries\n☐ has missed a flight\n☐ prefers mountains to beaches\n☐ has tried an extreme sport\n☐ wants to backpack across Africa\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Travel & Adventure\nWORD EXAMPLE\nitinerary We planned a detailed itinerary for the trip.\naccommodation We booked our accommodation in advance.\nbreathtaking The view from the mountain was breathtaking.\noff the beaten track We found a village off the beaten track.\nculture shock She experienced culture shock in her first week abroad.\nlayover We had a six-hour layover in Dubai.\nUseful Expressions\n• Guess what happened to me...\n• You won't believe this, but...\n• In the end...\n• To cut a long story short...\n• It turned out that...\n• That must have been terrifying!\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nitinerary • accommodation • adventure • destination • journey\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nLost in the Mountains\nREAD\nWe had been hiking for three hours when the fog suddenly rolled in. We couldn't see the path \nanymore, and I realised we had wandered off the main trail. My friend was checking her phone, \nbut there was no signal. Just as we were starting to panic, we heard voices — a group of local \nshepherds had been watching us from a distance and came to guide us back safely.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What had they been doing before the fog appeared?\n2. Why couldn't they call for help?\n3. Who helped them?\n4. How do you think they felt at the end?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nNarrative Tenses — Past Simple, Past Continuous, Past Perfect\nUse the past continuous for background actions, the past simple for main events, and the past perfect \nfor actions that happened before another past action.\nExamples\n• We were hiking when the fog rolled in.\n• We had wandered off the trail before we noticed.\n• She was checking her phone when the shepherds arrived.\n• By the time we found the path, it was dark.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (walk) along the beach when I ______ (see) the sunset.\n2. By the time we arrived, the bus ______ (already/leave).\n3. While I ______ (pack), my friend ______ (call) a taxi.\n4. We ______ (never/visit) that city before last year.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE TRAVELLER'S TALE\nTell your group about a real or imaginary travel experience that went wrong.\nTry to cover:\n• where you were going\n• what you were doing before things went wrong\n• the exact moment things changed\n• how you solved the problem\n• what you learned\nUse at least three narrative tenses in your story.\nChallenge\nYour group votes for the most dramatic story.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ travel vocabulary\n☐ narrative tenses\n☐ how to tell a story in order\n☐ how to react to someone's story\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a 100-word travel story using narrative tenses. OR Record yourself telling a travel story.\n\n"},{"number":4,"title":"Technology & Social Media","content":"LESSON 4 Technology & Social Media\nCAN-DO GOAL: Discuss habits and changes related to technology use.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How many hours a day do you spend online?\n2. What app could you not live without?\n3. Has social media changed how you communicate?\n4. Do you think technology makes life easier or more stressful?\n5. What was the last app you downloaded?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has deleted a social media app\n☐ spends more than 3 hours online daily\n☐ has learned a skill from a video\n☐ prefers texting to calling\n☐ has gone a day without their phone\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Technology & Social Media\nWORD EXAMPLE\nscreen time I'm trying to reduce my screen time.\naddictive Short videos can be really addictive.\nnotification I turned off notifications to focus better.\nupdate I need to update this app.\nonline privacy Online privacy is a growing concern.\nmultitask It's hard to multitask while studying.\nUseful Expressions\n• I've been meaning to...\n• I can't stop scrolling.\n• It's a double-edged sword.\n• I'm trying to cut down on...\n• That's gone viral.\n• I've been using it for years.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ntechnology • notification • algorithm • device • connectivity\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Digital Detox\nREAD\nYusuf has been working from his laptop for eight hours a day for the past three years. Recently, he \nhas been feeling more anxious and less able to concentrate. Last month, he decided to try a digital \ndetox — no social media for two weeks. He has been sleeping better and has been spending more \ntime with his family since he started.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. How long has Yusuf been working from his laptop this way?\n2. Why did he decide to try a digital detox?\n3. What has changed since he started?\n4. Do you think a digital detox would help you?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPresent Perfect Continuous\nUse the present perfect continuous to focus on the duration of an action that started in the past and \ncontinues now, or has recently stopped with visible results.\nExamples\n• I've been using this phone for two years.\n• She's been studying English since March.\n• We've been talking for an hour.\n• He's been feeling tired lately.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (learn) English for three years.\n2. She ______ (work) on this project since Monday.\n3. How long ______ you ______ (use) social media?\n4. They ______ (argue) about screen time all week.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE TECHNOLOGY DEBATE\nYour group is discussing whether social media does more harm than good.\nTry to cover:\n• one benefit of social media\n• one danger of social media\n• how it has changed relationships\n• how it has changed how you learn\n• one rule you would set for young people\nTry to disagree politely with at least one classmate.\nChallenge\nReach a group agreement on one rule for healthy technology use.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ technology vocabulary\n☐ present perfect continuous\n☐ how to discuss pros and cons\n☐ how to disagree politely\nHOMEWORK\nTrack your screen time for two days and report the results in English. OR Write a paragraph about \nyour relationship with technology.\n\n"},{"number":5,"title":"Health & Wellbeing","content":"LESSON 5 Health & Wellbeing\nCAN-DO GOAL: Give advice and talk about obligations related to health.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What does a healthy lifestyle mean to you?\n2. How do you deal with stress?\n3. Do you get enough sleep?\n4. What's one habit you'd like to change?\n5. How important is mental health compared to physical health?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ exercises every day\n☐ meditates or practises mindfulness\n☐ has trouble sleeping\n☐ drinks more than two coffees a day\n☐ has tried a new diet\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Health & Wellbeing\nWORD EXAMPLE\nburnout Too much overtime can lead to burnout.\nbalanced diet A balanced diet keeps you energised.\nstress relief Exercise is a good stress relief.\nchronic Chronic stress can affect your immune system.\nrecover It takes time to recover from an illness.\nwellbeing Mental wellbeing matters as much as physical health.\nUseful Expressions\n• You should really see a doctor.\n• You'd better rest.\n• I have to admit...\n• It's not good for you.\n• Take it easy.\n• Listen to your body.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nwellbeing • nutrition • exhausted • recovery • immune system\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nLearning to Slow Down\nREAD\nFardowsa used to work sixty hours a week and rarely took breaks. She often felt exhausted but \nkept pushing through. After she started experiencing constant headaches, her doctor told her she \nhad to make changes. She now takes short walks during the day, and she has started saying no to \nextra tasks. She says she should have made these changes years earlier.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did Fardowsa's lifestyle used to be like?\n2. What made her realise she needed to change?\n3. What has she started doing differently?\n4. What does she regret?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nModals of Advice and Obligation — should, must, have to\nUse should/shouldn't for advice, must/mustn't for strong obligation or prohibition, and have to/don't \nhave to for external rules or necessity.\nExamples\n• You should drink more water.\n• You must rest if you're feeling unwell.\n• I have to finish this report today.\n• You don't have to attend if you're sick.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. You ______ see a doctor if it doesn't improve.\n2. We ______ (not) be late for the exam.\n3. I ______ (have to) wake up early tomorrow.\n4. You ______ (not have to) come if you're busy.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE WELLNESS CLINIC\nOne classmate plays a doctor; the other describes a health problem and asks for advice.\nTry to cover:\n• describe your symptoms or habits\n• ask for advice\n• give at least three pieces of advice\n• discuss lifestyle changes\n• agree on a small, realistic goal\nSwitch roles after five minutes.\nChallenge\nShare the best piece of advice you received with the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ health vocabulary\n☐ should / must / have to\n☐ how to give advice\n☐ how to talk about habits\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short wellbeing plan for yourself using modals. OR Give a family member advice in English \nand note their reaction.\n\n"},{"number":6,"title":"Food & Culture","content":"LESSON 6 Food & Culture\nCAN-DO GOAL: Compare foods, dishes, and eating habits across cultures.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's your favourite dish from your culture?\n2. Have your eating habits changed over the years?\n3. Do you prefer eating out or cooking at home?\n4. What's a food you've never tried but want to?\n5. How does food bring people together in your culture?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ can cook a traditional dish\n☐ dislikes spicy food\n☐ has tried food from five countries\n☐ prefers street food to restaurants\n☐ has a food allergy\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Food & Culture\nWORD EXAMPLE\nspicy This sauce is too spicy for me.\nstaple food Rice is a staple food in many countries.\nflavour The dish has a rich, smoky flavour.\ningredient Cardamom is a key ingredient in this recipe.\novercooked The meat was slightly overcooked.\nappetite Travelling always gives me a huge appetite.\nUseful Expressions\n• It's an acquired taste.\n• This is much richer than...\n• Nothing beats home-cooked food.\n• I could eat this every day.\n• It tastes just like...\n• That's a bit bland for me.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ncuisine • delicious • ingredient • appetite • flavour\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nTwo Kitchens, One Love of Food\nREAD\nIn Hargeisa, Deeqa's grandmother taught her to cook the slowest, richest stews she has ever \ntasted, using far fewer spices than most restaurant food today. When Deeqa moved to Nairobi for \nuniversity, she discovered that Kenyan food was spicier and used a much wider variety of \nvegetables. She now blends both traditions, saying her cooking has become far more interesting \nthan either style alone.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. Who taught Deeqa to cook?\n2. How is Kenyan food different from what she grew up with?\n3. What has Deeqa done with the two traditions?\n4. What comparative words are used in the text?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nComparatives, Superlatives, and Quantifiers\nUse comparatives (-er / more) to compare two things, superlatives (-est / most) for extremes, and \nquantifiers (a few, much, a wide variety of) to talk about amounts.\nExamples\n• This dish is spicier than that one.\n• It's the most delicious meal I've had.\n• There's far too much salt in this.\n• We used a wide variety of vegetables.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. This soup is ______ (spicy) than the last one.\n2. It's ______ (good) meal I've ever had.\n3. There isn't ______ sugar in this recipe.\n4. We tried a ______ of dishes at the festival.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE FOOD FESTIVAL\nYou are organising an international food festival. Present a dish from your culture to the group.\nTry to cover:\n• describe the dish and its ingredients\n• compare it to a similar dish from another culture\n• explain when it's usually eaten\n• describe its taste and texture\n• answer at least two questions\nUse at least two comparative or superlative structures.\nChallenge\nVote on which dish the class would most like to try.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ food vocabulary\n☐ comparatives and superlatives\n☐ quantifiers\n☐ how to describe taste and texture\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a description comparing two dishes from different cultures. OR Describe a family recipe in \nEnglish.\n\n"},{"number":7,"title":"Education & Learning","content":"LESSON 7 Education & Learning\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about past habits and how learning styles have changed.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What was school like when you were a child?\n2. Did you use to enjoy studying?\n3. What's the best way for you to learn something new?\n4. Has online learning changed education for the better?\n5. What subject do you wish you had studied more?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ used to hate a subject they now enjoy\n☐ has taken an online course\n☐ learns better by listening than reading\n☐ used to walk far to school\n☐ wants to study abroad\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Education & Learning\nWORD EXAMPLE\ncurriculum The curriculum has changed a lot since I was young.\nmemorise We used to memorise long poems.\nself-taught She's largely self-taught in graphic design.\nscholarship He won a scholarship to study engineering.\nhands-on Hands-on experience is more useful than theory alone.\nprocrastinate I tend to procrastinate before exams.\nUseful Expressions\n• I used to struggle with...\n• We would always...\n• Looking back, I wish I had...\n• It really paid off.\n• I'm a visual learner.\n• Practice makes perfect.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ncurriculum • education • scholarship • memorise • achievement\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nLearning Without Walls\nREAD\nWhen Hodan was in school, students used to sit in rows and copy notes from the blackboard. \nTeachers would rarely use group work. Today, Hodan teaches at the same school, but classes now \nuse tablets, and students would often work together on projects instead of memorising facts alone. \nShe believes today's students think more independently than her generation did.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. How did students used to learn in Hodan's school?\n2. What has changed in the classroom?\n3. What does Hodan believe about today's students?\n4. Which phrase shows repeated past habits?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nUsed to / Would for Past Habits\nUse used to for past states and repeated actions that are no longer true. Use would only for repeated \nactions (not states) in the past.\nExamples\n• We used to sit in rows.\n• She used to hate maths.\n• Teachers would rarely use group work.\n• I would walk to school every day.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (used to) live in a small village.\n2. We ______ (would) play outside every afternoon.\n3. She didn't ______ (used to) like reading.\n4. Did you ______ (used to) study late at night?\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHEN AND NOW\nInterview a partner about how education has changed since they were children.\nTry to cover:\n• what a typical school day used to look like\n• one thing that has improved\n• one thing that has gotten worse\n• how they used to prepare for exams\n• advice for today's students\nUse 'used to' and 'would' at least three times combined.\nChallenge\nShare the most surprising difference you found with the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ education vocabulary\n☐ used to / would\n☐ how to talk about the past\n☐ how to interview someone\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a paragraph about how you used to study as a child. OR Interview an older family member \nabout their school days.\n\n"},{"number":8,"title":"Money & Business","content":"LESSON 8 Money & Business\nCAN-DO GOAL: Discuss hypothetical business decisions and financial choices.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. If you had extra money this month, what would you do with it?\n2. Would you rather start your own business or work for a company?\n3. What's the best investment someone can make?\n4. How do you usually manage your budget?\n5. If you started a business, what would it be?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has started a business\n☐ saves a fixed amount every month\n☐ would invest in property\n☐ has lent money to a friend\n☐ budgets carefully\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Money & Business\nWORD EXAMPLE\ninvestment Real estate is a popular investment.\nbudget We need to stick to our budget.\nprofit The company made a large profit this year.\nloan She took out a loan to start her shop.\nrisk Every business decision involves some risk.\nentrepreneur He's a young, ambitious entrepreneur.\nUseful Expressions\n• If I were you...\n• That's a smart investment.\n• It's a bit of a gamble.\n• I'd rather play it safe.\n• That could really pay off.\n• Money doesn't grow on trees.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ninvestment • entrepreneur • budget • finance • profit\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA Small Shop, A Big Risk\nREAD\nIf Khadar hadn't taken a small loan five years ago, he wouldn't have opened his electronics shop. It \nwas a risk — if the shop fails, he'll lose his savings. But if it continues to grow like this, he'll be able \nto open a second branch next year. He says that if he had waited for the 'perfect moment', he \nwould probably still be waiting today.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did Khadar do five years ago?\n2. What could happen if the shop fails?\n3. What is his plan if the shop keeps growing?\n4. What does he believe about waiting for the perfect moment?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nFirst and Second Conditionals\nUse the first conditional (if + present, will + verb) for real, likely future situations. Use the second \nconditional (if + past simple, would + verb) for hypothetical or unlikely situations.\nExamples\n• If the shop grows, he'll open a second branch.\n• If I had more savings, I would invest in property.\n• If you save early, you'll have more later.\n• If I won the lottery, I would help my family first.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. If I ______ (have) more time, I would learn a new skill.\n2. If the business ______ (grow), we will hire more staff.\n3. She would start a company if she ______ (have) enough capital.\n4. If you don't budget carefully, you ______ (run out) of money.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nPITCH YOUR BUSINESS\nIn pairs, pitch a small business idea and discuss what would happen in different scenarios.\nTry to cover:\n• describe your business idea\n• explain what you would do with your first profit\n• discuss what would happen if it failed\n• discuss what will happen if it succeeds\n• ask your partner for feedback\nUse both the first and second conditional at least once.\nChallenge\nVote on the most realistic business idea in the group.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ money and business vocabulary\n☐ first and second conditionals\n☐ how to discuss hypothetical situations\n☐ how to give a short pitch\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five conditional sentences about your financial goals. OR Plan a simple monthly budget and \nexplain it in English.\n\n"},{"number":9,"title":"Environment & Climate","content":"LESSON 9 Environment & Climate\nCAN-DO GOAL: Discuss environmental problems and describe processes using the passive.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How has the climate changed in your region?\n2. What's one environmental problem that worries you?\n3. Do you recycle or reuse things regularly?\n4. What can individuals really do about climate change?\n5. Should governments or citizens take more responsibility?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ recycles regularly\n☐ has planted a tree\n☐ avoids single-use plastic\n☐ worries about water scarcity\n☐ has experienced extreme weather\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Environment & Climate\nWORD EXAMPLE\ndrought The region has suffered a long drought.\nrenewable energy Solar power is a form of renewable energy.\npollution Air pollution is a major problem in big cities.\nsustainable We should aim for a more sustainable lifestyle.\ndeforestation Deforestation has affected local wildlife.\ncarbon footprint Flying often increases your carbon footprint.\nUseful Expressions\n• It's a growing concern.\n• Something needs to be done about it.\n• We can't ignore this any longer.\n• It's everyone's responsibility.\n• Small changes add up.\n• That's easier said than done.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nenvironment • sustainable • pollution • renewable • climate\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nWater Under Pressure\nREAD\nIn many parts of the Horn of Africa, water is not found as easily as it used to be. Wells are being \ndug deeper every year, and in some villages, water is now transported by truck from distant \nsources. New irrigation systems are being introduced to reduce waste, and trees are being planted \nto help the soil retain moisture. Local communities are increasingly being trained to manage these \nresources themselves.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What problem does the region face?\n2. What is being done to help with water shortages?\n3. Who is being trained to manage resources?\n4. Find three examples of the passive voice.\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nThe Passive Voice\nUse the passive voice (be + past participle) when the action is more important than who does it, \nespecially for processes, problems, and solutions.\nExamples\n• Water is transported by truck.\n• Trees are being planted in the region.\n• The problem has been studied by scientists.\n• New systems will be introduced next year.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. The forest ______ (destroy) by illegal logging.\n2. Solar panels ______ (install) in many villages now.\n3. This issue ______ (discuss) at the last conference.\n4. New laws ______ (introduce) to protect the environment.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE ENVIRONMENTAL REPORT\nYour group is preparing a short spoken report on an environmental issue in your community.\nTry to cover:\n• describe the problem\n• explain what is being done about it\n• explain what should be done\n• suggest one action individuals can take\n• suggest one action the government should take\nUse the passive voice at least three times.\nChallenge\n\nPresent your report to another group and compare issues.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ environmental vocabulary\n☐ the passive voice\n☐ how to describe processes\n☐ how to discuss solutions\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short passage about an environmental issue using the passive voice. OR Research one \nrenewable energy project and summarise it in English.\n\n"},{"number":10,"title":"Relationships & Family","content":"LESSON 10 Relationships & Family\nCAN-DO GOAL: Describe people and relationships in detail using relative clauses.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Who is someone in your family who has influenced you the most?\n2. What makes a friendship last a long time?\n3. How has your relationship with your parents changed over time?\n4. Do you think family structures are changing today?\n5. What quality do you value most in a partner or friend?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has a twin\n☐ is the eldest child\n☐ lives with extended family\n☐ has a best friend from childhood\n☐ has a mentor they admire\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Relationships & Family\nWORD EXAMPLE\nsibling I have three siblings.\nextended family We often gather with our extended family.\nbond They have a very close bond.\nsupportive My parents have always been supportive.\nin-laws She gets along well with her in-laws.\nrole model My grandmother was my role model growing up.\nUseful Expressions\n• We're really close.\n• We've grown apart over the years.\n• She means the world to me.\n• He's always been there for me.\n• We don't see eye to eye.\n• Blood is thicker than water.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nrelationship • sibling • generation • supportive • affectionate\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Woman Who Raised Three Generations\nREAD\nMy grandmother, who raised five children on her own, is the person who taught me the most \nabout resilience. The house where I grew up was always full of relatives who needed a place to \nstay. My cousin, whose mother passed away when she was young, lived with us for ten years. My \ngrandmother never complained about the responsibility that fell on her shoulders.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did the grandmother teach the writer?\n2. Why did the cousin live with the family?\n3. What kind of house did the writer grow up in?\n4. Find three relative clauses in the text.\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nRelative Clauses\nUse relative clauses to give extra information about a person, thing, or place without starting a new \nsentence. Use who/that for people, which/that for things, where for places, and whose for possession.\nExamples\n• My grandmother, who raised five children, taught me resilience.\n• The house where I grew up was always full of people.\n• My cousin, whose mother passed away, lived with us.\n• That's the friend who helped me move house.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. This is the teacher ______ inspired me to study English.\n2. The village ______ I was born has changed a lot.\n3. I have a cousin ______ father is a doctor.\n4. That's the book ______ changed my life.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE FAMILY TREE\nDescribe three important people in your life to your partner using relative clauses.\nTry to cover:\n• who they are and how you're connected\n• one memory that describes your relationship\n• a quality you admire in them\n• how they have influenced you\n• answer your partner's questions\nUse at least three different relative pronouns (who, which, where, whose).\nChallenge\nIntroduce your partner's most interesting family member to the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ family and relationship vocabulary\n☐ relative clauses\n☐ how to describe people in detail\n☐ how to talk about influence\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short description of your family using relative clauses. OR Interview a family member \nabout a relative you've never met.\n\n"},{"number":11,"title":"Media & News","content":"LESSON 11 Media & News\nCAN-DO GOAL: Report what other people have said using reported speech.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Where do you usually get your news from?\n2. Do you trust the news you read online?\n3. How has the news changed since your parents' generation?\n4. What's a news story that recently caught your attention?\n5. Do you think social media has replaced traditional news?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ reads the news every day\n☐ gets news mainly from social media\n☐ has shared a news story recently\n☐ doubts something they read online\n☐ prefers watching to reading the news\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Media & News\nWORD EXAMPLE\nheadline The headline caught my attention immediately.\nsource Always check the source of the information.\nfake news Fake news spreads faster than the truth sometimes.\nbias Every news outlet has some bias.\nbroadcast The event was broadcast live.\ncredible It's important to find credible sources.\nUseful Expressions\n• According to the article...\n• She claimed that...\n• It was reported that...\n• Apparently...\n• I read somewhere that...\n• Take it with a pinch of salt.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\njournalist • broadcast • headline • credible • coverage\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nChecking Before Sharing\nREAD\nA journalist said that misinformation spreads six times faster online than accurate news. She \nexplained that many people share headlines without reading the full article. She warned that this \nhabit can shape public opinion in dangerous ways. She also said that readers should always check \nwhether a source is credible before believing or sharing a story.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did the journalist say about misinformation?\n2. What habit did she warn about?\n3. What did she advise readers to do?\n4. Change one reported sentence back into direct speech.\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nReported Speech\nUse reported speech to tell someone what another person said. Tenses usually move one step back \n(present becomes past, will becomes would).\nExamples\n• \"I read the news every day,\" she said. → She said she read the news every day.\n• \"It's fake,\" he claimed. → He claimed it was fake.\n• \"I'll check the source,\" I said. → I said I would check the source.\n• She explained that many people share headlines without reading them.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. \"I don't trust this article,\" he said. → He said he ______ trust the article.\n2. \"I will verify the source,\" she said. → She said she ______ verify the source.\n3. \"This news is fake,\" they claimed. → They claimed it ______ fake.\n4. \"I read the news every morning,\" I said. → I said I ______ the news every morning.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE NEWSROOM\nRead a short 'news story' your partner tells you, then report it to a third classmate.\nTry to cover:\n• listen carefully to your partner's story\n• note the key facts\n• report the story to a new classmate\n• use reported speech throughout\n• compare how the story changed\nTry not to look at notes while reporting.\nChallenge\nDiscuss how information can change as it's passed from person to person.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ media vocabulary\n☐ reported speech\n☐ how to report what someone said\n☐ how to evaluate news sources\nHOMEWORK\nReport a conversation you had recently using reported speech. OR Summarise a news article in \nreported speech.\n\n"},{"number":12,"title":"Sports & Fitness","content":"LESSON 12 Sports & Fitness\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about ability and possibility in sports and fitness.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What sport are you best at?\n2. Could you swim before you could walk properly, or the other way round?\n3. What's a sport you'd love to be able to try?\n4. How do you stay fit and active?\n5. Do you think everyone should play a sport?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ can swim well\n☐ has completed a race\n☐ could play an instrument as a child\n☐ wants to try a new sport\n☐ exercises with a friend\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Sports & Fitness\nWORD EXAMPLE\nstamina Marathon runners need a lot of stamina.\ninjury She's recovering from a knee injury.\ncoach Our coach pushes us to improve every week.\ncompete He competes at a national level.\nwarm up Always warm up before exercising.\npersonal best She achieved a personal best in the race.\nUseful Expressions\n• I've always been able to...\n• I could never...\n• I'm not able to right now, but...\n• It takes a lot of discipline.\n• Practice makes perfect.\n• Give it your best shot.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nathlete • stamina • competition • discipline • achievement\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Long Road to the Marathon\nREAD\nTwo years ago, Ahmed could barely run for five minutes without stopping. He couldn't afford a \ngym membership, so he started running on the streets near his home. Slowly, he was able to run \nlonger distances, and last month he was finally able to complete his first marathon. He says he can \nnow run for two hours without feeling exhausted.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What could Ahmed do two years ago?\n2. How did he train?\n3. What was he finally able to do last month?\n4. What can he do now?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nModals of Ability and Possibility — can, could, be able to\nUse can/can't for present ability, could/couldn't for past general ability, and was/were able to for a \nspecific past achievement (especially a single successful action).\nExamples\n• I can run five kilometres now.\n• I couldn't swim when I was young.\n• She was able to finish the race despite the injury.\n• We'll be able to compete next season.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (not/swim) when I was a child, but now I can.\n2. Despite the rain, she ______ (able to) finish the match.\n3. He ______ (can) speak three languages fluently.\n4. We ______ (able to) book the stadium for next week.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE FITNESS COACH\nOne classmate is a new client; the other is a fitness coach designing a plan.\nTry to cover:\n• discuss what the client can and can't do now\n• discuss abilities from the past\n• set a realistic fitness goal\n• discuss what they'll be able to do in three months\n• agree on a simple weekly plan\nUse can, could, and be able to at least once each.\nChallenge\nShare your fitness plan with another pair and compare goals.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ sports and fitness vocabulary\n☐ can / could / be able to\n☐ how to talk about ability\n☐ how to set goals\nHOMEWORK\nWrite about a physical skill you've learned using can/could/be able to. OR Track your exercise for \na week and describe it in English.\n\n"},{"number":13,"title":"City vs Countryside","content":"LESSON 13 City vs Countryside\nCAN-DO GOAL: Compare lifestyles and express preferences using gerunds and infinitives.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Would you rather live in a city or the countryside?\n2. What do you enjoy doing in your free time where you live?\n3. What do you miss about living somewhere else?\n4. Is it easier to make friends in a city or a village?\n5. What would you like to change about your neighbourhood?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has lived in both a city and the countryside\n☐ prefers walking to driving\n☐ enjoys gardening\n☐ would like to move abroad\n☐ misses their hometown\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About City vs Countryside\nWORD EXAMPLE\ncommute My commute takes about 40 minutes.\ncommunity There's a strong sense of community here.\ncongestion Traffic congestion is a big issue downtown.\npeaceful Life in the village feels more peaceful.\nopportunity Cities offer more job opportunities.\nisolated Rural areas can sometimes feel isolated.\nUseful Expressions\n• I can't stand...\n• I don't mind...\n• I'd love to try...\n• It's worth considering...\n• There's nothing quite like...\n• I miss being able to...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ncommunity • opportunity • congestion • environment • neighbourhood\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nChoosing Where to Belong\nREAD\nAfter finishing university, Faisal enjoyed living in the city for the fast pace and job opportunities, \nbut he found it hard to avoid feeling isolated despite being surrounded by people. He decided to \ntry living in his family's hometown for six months. He loves being close to nature and enjoys \nknowing his neighbours by name, but he admits he sometimes misses having so many things to do.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did Faisal enjoy about city life?\n2. Why did he decide to move?\n3. What does he love about the countryside?\n4. What does he sometimes miss?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nGerunds and Infinitives\nSome verbs are followed by a gerund (-ing), some by an infinitive (to + verb), and some by either with a \nchange in meaning. Enjoy, avoid, and mind take the gerund; decide, want, and hope take the infinitive.\nExamples\n• I enjoy living in the countryside.\n• She decided to move to the city.\n• He avoids driving during rush hour.\n• We hope to visit next summer.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I don't mind ______ (walk) to work.\n2. They decided ______ (move) closer to the city centre.\n3. She avoids ______ (drive) in heavy traffic.\n4. We're hoping ______ (find) a quieter neighbourhood.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nCITY OR VILLAGE?\nDebate: one half of the group argues for city life, the other for countryside life.\nTry to cover:\n• prepare three arguments for your side\n• use gerunds and infinitives naturally\n• respond to the other side's points\n• find one point you actually agree on\n• summarise your position in one sentence\nTry to use at least four gerund/infinitive structures during the debate.\nChallenge\nAs a class, vote on which lifestyle sounds more appealing.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ lifestyle vocabulary\n☐ gerunds and infinitives\n☐ how to compare preferences\n☐ how to debate respectfully\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a paragraph comparing city and countryside life using gerunds and infinitives. OR Interview \nsomeone who has lived in both.\n\n"},{"number":14,"title":"Dreams & Ambitions","content":"LESSON 14 Dreams & Ambitions\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about future plans, intentions, and predictions.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Where do you see yourself in five years?\n2. What's a goal you're currently working towards?\n3. Do you have any plans for next weekend?\n4. What do you predict will change in your field in ten years?\n5. Is it better to plan carefully or stay flexible?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has a five-year plan\n☐ is learning a new skill right now\n☐ will travel somewhere this year\n☐ wants to own a business one day\n☐ has a fixed plan for next month\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Dreams & Ambitions\nWORD EXAMPLE\nambition Her ambition is to open a school.\nmilestone Graduating was a major milestone.\ndetermined He's determined to succeed despite setbacks.\nlong-term We need a long-term strategy.\naspire She aspires to become a doctor.\nfulfil I want to fulfil my potential.\nUseful Expressions\n• I'm planning to...\n• I'm thinking of...\n• I'm bound to...\n• Sooner or later...\n• I'm determined to...\n• Only time will tell.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nambition • determined • aspire • milestone • achievement\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nFive Years From Now\nREAD\nSagal is going to graduate next year, and she's already planning her next steps. She's going to apply \nfor a master's programme abroad, and she's meeting with an advisor next week to discuss \nscholarships. She predicts that competition for scholarships will increase, but she's determined to \nprepare early. In ten years, she believes she'll be running her own consultancy.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What is Sagal going to do next year?\n2. What has she already arranged?\n3. What does she predict about scholarships?\n4. What does she believe about her future?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nFuture Forms — will, going to, present continuous\nUse will for predictions and spontaneous decisions, going to for plans and intentions already decided, \nand the present continuous for fixed future arrangements.\nExamples\n• I think competition will increase.\n• She's going to apply for a scholarship.\n• I'm meeting my advisor on Tuesday.\n• We'll probably move next year.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I ______ (going to) start my own business next year.\n2. I think the exam ______ (be) difficult.\n3. We ______ (meet) the recruiter at 3pm on Friday.\n4. She ______ (probably/get) the scholarship.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE FIVE-YEAR PLAN\nShare your goals for the next five years with your group.\nTry to cover:\n• one fixed plan you already have arranged\n• one intention you've decided on\n• one prediction about your future\n• one dream you're not sure will happen\n• one piece of advice you'd give your future self\nUse all three future forms at least once.\nChallenge\nEach group chooses the most ambitious plan to share with the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ future vocabulary\n☐ will / going to / present continuous\n☐ how to talk about plans and predictions\n☐ how to describe ambitions\nHOMEWORK\nWrite your five-year plan using different future forms. OR Interview a classmate about their \nambitions.\n\n"},{"number":15,"title":"Crime & Justice","content":"LESSON 15 Crime & Justice\nCAN-DO GOAL: Discuss hypothetical past situations and their consequences.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. Do you think punishments for crime are usually fair?\n2. What's a small mistake that could have had big consequences?\n3. How has crime in your area changed over time?\n4. Do you think prevention is more effective than punishment?\n5. What would you do if you witnessed a crime?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has witnessed a crime\n☐ has reported something to the police\n☐ believes in second chances\n☐ has lost something valuable\n☐ thinks punishments should be stricter\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Crime & Justice\nWORD EXAMPLE\nwitness She was a witness to the accident.\nevidence The police gathered enough evidence.\ntheft There was a theft at the shop last night.\npunishment The punishment didn't fit the crime.\njustice They fought hard for justice.\nprevent Better lighting could prevent street crime.\nUseful Expressions\n• If only I had...\n• In hindsight...\n• It could have been avoided.\n• That was a close call.\n• They got away with it.\n• Justice was finally served.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\njustice • evidence • witness • punishment • consequence\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA Close Call\nREAD\nIf the shopkeeper hadn't installed a security camera, the theft would never have been solved. The \nfootage showed exactly what had happened, and police were able to identify the person \nresponsible within a day. If the neighbours hadn't heard the noise and called the police \nimmediately, the thief would have escaped completely. The shopkeeper says that if he had known \nhow useful the camera would be, he would have installed it years earlier.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What helped solve the theft?\n2. What would have happened if the neighbours hadn't called the police?\n3. What does the shopkeeper regret?\n4. Identify two third conditional sentences.\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nThird Conditional\nUse the third conditional (if + past perfect, would have + past participle) to talk about hypothetical \nsituations in the past and their imagined results.\nExamples\n• If he hadn't installed a camera, the theft wouldn't have been solved.\n• If I had seen it, I would have called the police.\n• She wouldn't have been caught if she hadn't returned.\n• If they had left earlier, they wouldn't have missed the bus.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. If I ______ (see) the accident, I would have called for help.\n2. She wouldn't have been late if she ______ (leave) earlier.\n3. If they had installed better locks, the theft ______ (not/happen).\n4. We would have helped if we ______ (know) about the problem.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nWHAT WOULD YOU HAVE DONE?\nDiscuss real or imagined situations with your group using the third conditional.\nTry to cover:\n• a mistake you made and what you'd have done differently\n• a decision that changed your life\n• what would have happened if you'd made a different choice\n• a time something worked out because of luck\n• one lesson you learned\nUse the third conditional at least three times.\nChallenge\nShare the most interesting 'what if' story with the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ crime and justice vocabulary\n☐ the third conditional\n☐ how to discuss hypothetical past events\n☐ how to express regret\nHOMEWORK\nWrite five third conditional sentences about your own life. OR Describe a news story about crime \nusing the third conditional.\n\n"},{"number":16,"title":"Science & Innovation","content":"LESSON 16 Science & Innovation\nCAN-DO GOAL: Speculate and make deductions about facts and situations.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What invention has changed your life the most?\n2. What technology do you think will exist in 20 years?\n3. Do you follow any news about science or space?\n4. What's a scientific fact that surprised you?\n5. Should countries invest more in scientific research?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ is interested in space\n☐ thinks AI will change most jobs\n☐ reads about new inventions\n☐ wanted to be a scientist as a child\n☐ believes in life on other planets\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Science & Innovation\nWORD EXAMPLE\ninnovation The company is known for its innovation.\nbreakthrough Scientists made a breakthrough in the treatment.\nartificial intelligence Artificial intelligence is changing many industries.\nexperiment The experiment took months to complete.\ntheory It's just a theory, not a proven fact.\ndiscovery The discovery surprised the whole research team.\nUseful Expressions\n• It must be...\n• It could be...\n• It can't possibly be...\n• That might explain why...\n• There's no doubt that...\n• It's hard to say for certain.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ntechnology • innovation • artificial • discovery • research\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Mystery Signal\nREAD\nScientists recently detected a strange signal from deep space. Some researchers believe it might be \ncaused by a distant star, while others think it could be interference from equipment on Earth. A \nfew scientists have joked that it must be something more exciting, though most agree that it can't \nbe evidence of alien life — at least not yet. Further research may reveal the answer within the next \nfew years.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What did scientists detect?\n2. What are two possible explanations?\n3. What do most scientists agree it can't be?\n4. Find three modals of speculation in the text.\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nModals of Speculation and Deduction\nUse must be for strong certainty, could/might/may be for possibility, and can't be for something you \nbelieve is impossible, when speculating about present situations.\nExamples\n• It must be an important discovery.\n• It could be a natural phenomenon.\n• It can't be a mistake — they checked twice.\n• It might be years before we know the answer.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. This ______ (must) be a huge breakthrough.\n2. The results ______ (might) be inaccurate.\n3. That theory ______ (can't) be true — it's been disproven.\n4. The signal ______ (could) be coming from a satellite.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE UNEXPLAINED OBJECT\nYour group is shown a description of a strange, unexplained object. Speculate about what it could \nbe.\nTry to cover:\n• three possible explanations\n• the most likely explanation and why\n• an explanation you think is impossible\n• one question you'd want answered\n• your final group decision\nUse must be, might be, could be, and can't be at least once each.\nChallenge\nCompare your group's theory with another group's.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ science vocabulary\n☐ modals of speculation and deduction\n☐ how to speculate about the present\n☐ how to express certainty and doubt\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short paragraph speculating about an unexplained mystery. OR Research one recent \nscientific discovery and summarise it in English.\n\n"},{"number":17,"title":"Arts & Entertainment","content":"LESSON 17 Arts & Entertainment\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk about imaginary past and present situations related to creativity.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What kind of music, film, or art do you enjoy most?\n2. Have you ever wanted to be a performer or artist?\n3. How has entertainment changed since you were a child?\n4. What's a film or song that changed how you see things?\n5. Do you think talent or hard work matters more in the arts?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ plays a musical instrument\n☐ can draw or paint well\n☐ has performed in front of an audience\n☐ prefers books to films\n☐ wishes they had learned an instrument\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Arts & Entertainment\nWORD EXAMPLE\ntalented She's incredibly talented at singing.\nmasterpiece The film is considered a masterpiece.\ninspire Great art can inspire real change.\nperformance The performance received a standing ovation.\ncreative He's always been a creative thinker.\naudience The audience loved the show.\nUseful Expressions\n• If I'd stuck with it, I'd be...\n• I regret not...\n• It's never too late to...\n• That really speaks to me.\n• It moved me to tears.\n• I wasn't a fan at first, but...\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ntalented • masterpiece • performance • creative • audience\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Musician Who Almost Quit\nREAD\nIf Bashir hadn't kept practising during difficult years, he wouldn't be a professional musician \ntoday. He almost gave up when his family couldn't afford lessons, but a local teacher offered to \ntrain him for free. If that teacher hadn't believed in him, Bashir probably wouldn't be performing \non stages across the country now. He says that if he gave up creativity today, he wouldn't feel like \nhimself.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What might have happened if Bashir had given up?\n2. Who helped him continue?\n3. What is his life like now as a result?\n4. Find a mixed conditional (past condition, present result) in the text.\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nMixed Conditionals\nUse mixed conditionals to connect a past condition with a present result, or a present condition with a \npast result, when the timing doesn't match a standard conditional.\nExamples\n• If I hadn't studied art, I wouldn't be a designer now.\n• If she weren't so disciplined, she wouldn't have finished the album.\n• If he hadn't quit his job, he wouldn't be famous today.\n• If I were more confident, I would have auditioned last year.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. If I ______ (not/study) music, I wouldn't be a teacher now.\n2. If she ______ (be) braver, she would have performed at the concert.\n3. If he hadn't practised so much, he ______ (not/be) so skilled now.\n4. If I were more creative, I ______ (write) a song by now.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE LIFE NOT LIVED\nDiscuss with your partner how your life might be different because of past decisions related to art \nor creativity.\nTry to cover:\n• a creative skill you gave up or never tried\n• how your life might be different now if you hadn't\n• a decision that shaped who you are creatively today\n• one creative goal for the future\n• answer your partner's follow-up questions\nUse at least two mixed conditional sentences.\nChallenge\nShare the most surprising 'what if' with the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ arts and entertainment vocabulary\n☐ mixed conditionals\n☐ how to connect past and present hypotheticals\n☐ how to talk about regret and reflection\nHOMEWORK\nWrite three mixed conditional sentences about your own life. OR Describe a piece of art or music \nthat has influenced you.\n\n"},{"number":18,"title":"Global Issues","content":"LESSON 18 Global Issues\nCAN-DO GOAL: Report general opinions and beliefs using passive reporting structures.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What global issue concerns you the most?\n2. Do you think individuals can make a real difference?\n3. How connected do you feel to news from other countries?\n4. What's an issue that gets less attention than it should?\n5. Do you believe the world is generally improving or declining?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ follows international news closely\n☐ has donated to a cause\n☐ has volunteered for something\n☐ believes technology will solve major problems\n☐ worries about global inequality\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Global Issues\nWORD EXAMPLE\ninequality Global inequality remains a serious issue.\npoverty Millions of people still live in poverty.\nhumanitarian The organisation provides humanitarian aid.\nawareness Raising awareness is the first step to change.\nglobal Climate change is a global issue.\nsolidarity The campaign was built on international solidarity.\nUseful Expressions\n• It is widely believed that...\n• It is often said that...\n• It is thought that...\n• Some argue that...\n• There's a growing consensus that...\n• Opinions are divided on this.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ninequality • humanitarian • solidarity • poverty • global\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA World of Contrasts\nREAD\nIt is often said that the world has never been more connected, yet it is also believed by many \neconomists that inequality between regions continues to grow. It is reported that access to clean \nwater has improved significantly over the past twenty years, but it is estimated that hundreds of \nmillions of people still lack basic sanitation. It is thought that solving these problems will require \ncooperation between governments, businesses, and individuals.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What is often said about the world?\n2. What do many economists believe?\n3. What has improved, according to the text?\n4. What is thought to be needed to solve global problems?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPassive Reporting Structures — It is said that...\nUse passive reporting structures (It is said/believed/thought/reported that...) to talk about general \nopinions or facts without naming a specific source.\nExamples\n• It is said that education is the key to progress.\n• It is believed that the situation will improve.\n• It is reported that the numbers have increased.\n• It is thought that cooperation is essential.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. It ______ (say) that clean water access has improved.\n2. It ______ (believe) that poverty rates will fall by 2030.\n3. It ______ (report) that the programme helped thousands of families.\n4. It ______ (think) that education reduces inequality.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE GLOBAL PANEL\nYour group is a panel discussing a global issue of your choice for an international audience.\nTry to cover:\n• state the issue and why it matters\n• share what is generally believed about it\n• share what is often reported about it\n• suggest one possible solution\n• answer questions from the 'audience'\nUse at least three passive reporting structures.\nChallenge\nAnother group asks your panel three follow-up questions.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ global issues vocabulary\n☐ passive reporting structures\n☐ how to report general opinions\n☐ how to discuss solutions\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short paragraph about a global issue using passive reporting structures. OR Summarise a \nnews article using It is said/reported/believed that...\n\n"},{"number":19,"title":"Free Time & Hobbies","content":"LESSON 19 Free Time & Hobbies\nCAN-DO GOAL: Talk naturally about hobbies and habits using common phrasal verbs.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How do you usually unwind after a busy day?\n2. What hobby have you picked up recently?\n3. Is there a hobby you gave up that you'd like to take up again?\n4. Do you prefer active or relaxing hobbies?\n5. What hobby would you like to try out one day?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has taken up a new hobby this year\n☐ has given up a hobby\n☐ spends free time outdoors\n☐ collects something\n☐ would like to take up painting or drawing\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Free Time & Hobbies\nWORD EXAMPLE\n\ntake up She took up photography last year.\ngive up He gave up playing guitar years ago.\nhang out We usually hang out on weekends.\nunwind Reading helps me unwind after work.\ntry out I want to try out rock climbing.\nget into I recently got into painting.\nUseful Expressions\n• I've really got into...\n• It helps me switch off.\n• It's a great way to unwind.\n• I picked it up during lockdown.\n• I never stuck with it.\n• It's my go-to hobby.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nhobby • leisure • relaxation • creativity • unwind\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nFinding Time for Fun\nREAD\nLayla recently took up gardening after giving up her old hobby of painting, which she found too \nstressful to keep up with alongside work. She now spends weekends hanging out in her small \ngarden, growing vegetables and flowers. She says it helps her switch off completely, and she has \neven talked her sister into trying it out too. Her sister has since got into growing herbs on her \nbalcony.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What hobby did Layla give up and why?\n2. What does she do now instead?\n3. How does she describe the effect of gardening?\n4. What has her sister started doing?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nPhrasal Verbs\nPhrasal verbs are verbs combined with a particle that often change meaning. Learn them as whole \nchunks, since the meaning usually can't be guessed from the individual words.\nExamples\n• I took up painting last year.\n• She gave up playing the piano.\n• We hung out at the park all afternoon.\n• He talked me into trying yoga.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I really want to ______ (try out) surfing this summer.\n2. He ______ (give up) football after his injury.\n3. Reading always helps me ______ (unwind) before bed.\n4. She ______ (get into) painting during the holidays.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE HOBBY SWAP\nIntroduce a partner to one of your hobbies and try to convince them to take it up.\nTry to cover:\n• explain how you got into the hobby\n• explain why you haven't given it up\n• describe how it helps you unwind\n• convince your partner to try it out\n• ask what hobby they'd recommend to you\nUse at least four phrasal verbs from this lesson.\nChallenge\nReport back which hobby you'd most like to take up.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ hobby-related phrasal verbs\n☐ how phrasal verbs change meaning\n☐ how to talk about free time\n☐ how to persuade someone\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a paragraph about your hobbies using at least five phrasal verbs. OR Try a new hobby this \nweek and describe the experience.\n\n"},{"number":20,"title":"Cultural Identity","content":"LESSON 20 Cultural Identity\nCAN-DO GOAL: Express wishes and regrets about the present and past.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What part of your culture are you most proud of?\n2. Has your sense of identity changed as you've grown older?\n3. Do you think it's important to preserve traditions?\n4. What's something about your culture that outsiders often misunderstand?\n5. Is there a tradition you wish you knew more about?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ speaks more than two languages\n☐ wishes they knew their family history better\n☐ has taught someone about their culture\n☐ feels connected to more than one culture\n☐ wishes they practised a tradition more often\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Cultural Identity\nWORD EXAMPLE\nheritage She's proud of her cultural heritage.\ntradition This tradition has been passed down for generations.\nidentity Language is a big part of my identity.\nbelong I feel like I truly belong here.\npreserve We should preserve our traditions for future generations.\ndiaspora The Somali diaspora is spread across the world.\nUseful Expressions\n• I wish I knew more about...\n• If only I had learned...\n• I sometimes feel torn between...\n• It's part of who I am.\n• I wish things were different.\n• I regret not asking my grandparents more.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nheritage • identity • tradition • diaspora • generation\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nBetween Two Worlds\nREAD\nIfrah grew up abroad but often wishes she had learned more of her mother tongue as a child. If \nonly her parents had spoken it more often at home, she says, she wouldn't struggle so much with it \nnow. She wishes she knew more traditional stories, the ones her grandmother used to tell. \nRecently, she has started taking language classes, hoping it's not too late to reconnect with that \npart of her identity.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What does Ifrah wish had happened when she was a child?\n2. What does she wish she knew more about?\n3. What has she started doing recently?\n4. How does she feel about her identity?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nWish / If Only\nUse wish + past simple for present regrets, wish + past perfect for past regrets, and if only for a \nstronger, more emotional version of wish.\nExamples\n• I wish I spoke my language more fluently.\n• I wish I had learned it as a child.\n• If only I had listened more carefully.\n• She wishes she knew more traditional stories.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. I wish I ______ (speak) my mother tongue more fluently.\n2. If only I ______ (learn) more about my culture as a child.\n3. She wishes she ______ (know) more traditional recipes.\n4. I wish my grandparents ______ (be) here to see this.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nROOTS AND WISHES\nShare with your group something you wish you knew more about regarding your own culture or \nfamily history.\nTry to cover:\n• something you wish you had learned as a child\n• a tradition you'd like to preserve\n• a question you wish you could ask an older relative\n• how your identity has changed over time\n• one thing you're proud of about your heritage\nUse wish and if only at least twice combined.\nChallenge\nShare one tradition your group would like to learn more about together.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ identity and heritage vocabulary\n☐ wish / if only\n☐ how to express regret\n☐ how to talk about identity\nHOMEWORK\nWrite three sentences about things you wish were different, using wish or if only. OR Interview a \nrelative about a family tradition.\n\n"},{"number":21,"title":"Making Decisions","content":"LESSON 21 Making Decisions\nCAN-DO GOAL: Ask indirect questions politely and check understanding using question tags.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. How do you usually make big decisions?\n2. Do you ask others for advice, or decide alone?\n3. What's the hardest decision you've had to make?\n4. Do you tend to decide quickly or take your time?\n5. What helps you feel confident about a decision?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ makes decisions quickly\n☐ always asks for advice first\n☐ has changed their mind about something big\n☐ regrets a decision\n☐ trusts their instincts\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Making Decisions\nWORD EXAMPLE\ninstinct I trusted my instinct and it worked out.\nweigh up I need to weigh up the pros and cons.\ncommit It's hard to commit to a final decision.\nhesitate Don't hesitate to ask for help.\nindecisive I can be quite indecisive sometimes.\nconfident She felt confident about her choice.\nUseful Expressions\n• Could you tell me...?\n• I was wondering if...\n• You made the right choice, didn't you?\n• It's a tough one, isn't it?\n• Do you happen to know...?\n• I'd like to ask you something, if that's okay.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\ndecision • instinct • confident • hesitate • indecisive\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nThe Job Offer\nREAD\nNasra was offered two jobs at the same time, and she wasn't sure which to choose. She asked her \nmentor if she could help her weigh up the options, didn't she always give good advice? Her mentor \nasked her what really mattered to her — money, growth, or flexibility. In the end, Nasra realised \nshe already knew the answer; she just needed someone to ask the right questions.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What decision did Nasra face?\n2. Who did she ask for help?\n3. What question did her mentor ask her?\n4. What did Nasra realise in the end?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nQuestion Tags and Indirect Questions\nUse question tags to check information or invite agreement (positive statement + negative tag, or the \nreverse). Use indirect questions to sound more polite, especially with strangers or in formal situations.\nExamples\n• You've decided already, haven't you?\n• It's a difficult choice, isn't it?\n• Could you tell me what you would do?\n• I was wondering if you could give me some advice.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. You're going to accept the offer, ______?\n2. She hasn't decided yet, ______?\n3. Could you tell me ______ (what/should I do)?\n4. I was wondering ______ (if/you could help) me decide.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE ADVICE BOOTH\nOne classmate has a real or imaginary decision to make; the other gives advice using indirect \nquestions.\nTry to cover:\n• explain the decision you're facing\n• ask for advice politely using indirect questions\n• use at least two question tags in the conversation\n• consider the pros and cons together\n• reach a final decision\nSwitch roles after five minutes.\nChallenge\nShare the most interesting decision discussed in your pair with the class.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ decision-making vocabulary\n☐ question tags\n☐ indirect questions\n☐ how to ask for advice politely\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a short dialogue using question tags and indirect questions. OR Ask someone for advice in \nEnglish and note down their response.\n\n"},{"number":22,"title":"Looking Back, Looking Forward","content":"LESSON 22 Looking Back, Looking Forward\nCAN-DO GOAL: Review key structures from the course and confidently discuss personal growth.\nPAGE 1 — WARM UP\nThink & Talk\n1. What's the biggest improvement you've noticed in your English?\n2. Which lesson topic did you enjoy the most?\n3. What was the most difficult grammar point for you?\n4. How has your confidence in speaking changed since Lesson 1?\n5. What's your goal for your English over the next six months?\nSPEAKING CHALLENGE\nFind someone who...\n☐ has used English outside class this term\n☐ feels more confident speaking now\n☐ wants to take an exam\n☐ has a favourite lesson from this book\n☐ plans to keep studying after this course\nWrite their names below:\n_________________________ _________________________ _________________________\n\n\n\nPAGE 2 — VOCABULARY\nTalking About Looking Back, Looking Forward\nWORD EXAMPLE\nfluency Her fluency has improved a lot this term.\nprogress We've made real progress since Lesson 1.\nconfidence Speaking practice builds confidence.\nmilestone Finishing this book is a big milestone.\nconsistency Consistency is the key to improving a language.\nachievement This course has been a real achievement.\nUseful Expressions\n• Looking back, I've realised...\n• I've come a long way.\n• I still need to work on...\n• I'm proud of how far I've come.\n• Going forward, I plan to...\n• This has been a real journey.\nPronunciation — Repeat after your teacher\nfluency • confidence • achievement • progress • consistency\n\n\n\nPAGE 3 — READING\nA Term of Growth\nREAD\nWhen Deeqa started this course, she could barely hold a two-minute conversation in English. If \nsomeone had told her she would be discussing global issues and giving business pitches in English \nby the end of the term, she wouldn't have believed them. She has been practising consistently, and \nshe has made mistakes along the way — but she says that's exactly how she has learned the most.\nCheck Your Understanding\n1. What could Deeqa barely do at the start of the course?\n2. What is she able to do now?\n3. What does she say helped her learn the most?\n4. Which grammar structures can you find in this passage?\n\n\n\nPAGE 4 — LANGUAGE FOCUS\nMixed Review — Tenses, Conditionals, and Future Forms\nThis lesson reviews structures from across the book: present and past tenses, present perfect, \nconditionals, passives, modals, and future forms — all working together in natural speech.\nExamples\n• I've learned a lot since I started this course.\n• If I hadn't practised every week, I wouldn't be this confident.\n• I'm going to keep studying after this term.\n• It's said that consistency is the key to fluency.\nComplete the Sentences\n1. Since starting this course, I ______ (learn) so much.\n2. If I ______ (not/practise) regularly, I wouldn't be this confident now.\n3. Next term, I ______ (going to) focus on speaking more.\n4. It ______ (say) that mistakes are part of learning.\nMake It Personal — write four true sentences about yourself using this structure.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4.\n\n\n\nPAGE 5 — FLUENCY MISSION\nTHE FINAL FLUENCY MISSION\nGive a two-minute talk to your group reflecting on your journey through this course.\nTry to cover:\n• one lesson or topic that stood out to you\n• one grammar point you now feel confident using\n• a challenge you overcame this term\n• how your speaking confidence has changed\n• your goal for continuing your English journey\nTry to use at least five different grammar structures from across the book.\nChallenge\nAs a class, share one thing you're proud of from this course.\n\n\n\nPAGE 6 — REFLECTION\nToday I learned...\n☐ a full review of course vocabulary\n☐ how the grammar points connect\n☐ how much your fluency has grown\n☐ your plan for continuing to learn\nHOMEWORK\nWrite a one-page reflection on your English learning journey this term. OR Record a two-minute \nvideo reflecting on your progress, in English."}]},"career-fluency-muna":{"title":"Career Fluency — Muna Edition","lessons":[{"number":1,"title":"Your starting point","content":"WEEK 1 | LESSON 1 | EXPLORE\nYour starting point\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Complete a friendly speaking diagnostic and identify one clear fluency goal.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n10.Where did you last use English?\n11.What can you already do well?\n12.What speaking situation feels difficult?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nTutor: Tell me a little about yourself and how you use English.\nStudent: In my experience, I use English at work, online and when I travel.\nTutor: What would you most like to improve?\nStudent: Let me think for a moment. One goal I have is to speak with fewer pauses.\nTutor: When do you feel most confident?\nStudent: I feel confident when I read or listen, but spontaneous speaking is harder.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n13.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n14.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n15.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nLet me think for a moment. need time without becoming silent\nWhat I mean is... restate an idea more clearly\nCould you repeat the question? need to hear a question again\nIn my experience... connect an answer to real life\nOne goal I have is... state a learning or career goal\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ me think for a moment.\n2. _______ I mean is...\n3. Could you _______ the question?\n4. In my __________...\n5. _______ goal I have is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Use complete simple sentences; accuracy is observed but not taught today.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 1 | LESSON 1 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nYour starting point\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Speak at a natural volume and finish each thought clearly.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask three diagnostic questions about work, daily life and future goals. Take brief notes. B: Answer each \nquestion for 30-60 seconds. Ask for repetition when needed.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes about who you are, how you use English and what you want to achieve in twelve \nweeks.\nSUCCESS CHECK I complete the task, use at least two rescue phrases and choose one measurable goal.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord a two-minute baseline voice note. Do not restart it. Write three things you want to improve.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":2,"title":"A strong professional introduction","content":"WEEK 1 | LESSON 2 | BUILD\nA strong professional introduction\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Introduce yourself with a clear beginning, useful details and a forward-looking ending.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n16.Which three facts belong in a professional introduction?\n17.What detail should you leave out?\n18.How can you end confidently?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nAmina: It’s good to meet you. What do you do?\nHassan\nAmina: What are you responsible for?\nHassan\nAmina: What would you like to do next?\nHassan\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n19.How does the speaker begin?\n20.What detail develops the message?\n21.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI currently work as... state your present role\nMy background is in... describe education or professional field\nI’m responsible for... explain a main duty\nI’m particularly interested in... name a professional interest\nMy current goal is to... finish with a future direction\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I _________ work as...\n2. My __________ is in...\n3. I’m ___________ for...\n4. I’m ____________ interested in...\n5. My _______ goal is to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple with be, have and work; because for reasons.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nI currently work as a general practitioner. My background is in medicine and primary healthcare.\nI’m responsible for assessing patients, explaining treatment and preparing clinical notes.\nMy current goal is to communicate confidently with patients, colleagues and interviewers.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 1 | LESSON 2 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nA strong professional introduction\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the key words: name, role, field, skills and goal.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: You are meeting a new colleague at a regional conference. Ask about role, background and goals. B: Give a \n60-second professional introduction, then ask your partner one question.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a 90-second introduction without reading full sentences.\nSUCCESS CHECK My introduction includes role, background, responsibility, interest and goal.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord the introduction twice. Compare the recordings and note one improvement in fluency.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":3,"title":"Keep speaking without long pauses","content":"WEEK 1 | LESSON 3 | PERFORM\nKeep speaking without long pauses\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Use thinking phrases, connectors and repair strategies to maintain your turn.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n22.What topic can you discuss easily for one minute?\n23.Which connector do you use most?\n24.What do you say when a sentence goes wrong?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: Why is English important for your career?\nStudent: There are two main reasons. The first point is communication at work.\nTutor: Can you give me an example?\nStudent: For example, I sometimes need to speak with people from different countries.\nTutor: And the second reason?\nStudent: Another thing is career growth. Let me say that another way: English can open more professional opportunities.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n25.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n26.Where does the speaker pause?\n27.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThere are two main reasons. organise an answer before giving details\nThe first point is... start your first idea\nAnother thing is... add a second idea\nFor example... support an idea with evidence\nLet me say that another way. repair an unclear sentence\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ are two main reasons.\n2. The _______ point is...\n3. _______ thing is...\n4. For _______...\n5. _______ me say that another way.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Join ideas with and, but, because and so.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 1 | LESSON 3 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nKeep speaking without long pauses\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause between idea groups, not after every word.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask ‘why’ and ‘how’ questions. Wait silently while your partner uses a thinking phrase. B: Answer each \nquestion with two points and one example.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes: ‘Why I am learning English now.’ Repeat the same talk with fewer pauses.\nSUCCESS CHECK I use two organising phrases, one example and one repair phrase instead of stopping.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nComplete three one-minute speaking sprints: work, technology and travel. Save the final recording.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":4,"title":"Start conversations naturally","content":"WEEK 2 | LESSON 4 | EXPLORE\nStart conversations naturally\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Open a conversation in social and professional situations.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n28.Where do you usually meet new people?\n29.Which opening question feels natural to you?\n30.What makes an opening question too personal?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nSalma: Hi, have we met before?\nOmar: I don’t think so.\nSalma: Nice to meet you. What brings you here?\nOmar: I’m attending the digital skills workshop.\nSalma: That sounds interesting. How do you know the organiser?\nOmar: We worked together on a community project last year.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n31.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n32.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n33.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nHow has your day been so far? open a relaxed conversation\nWhat brings you here? ask about purpose at an event\nHave we met before? check whether you know someone\nHow do you know the team? find a shared connection\nThat sounds interesting. respond positively before another question\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ has your day been so far?\n2. _______ brings you here?\n3. Have we _______ before?\n4. _______ do you know the team?\n5. That _______ interesting.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Wh- questions and present simple question order.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nI’m Omar from the clinic team.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 2 | LESSON 4 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nStart conversations naturally\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use rising intonation for friendly opening questions.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Choose a situation: workshop, airport, online meeting or office reception. Start the conversation. B: \nRespond, add one detail and ask a question back.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Start three different two-minute conversations without using the same opening twice.\nSUCCESS CHECK I greet, open, respond to the answer and move to a relevant follow-up question.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite five safe opening questions. Use two of them in a real or simulated conversation.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":5,"title":"Show interest and ask follow-ups","content":"WEEK 2 | LESSON 5 | BUILD\nShow interest and ask follow-ups\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Keep a conversation going by responding, echoing and asking connected questions.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n34.How do you show interest in Somali?\n35.Which English reactions sound natural?\n36.Which question can follow almost any story?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nAli: I recently started learning mapping software.\nNimo: Really? How did you get interested in that?\nAli:\nNimo: What was that like at the beginning?\nAli: It was challenging, but I enjoyed it.\nNimo: I can relate to that. New software often feels difficult before it becomes useful.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n37.How does the speaker begin?\n38.What detail develops the message?\n39.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nReally? What happened next? invite someone to continue a story\nHow did you get interested in that? ask about the origin of an interest\nWhat was that like? ask about an experience\nSo, you mean that...? check your understanding\nI can relate to that. connect the speaker’s experience to yours\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______? What happened next?\n2. _______ did you get interested in that?\n3. _______ was that like?\n4. So, you _______ that...?\n5. I _______ relate to that.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Follow-up questions with how, why, when and what happened next.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nA colleague showed me how it can improve patient communication.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 2 | LESSON 5 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nShow interest and ask follow-ups\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Let your voice rise slightly to show real interest.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Tell a short story about learning a skill. Pause after each important detail. B: React first, then ask a \nconnected follow-up. Do not change the topic suddenly.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Keep one conversation going for six exchanges using at least three follow-up expressions.\nSUCCESS CHECK Every question connects to my partner’s previous answer.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nListen to a short interview or conversation. Write down three follow-up questions you hear or could ask.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":6,"title":"End conversations professionally","content":"WEEK 2 | LESSON 6 | PERFORM\nEnd conversations professionally\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Close conversations warmly, confirm next steps and leave a positive final impression.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n40.Why can ending a conversation feel difficult?\n41.What next step might you confirm?\n42.Which closing is formal enough for work?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nMaryan: I should let you get back to the workshop.\nDavid: Of course. It was great speaking with you.\nMaryan: Let’s stay in touch. I’ll send you the project link this afternoon.\nDavid: Thank you. I’ll review it tomorrow.\nMaryan: Excellent. I look forward to speaking again.\nDavid: Likewise. Enjoy the rest of the event.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n43.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n44.Where does the speaker pause?\n45.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nIt was great speaking with you. signal a warm ending\nI should let you get back to... respect the other person’s time\nLet’s stay in touch. suggest future contact\nI’ll send you the details. promise a next action\nI look forward to speaking again. close a professional interaction\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. It _______ great speaking with you.\n2. I _______ let you get back to...\n3. _______’s stay in touch.\n4. I’ll _______ you the details.\n5. I _______ forward to speaking again.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future arrangements with will and present continuous.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 2 | LESSON 6 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nEnd conversations professionally\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use a calm falling tone for a clear, polite ending.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Continue a conversation for two minutes. Then give a time signal so your partner can close it. B: Notice the \nsignal, close politely and confirm one next action.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Open, maintain and close a complete four-minute conversation.\nSUCCESS CHECK My closing is polite, natural and includes a clear next step when appropriate.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nSend a 45-second voice message that ends a professional conversation and confirms a follow-up.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":7,"title":"Describe your work clearly","content":"WEEK 3 | LESSON 7 | EXPLORE\nDescribe your work clearly\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain your role, team and responsibilities to someone outside your field.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n46.How would you explain your job to a 12-year-old?\n47.Who depends on your work?\n48.What result does your role produce?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nGuest: What exactly do you do?\nGuest: What does a typical task involve?\nGuest: Who do you work with?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n49.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n50.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n51.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nMy main responsibility is... name your most important duty\nI work closely with... describe collaboration\nA typical task involves... explain a regular activity\nThe purpose of my role is to... explain why your job exists\nIn simple terms... make technical information accessible\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. My _______ responsibility is...\n2. I _______ closely with...\n3. A _______ task involves...\n4. The _______ of my role is to...\n5. In _______ terms...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple for regular duties; noun phrases for roles and departments.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nAdvisor:\nAdvisor:\nAdvisor:\nIn simple terms, I examine patients, identify common health problems and explain what to do next.\nA typical task involves taking a history, examining a patient and deciding on appropriate care.\nI work closely with nurses, laboratory staff and other healthcare professionals.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 3 | LESSON 7 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDescribe your work clearly\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR \nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: You know nothing about your partner’s job. Ask for simple explanations and examples. B: Explain your role\nwithout unexplained technical words.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute ‘My work in simple terms’ explanation.\nSUCCESS CHECK A listener outside my field can understand my role, main duty, collaborators and result.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a personal job vocabulary map with ten words. Record five sentences using the most useful words.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -\nMark word stress in responsibility, patient, medical and communication.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":8,"title":"Routines and current priorities","content":"WEEK 3 | LESSON 8 | BUILD\nRoutines and current priorities\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Compare regular responsibilities with what you are working on now.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n52.Which duties happen every week?\n53.What is different this week?\n54.How do you decide your top priority?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nTutor: What does a normal week look like for you?\nStudent: Most weeks, I review plans and coordinate with colleagues.\nTutor: What are you working on at the moment?\nStudent\nTutor: Is this week typical?\nStudent: This week is different because we have an important deadline on Thursday.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n55.How does the speaker begin?\n56.What detail develops the message?\n57.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI usually spend my mornings... describe a normal routine\nAt the moment, I’m working on...\nMost weeks, I... describe a frequent pattern\nThis week is different because... contrast routine and present\nMy top priority today is... identify the most urgent task\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I _______ spend my mornings...\n2. At the _______, I’m working on...\n3. _______ weeks, I...\n4. This _______ is different because...\n5. My _______ priority today is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple versus present continuous; adverbs of frequency.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nname a current assignment\nAt the moment, I’m preparing patient notes and following up on test results.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 3 | LESSON 8 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nRoutines and current priorities\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pronounce final -s clearly in works, checks, plans and manages.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask about normal routines, current work and today’s priority. B: Contrast at least two routine duties with \nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes about ‘My normal work and my current priority.’\nSUCCESS CHECK I use present simple for routine and present continuous for current action.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nKeep a one-day English work log. Write five routine statements and three current- action statements.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -\none current assignment.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":9,"title":"Give a useful work update","content":"WEEK 3 | LESSON 9 | PERFORM\nGive a useful work update\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Report progress, completed actions, current work and next steps.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n58.What makes an update useful?\n59.Which detail should come first?\n60.How can you report a problem without sounding negative?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nManager: Can you give me a quick update?\nEmployee: So far, I’ve completed the first review. I’m currently checking the final details.\nManager: Is anything blocking the work?\nEmployee: The main issue is one missing document, but I expect to receive it today.\nManager: What happens next?\nEmployee: My next step is to update the plan and send it by 3 p.m.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n61.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n62.Where does the speaker pause?\n63.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nSo far, I’ve completed... report completed progress\nI’m currently checking... describe work in progress\nThe main issue is... identify a blocker\nI expect to finish by... give a realistic deadline\nMy next step is to... state the immediate action\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. So _______, I’ve completed...\n2. I’m _________ checking...\n3. The _______ issue is...\n4. I _______ to finish by...\n5. My _______ step is to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present perfect for progress; present continuous for current work; will for next action.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 3 | LESSON 9 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nGive a useful work update\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Link contractions smoothly: I’ve completed, we’re checking, I’ll send.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: You are a manager. Ask about progress, current work, blockers, deadline and next step. B: Give a concise \n60-second update using the five-part structure.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a work update in one minute, then answer two follow-up questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My listener knows what is done, happening now, blocked, due and next.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord two updates: a 30-second version and a 60-second version. Keep the clearest one.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nMODULE 2\nWORKPLACE & INTERNATIONAL ENGLISH\nUse English for experiences, international communication, problems, meetings and professional\nmessages.\n10 EXPLORE Tell a past experience\n11 BUILD Sequence a professional story\n12 PERFORM Describe success and challenge\n13 EXPLORE Meet international contacts\n14 BUILD Clarify and confirm meaning\n15 PERFORM Phone and video calls\n16 EXPLORE Describe a workplace problem\n17 BUILD Suggest and compare solutions\n18 PERFORM Explain a process step by step\n19 EXPLORE Share opinions in meetings\n20 BUILD Agree and disagree politely\n21 PERFORM Messages and follow-up emails\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":10,"title":"Tell a past experience","content":"WEEK 4 | LESSON 10 | EXPLORE\nTell a past experience\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe a past professional or personal experience with clear time references.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n64.Which recent experience helped you grow?\n65.What exactly did you do?\n66.What did the experience teach you?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a useful learning experience.\nCandidate\nInterviewer: What was your role?\nCandidate: My role was to complete the practical tasks and support another participant.\nInterviewer: What did you learn?\nCandidate: The most useful part was solving a real problem. That experience taught me to ask clearer questions.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n67.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n68.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n69.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nA few months ago... place an experience in time\nI had the opportunity to... introduce a valuable experience\nMy role was to... state your responsibility then\nThe most useful part was... highlight value\nThat experience taught me... state a lesson\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. A _______ months ago...\n2. I _______ the opportunity to...\n3. My _______ was to...\n4. The _______ useful part was...\n5. That __________ taught me...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple: regular and common irregular verbs.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nA few months ago, I had the opportunity to join a short clinical training workshop.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 4 | LESSON 10 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nTell a past experience\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Practise -ed endings in worked, planned, needed and completed.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask for the time, situation, role, action and lesson. B: Tell one past experience in chronological order.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Tell a two-minute story about learning, work, travel or teamwork.\nSUCCESS CHECK My listener can identify when it happened, what I did and what I learned.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite six past-tense verbs from your story. Record the story again with clear endings.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":11,"title":"Sequence a professional story","content":"WEEK 4 | LESSON 11 | BUILD\nSequence a professional story\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Organise events so a listener can follow what happened from beginning to end.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n70.What happens when a story has no sequence markers?\n71.Which action was happening in the background?\n72.What result ended your story?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nColleague: How did you solve the scheduling problem?\nEmployee:\nColleague: What did you do?\nEmployee\nColleague: Did that work?\nEmployee\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n73.How does the speaker begin?\n74.What detail develops the message?\n75.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nAt first... describe the starting situation\nWhile I was..., ... show two actions happening together\nAfter that... move to the next event\nAs a result... connect an action to its effect\nIn the end... give the final outcome\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. At _______...\n2. _______ I was..., ...\n3. _______ that...\n4. As a _______...\n5. In the _______...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple and past continuous; sequence markers.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nAt first, we did not know which test result was correct.\nWhile I was checking the patient notes, my colleague contacted the laboratory. After that, we compared both results.\nYes. As a result, we clarified the result. In the end, the patient received the right follow-up.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 4 | LESSON 11 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nSequence a professional story\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause briefly after first, while, after that and finally.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Listen and draw a four-step timeline. Ask questions if an event is unclear. B: Tell a story with a clear \nbeginning, middle, result and ending.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Retell one professional story in three minutes using all five sequence expressions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My story is easy to follow and each event leads naturally to the next.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a five-box timeline for your story. Practise speaking from the timeline, not a full script.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":12,"title":"Describe success and challenge","content":"WEEK 4 | LESSON 12 | PERFORM\nDescribe success and challenge\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain what went well, what was difficult and how you responded.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n76.What challenge can you discuss professionally?\n77.What action did you take?\n78.What would you improve next time?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: Tell me about a challenging task.\nStudent: The main challenge was a very short deadline.\nTutor: How did you respond?\nStudent: Although it was difficult, we divided the work clearly. We decided to check progress every afternoon.\nTutor: What worked well?\nStudent: What worked well was the communication. If I did it again, I would confirm responsibilities earlier.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n79.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n80.Where does the speaker pause?\n81.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThe main challenge was... identify the difficult part\nAlthough it was difficult, ... show a positive contrast\nWe decided to... describe a chosen response\nWhat worked well was... identify a success factor\nIf I did it again, I would... reflect on improvement\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. The _______ challenge was...\n2. ________ it was difficult, ...\n3. We _______ to...\n4. _______ worked well was...\n5. If I _______ it again, I would...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION because, so, although and however for cause and contrast.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 4 | LESSON 12 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDescribe success and challenge\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress contrast words to make the message clear.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask neutral questions about challenge, response, result and reflection. B: Describe a challenge without \nblaming another person.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a balanced two-minute account of a challenge and successful response.\nSUCCESS CHECK I explain the problem, action, positive result and lesson in a professional tone.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\n\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a six-sentence reflection using because, so, although and however.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":13,"title":"Meet international contacts","content":"WEEK 5 | LESSON 13 | EXPLORE\nMeet international contacts\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Introduce yourself, find common ground and adapt your language for a new international contact.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n82.What information is safe to share in a first meeting?\n83.How can you find common ground?\n84.How do you show respect for a different perspective?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nLayla: Hello, where are you joining from?\nDaniel: I’m joining from Nairobi. What kind of work do you do?\nLayla\nDaniel\nLayla: We seem to have that in common. It’s useful to hear your perspective.\nDaniel\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n85.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n86.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n87.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nWhere are you joining from? open an online international meeting\nWhat kind of work do you do? ask about a professional field\nHow long have you worked in that area? ask about experience\nWe seem to have that in common. identify shared experience\nIt’s useful to hear your perspective. value another viewpoint\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ are you joining from?\n2. _______ kind of work do you do?\n3. _______ long have you worked in that area?\n4. We _______ to have that in common.\n5. It’s _______ to hear your perspective.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple and present perfect for background and experience.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\n Likewise. I’d like to learn more about your current assignment.\nI work as a general practitioner. I’ve worked in healthcare for several years.\nInteresting. I also work with patients and healthcare teams.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 5 | LESSON 13 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nMeet international contacts\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Say names, countries and organisations slowly and clearly.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Choose a fictional country and professional role. Ask about location, work and experience. B: Introduce \nyour real background and identify one shared interest.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete a five-minute international networking conversation.\nSUCCESS CHECK I speak clearly, avoid local-only references and ask respectful follow-up questions.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nPrepare a 60-second introduction for an international online meeting. Record it with clear names and places.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":14,"title":"Clarify and confirm meaning","content":"WEEK 5 | LESSON 14 | BUILD\nClarify and confirm meaning\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Prevent misunderstandings by checking, paraphrasing and confirming important information.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n88.Which work details must always be confirmed?\n89.What is the difference between clarifying and confirming?\n90.How can you ask without sounding rude?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nClient\nEmployee: Could you clarify what you mean by early next week?\nClient: Tuesday morning, before ten.\nEmployee: If I understand correctly, you need the final version by 10 a.m. on Tuesday.\nClient: That’s right.\nEmployee: Let me confirm the details in an email after this call.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n91.How does the speaker begin?\n92.What detail develops the message?\n93.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCould you clarify what you mean by...? ask for a clearer explanation\nIf I understand correctly, ... paraphrase the message\nDo you mean...? check one interpretation\nLet me confirm the details. review important information\nCould you give me an example? request concrete evidence\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. Could you _______ what you mean by...?\n2. If I __________ correctly, ...\n3. Do you _______...?\n4. _______ me confirm the details.\n5. Could you _______ me an example?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect questions and checking structures.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nWe need the updated patient summary early next week.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 5 | LESSON 14 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nClarify and confirm meaning\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the word that needs correction or confirmation.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Give instructions containing one vague time, term or quantity. B: Ask two clarification questions, \nparaphrase and confirm the final details.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Resolve three misunderstandings in a role-play without switching languages.\nSUCCESS CHECK I clarify politely and repeat the final meaning accurately.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite five clarification questions connected to your work. Use two in a recorded role-play.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":15,"title":"Phone and video calls","content":"WEEK 5 | LESSON 15 | PERFORM\nPhone and video calls\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Open, manage and close remote conversations when sound or connection is imperfect.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n94.What call problems happen most often?\n95.How do you check a number accurately?\n96.What should happen before a call ends?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nHost: Good morning. Can you hear me clearly?\nGuest: Mostly, but you’re breaking up a little.\nHost: Thanks for telling me. Is this better?\nGuest\nHost: Certainly: B-4-7-2. Let me share my screen as well.\nGuest: Perfect. At the end, I’ll summarise the next steps.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n97.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n98.Where does the speaker pause?\n99.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCan you hear me clearly? check the audio connection\nYou’re breaking up a little. report an unstable connection\nCould you say that again more slowly? request a clearer repetition\nLet me share my screen. move to visual information\nI’ll summarise the next steps. close with an action review\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ you hear me clearly?\n2. You’re ________ up a little.\n3. Could you _______ that again more slowly?\n4. _______ me share my screen.\n5. I’ll _________ the next steps.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Can, could and would for requests; present continuous for connection problems.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -\nYes. Could you say the patient ID again more slowly?\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 5 | LESSON 15 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nPhone and video calls\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Practise letters, numbers, dates and spelling in short groups.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF \nconfirm the details and summarise the next action.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete a four-minute audio-only work call.\nSUCCESS CHECK I manage the opening, repair one communication problem and close with confirmed steps.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord a simulated phone call. Include a spelling check, a number and a clear closing summary.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -\nA: B: Manage the problem, Read a short patient ID, date and email address.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":16,"title":"Describe a workplace problem","content":"WEEK 6 | LESSON 16 | EXPLORE\nDescribe a workplace problem\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain a problem objectively, including its impact and urgency.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n100. What facts belong in a problem report?\n101. How is impact different from cause?\n102. Why should you avoid guessing?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nManager: What seems to be the problem?\nEmployee\nManager: When did it start?\nEmployee\nManager: What is the most urgent concern?\nEmployee\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n103. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n104. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n105. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nWe’ve noticed an issue with... introduce a problem neutrally\nThe problem started when... give the origin\nThis is affecting... explain impact\nThe most urgent concern is... identify priority\nWhat we know so far is... separate facts from guesses\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. We’ve _______ an issue with...\n2. The _______ started when...\n3. This is _________...\n4. The _______ urgent concern is...\n5. _______ we know so far is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION There is/are, present perfect and passive forms for current problems.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\n The most urgent concern is tomorrow’s submission. What we know so far is that the team has not received the update.\nWe’ve noticed an issue with the latest patient record.\nThe problem started when one test result was entered incorrectly. This is affecting the treatment plan.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 6 | LESSON 16 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDescribe a workplace problem\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use clear final consonants in stopped, missed, changed and delayed.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Give a scenario with a missing document, delayed task or incorrect detail. Ask for facts. B: Report the \nproblem, cause, impact, urgency and known facts.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a 90-second problem report followed by three questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK I am factual, specific and clear about urgency without blaming people.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a short problem report with five headings: issue, start, impact, urgency and known facts.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":17,"title":"Suggest and compare solutions","content":"WEEK 6 | LESSON 17 | BUILD\nSuggest and compare solutions\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Offer options, explain advantages and recommend a practical response.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n106. What makes a recommendation convincing?\n107. How many options should you compare?\n108. Which matters more: speed, cost or quality?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nTeam lead: How can we handle the delay?\nEmployee\nTeam lead: Is there another possibility?\nEmployee\nlimited preparation.\nTeam lead: Which do you prefer?\nEmployee\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n109. How does the speaker begin?\n110. What detail develops the message?\n111. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nOne option would be to... introduce a possible solution\nAnother possibility is... add an alternative\nThe advantage of this option is... explain a benefit\nThe main risk is... identify a disadvantage\nI’d recommend... because... make a reasoned choice\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ option would be to...\n2. _______ possibility is...\n3. The _________ of this option is...\n4. The _______ risk is...\n5. I’d _________... because...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Should, could and might; comparatives for options.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\n One option would be to move the submission to Thursday.\n Another possibility is to ask another analyst to help. The advantage is that we keep the original date, but the main risk is\nI’d recommend Thursday because the current team already knows the patient case.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 6 | LESSON 17 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nSuggest and compare solutions\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Reduce modals naturally: we could, we should, it might.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Present a simple workplace problem and ask for two options. B: Offer two solutions, compare benefits and \nrisks, then recommend one.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Lead a three-minute solution discussion and reach a decision.\nSUCCESS CHECK My recommendation follows a fair comparison and includes a clear reason.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nChoose one everyday problem. Record two options, one risk and your recommendation.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":18,"title":"Explain a process step by step","content":"WEEK 6 | LESSON 18 | PERFORM\nExplain a process step by step\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Make a work or daily process easy to follow for a new person.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n112. Which process do you know well?\n113. Where do beginners usually get confused?\n114. Why does each main step matter?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nNew colleague: How do we prepare the weekly update?\nEmployee:\nNew colleague: What happens next?\nEmployee: Once that is complete, we check the dates and progress. The next step is to write a short summary.\nNew colleague: Is there a final check?\nEmployee:\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n115. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n116. Where does the speaker pause?\n117. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThe process begins with... introduce the first stage\nOnce that is complete, ... show a required sequence\nThe next step is to... move forward\nBefore we finish, we need to... name a final check\nThe purpose of this step is... explain why a step matters\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. The _______ begins with...\n2. _______ that is complete, ...\n3. The _______ step is to...\n4. _______ we finish, we need to...\n5. The _______ of this step is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Sequencing; imperatives and present simple passive where useful.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -\nThe process begins with collecting information from the patient.\nYes. Before we finish, we need to confirm the key details. The purpose of this step is to prevent clinical errors.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 6 | LESSON 18 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nExplain a process step by step\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause after each step and stress action verbs.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Act as a new colleague. Interrupt only when a step, order or purpose is unclear. B: Explain a four- to six￾step process without reading full sentences.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Teach your partner one process and answer clarification questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My explanation has a clear sequence, action verbs and at least one reason.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nDraw a six-step process map. Record the explanation using only the map as notes.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":19,"title":"Share opinions in meetings","content":"WEEK 7 | LESSON 19 | EXPLORE\nShare opinions in meetings\nTODAY'S OUTCOME State an opinion, support it and invite another person to respond.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n118. Which meeting topics require opinions?\n119. What evidence can support an opinion?\n120. How do you invite quieter participants?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nChair: Should we change the weekly meeting time?\nMember: From my point of view, we should move it to the morning.\nChair: Why?\nMember\nChair: Anything else?\nMember: Morning would improve attendance. That’s how I see it. What’s your view?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n121. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n122. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n123. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nFrom my point of view... state a considered opinion\nThe main reason is... support the opinion\nFor instance... add evidence\nThat’s how I see it. close your explanation\nWhat’s your view? invite another speaker\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ my point of view...\n2. The _______ reason is...\n3. For ________...\n4. That’s _______ I see it.\n5. _______’s your view?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Opinion clauses and because for reasons.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nThe main reason is that clinic duties often finish later. For instance, two people missed last week’s meeting.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 7 | LESSON 19 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nShare opinions in meetings\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the main opinion word and the strongest reason.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Give a meeting topic and listen without interrupting. Ask for a reason and example. B: State an opinion, \nreason and example, then invite your partner’s view.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Contribute twice to a five-minute mini-meeting.\nSUCCESS CHECK Each contribution has one clear point, supporting reason and invitation to respond.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord a 90-second opinion about remote work, professional training or technology.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":20,"title":"Agree and disagree politely","content":"WEEK 7 | LESSON 20 | BUILD\nAgree and disagree politely\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Respond to ideas with respect while adding, qualifying or challenging a point.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n124. Why do people sometimes avoid disagreement?\n125. Which phrase softens a challenge?\n126. How can disagreement improve a decision?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nColleague A:\nColleague B:\nColleague A: What would you suggest?\nColleague B:\nColleague A:\nColleague B: I agree with that because it gives us both speed and detail.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n127. How does the speaker begin?\n128. What detail develops the message?\n129. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI agree with that because... give supported agreement\nThat’s a good point. I’d add... build on an idea\nI see your point; however... disagree respectfully\nI’m not completely convinced that... express careful doubt\nCould we consider... instead? offer an alternative\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I _______ with that because...\n2. That’s a _______ point. I’d add...\n3. I _______ your point; however...\n4. I’m _______ completely convinced that...\n5. Could we ________... instead?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Contrast with but, however and while; comparatives.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nI think every case update should be a detailed written note.\nI see your point; however, detailed notes take time to read.\nCould we consider a short handover summary instead?\nThat’s a good point. I’d add a detailed note for complex cases.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 7 | LESSON 20 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nAgree and disagree politely\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use a warm tone when disagreeing; stress the idea, not the person.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: with \nanother and offer an alternative.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Reach agreement after a four-minute respectful disagreement.\nSUCCESS CHECK I respond to the idea, give a reason and avoid personal language.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite three polite disagreements. Read them aloud twice: neutral tone, then warmer tone.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -\nDefend one option: detailed note, handover summary or verbal update.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":21,"title":"Messages and follow-up emails","content":"WEEK 7 | LESSON 21 | PERFORM\nMessages and follow-up emails\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Write and speak concise professional requests, updates and follow-ups.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n130. When should you send a follow-up?\n131. What belongs in the subject line?\n132. How can you make the requested action clear?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: What should your follow-up message include?\nStudent: I’m writing to follow up on yesterday’s meeting.\nTutor: What information do you need?\nStudent: Could you please confirm the final deadline? As discussed, I’ll prepare the first draft.\nTutor: How will you close?\nStudent: Please let me know if you need anything else. Thank you for your assistance.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n133. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n134. Where does the speaker pause?\n135. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI’m writing to follow up on... reopen an earlier topic\nCould you please confirm...? request important information\nAs discussed, ... refer to an agreed point\nPlease let me know if... invite a response or question\nThank you for your assistance. close a professional request\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I’m _______ to follow up on...\n2. Could you please _______...?\n3. As _________, ...\n4. Please _______ me know if...\n5. _______ you for your assistance.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Polite requests with could/would; present perfect for follow-up.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 7 | LESSON 21 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nMessages and follow-up emails\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Read messages aloud in meaningful chunks, not word by word.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Explain a meeting decision and one missing detail. B: Give an oral summary, then draft a 70-word follow-up\nmessage.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Read your message aloud; your partner identifies purpose, action and deadline.\nSUCCESS CHECK My message is polite, concise and makes the next action unmistakable.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a 100-120-word professional email requesting confirmation of a deadline or meeting.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nMODULE 3\nINTERVIEW & CAREER COMMUNICATION\nTurn qualifications and experience into clear, evidence-based interview answers.\n22 EXPLORE Answer: Tell me about yourself\n23 BUILD Qualifications and experience\n24 PERFORM Strengths and development areas\n25 EXPLORE Build a STAR answer\n26 BUILD Present achievements with evidence\n27 PERFORM Discuss challenges and lessons\n28 EXPLORE Career goals and motivation\n29 BUILD Why should we hire you?\n30 PERFORM Ask intelligent interview questions\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":22,"title":"Answer: Tell me about yourself","content":"WEEK 8 | LESSON 22 | EXPLORE\nAnswer: Tell me about yourself\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Give a focused interview introduction connecting present work, relevant background and future \nvalue.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n136. What information is relevant to the job?\n137. How is this answer different from a personal biography?\n138. What value can you offer?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Tell me about yourself.\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n139. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n140. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n141. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCurrently, I... begin with present professional activity\nBefore that, I... add relevant background\nThrough that experience, I developed... name transferable skills\nWhat interests me about this opportunity is... connect to the role\nI believe I can contribute by... state potential value\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _________, I...\n2. _______ that, I...\n3. _______ that experience, I developed...\n4. _______ interests me about this opportunity is...\n5. I _______ I can contribute by...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present-past-future structure; present simple and past simple.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\n What interests me about this opportunity is the chance to take on greater responsibility.\n I believe I can contribute by combining analytical thinking with careful coordination.\nCurrently, I work as a general practitioner, where I assess patients, explain care and write clinical notes.\nBefore that, I completed medical study and developed experience in clinical care.\nThrough that experience, I developed patient communication, teamwork and problem-solving skills.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 8 | LESSON 22 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nAnswer: Tell me about yourself\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Begin strongly and avoid a rising, uncertain ending.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Act as interviewer. Ask the main question and two follow-ups about experience and value. B: Answer in 60-\n90 seconds using present, past and future.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver the answer twice: first with notes, then with five keywords only.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer is relevant, structured and connected to the opportunity.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite five keywords for your answer. Record a 90-second version without a full script.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":23,"title":"Qualifications and experience","content":"WEEK 8 | LESSON 23 | BUILD\nQualifications and experience\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Connect education, training and experience directly to a job requirement.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n142. Which qualification best matches your target role?\n143. What evidence proves you can use a skill?\n144. When should you use past simple?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: What relevant experience do you have?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: Where did you gain it?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: How does it prepare you for this role?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n145. How does the speaker begin?\n146. What detail develops the message?\n147. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI have experience in... summarise relevant experience\nI gained this experience while... locate where learning happened\nI have used... for... show duration or purpose\nThis prepared me to... connect experience to readiness\nA relevant example is... provide evidence\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I have __________ in...\n2. I _______ this experience while...\n3. I have _______... for...\n4. This ________ me to...\n5. A ________ example is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present perfect versus past simple; for and since.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nI have experience in general practice, patient communication and clinical documentation.\nI gained this experience through medical training and practical clinical work.\nThis prepared me to organise information carefully. A relevant example is a case where I coordinated care with a small team.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 8 | LESSON 23 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nQualifications and experience\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR \nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Read three fictional job requirements and ask for evidence for each. B: Match two real qualifications or \nexperiences to the requirements.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Answer: ‘What relevant experience do you have?’ with two pieces of evidence.\nSUCCESS CHECK I do more than list qualifications; I explain relevance and evidence.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nChoose a real job advertisement. Highlight three requirements and prepare one evidence sentence for each.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -\nPronounce qualifications, experience, practitioner and communication clearly.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":24,"title":"Strengths and development areas","content":"WEEK 8 | LESSON 24 | PERFORM\nStrengths and development areas\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe strengths with evidence and discuss improvement honestly and constructively.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n148. Which strengths matter for your target career?\n149. What evidence can you give?\n150. Which development area is honest but manageable?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nInterviewer: What is one of your strengths?\nCandidate:\nInterviewer: How do others see that strength?\nCandidate:\nInterviewer: What are you currently improving?\nCandidate: An area I’m developing is public speaking. I’m improving it by practising short presentations every week.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n151. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n152. Where does the speaker pause?\n153. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nOne of my strengths is... name a relevant strength\nFor example, I... prove the strength\nColleagues often rely on me to... show external evidence\nAn area I’m developing is... name a manageable improvement area\nI’m improving it by... show active development\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ of my strengths is...\n2. For _______, I...\n3. __________ often rely on me to...\n4. An _______ I’m developing is...\n5. I’m _________ it by...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Adjective-noun collocations; can and have improved.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -\nOne of my strengths is careful listening. For example, I ask clear follow-up questions.\nColleagues often rely on me to communicate clearly with patients.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 8 | LESSON 24 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nStrengths and development areas\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the strength and the evidence, not every word.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask for one strength, proof, one development area and current action. B: Give a balanced answer without \nusing clichés such as ‘I work too hard.’\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Answer two interview questions: one strength and one development area.\nSUCCESS CHECK Every claim includes evidence or a concrete improvement action.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a strength-evidence-action chart for three strengths and one development area.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":25,"title":"Build a STAR answer","content":"WEEK 9 | LESSON 25 | EXPLORE\nBuild a STAR answer\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Use Situation, Task, Action and Result to answer behavioural interview questions.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n154. What does each STAR letter mean?\n155. Which stage should usually be longest?\n156. Why should you say ‘I’ when describing your action?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a time you solved a problem.\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate: What I learned was to confirm critical information early.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n157. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n158. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n159. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThe situation was... set the context briefly\nMy responsibility was to... state the task\nThe action I took was... focus on your contribution\nAs a result, ... show the outcome\nWhat I learned was... add reflection when useful\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. The _________ was...\n2. My ______________ was to...\n3. The _______ I took was...\n4. As a _______, ...\n5. _______ I learned was...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple and past continuous; sequence and result connectors.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nThe situation was a patient record with inconsistent information.\nMy responsibility was to identify the correct details before the consultation.\nThe action I took was to compare the notes and ask the relevant colleague.\nAs a result, we corrected the record and continued with the consultation.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 9 | LESSON 25 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nBuild a STAR answer\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause clearly between the four STAR stages.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask one behavioural question and signal if any STAR stage is missing. B: Answer using four short sections; \nfocus most on Action.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute STAR answer about problem-solving or teamwork.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer contains all four stages and makes my personal action clear.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nComplete a STAR planner for a real experience. Record the answer using only four headings.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\n"},{"number":26,"title":"Present achievements with evidence","content":"WEEK 9 | LESSON 26 | BUILD\nPresent achievements with evidence\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe an achievement using specific actions, measurable results and relevant learning.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n160. Which achievement is relevant to your target role?\n161. What was your personal contribution?\n162. Can you express the result with a number or comparison?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: Which achievement are you most proud of?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: What was your contribution?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: What was the outcome?\nCandidate: The outcome was an early submission with fewer errors. This demonstrates my ability to coordinate work carefully.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n163. How does the speaker begin?\n164. What detail develops the message?\n165. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI’m particularly proud of... introduce a meaningful achievement\nMy specific contribution was... separate your role from the team’s\nWe improved... by... quantify positive change\nThe outcome was... state the final result\nThis demonstrates my ability to... connect achievement to a competency\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I’m ____________ proud of...\n2. My ________ contribution was...\n3. We ________... by...\n4. The _______ was...\n5. This ____________ my ability to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Result language, comparatives and quantifiers.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nI’m particularly proud of completing a demanding clinical rotation successfully.\nMy specific contribution was organising patient information and communicating with the team.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 9 | LESSON 26 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nPresent achievements with evidence\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Say numbers, dates and percentages slowly enough to be understood.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask for achievement, contribution, evidence and relevance. B: Present one achievement and answer follow￾up questions with specific details.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a 90-second achievement answer containing one measurable or observable result.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer proves impact instead of only saying the work was successful.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite three result statements using a number, time saved, quality improvement or completed output.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":27,"title":"Discuss challenges and lessons","content":"WEEK 9 | LESSON 27 | PERFORM\nDiscuss challenges and lessons\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Take responsibility, explain a constructive response and show learning.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n166. What type of challenge is safe and relevant to discuss?\n167. How do you show responsibility?\n168. What lasting change proves learning?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a mistake or difficult situation.\nCandidate: One difficulty I encountered was underestimating how long a task would take.\nCandidate: I realised that I had not allowed enough time for checking.\nCandidate: I took responsibility for informing the team early.\nCandidate\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n169. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n170. Where does the speaker pause?\n171. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nOne difficulty I encountered was... introduce a professional challenge\nI realised that... show awareness\nI took responsibility for... explain ownership\nTo address it, I... describe corrective action\nSince then, I have... show lasting improvement\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ difficulty I encountered was...\n2. I ________ that...\n3. I _______ responsibility for...\n4. To _______ it, I...\n5. _______ then, I have...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past forms; if + present for future response.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -\n To address it, I revised the work plan and asked for feedback.\nSince then, I have added review time before every important patient consultation.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 9 | LESSON 27 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDiscuss challenges and lessons\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use a calm, responsible tone and avoid defensive stress.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask about challenge, awareness, responsibility, action and lasting improvement. B: Discuss a genuine but \nmanageable challenge without blaming other people.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute lesson-learned answer using all five stages.\nSUCCESS CHECK I show honesty, action and improvement; the answer ends positively.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a before-and-after reflection: what I did before, what happened and what I do now.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":28,"title":"Career goals and motivation","content":"WEEK 10 | LESSON 28 | EXPLORE\nCareer goals and motivation\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain why a role fits your interests, skills and realistic future direction.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n172. What genuinely motivates you?\n173. Which part of the role fits your background?\n174. What future goal sounds realistic?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Why are you interested in this role?\nCandidate\nCandidate\nInterviewer: Where do you see yourself in a few years?\nCandidate\nCandidate: This opportunity would allow me to learn from an experienced international team.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n175. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n176. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n177. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI’m motivated by... explain a genuine source of energy\nThis role appeals to me because... connect motivation to the opportunity\nIn the next few years, I hope to... state a realistic direction\nI’d like to develop... identify a growth goal\nThis opportunity would allow me to... connect the role to progress\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I’m _________ by...\n2. This _______ appeals to me because...\n3. In the _______ few years, I hope to...\n4. I’d _______ to develop...\n5. This ___________ would allow me to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future forms: want to, hope to, plan to and would like to.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\n I’m motivated by roles that improve how organisations and public services work.\nThis role appeals to me because it combines patient care, communication and teamwork.\nI hope to take greater responsibility and develop stronger clinical and communication skills.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 10 | LESSON 28 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nCareer goals and motivation\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use contractions naturally but make key career words clear.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask why this role, why this organisation and what next. B: Connect personal motivation to actual job \nresponsibilities.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute motivation and career-goal answer.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer is specific to the role and does not sound memorised or exaggerated.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nResearch one organisation. Prepare three honest reasons you could be interested in its work.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":29,"title":"Why should we hire you?","content":"WEEK 10 | LESSON 29 | BUILD\nWhy should we hire you?\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Make a concise value argument using requirement, evidence and contribution.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n178. What are the employer’s top three needs?\n179. Which evidence supports each need?\n180. What value can you contribute immediately?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: Why should we hire you?\nCandidate:\nCandidate\nCandidate: I can bring a calm approach and strong attention to detail.\nCandidate\nCandidate: For these reasons, I believe I can contribute effectively from the beginning.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n181. How does the speaker begin?\n182. What detail develops the message?\n183. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThis role requires... identify the employer’s need\nMy experience has prepared me to... connect background to performance\nI can bring... state a useful contribution\nA strong example is... support the claim\nFor these reasons, I believe... close the argument\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. This _______ requires...\n2. My __________ has prepared me to...\n3. I _______ bring...\n4. A _______ example is...\n5. For _______ reasons, I believe...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Because, therefore and can to connect evidence to value.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nThis role requires careful listening, communication and reliable follow-up.\nMy experience has prepared me to assess information and communicate with different people.\nA strong example is a difficult consultation that I handled calmly and clearly.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 10 | LESSON 29 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nWhy should we hire you?\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the three key value words in your answer.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Read a fictional job description and challenge any unsupported claim. B: Give a 60-second value argument \nwith one strong example.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Answer ‘Why should we hire you?’ and respond to two evidence questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My claims match the role and are supported by believable evidence.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a three-column note: requirement, my evidence, my contribution. Record the final answer.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":30,"title":"Ask intelligent interview questions","content":"WEEK 10 | LESSON 30 | PERFORM\nAsk intelligent interview questions\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Ask relevant questions, respond to answers and close an interview professionally.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n184. Which questions show preparation?\n185. Which questions can be answered from the website?\n186. How many questions should you prepare?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nInterviewer: Do you have any questions for us?\nCandidate: Yes. What would success look like in the first three months?\nInterviewer\nCandidate: That’s helpful. Could you tell me more about how the team works together?\nInterviewer\nCandidate: Thank you. What are the next steps in the process?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n187. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n188. Where does the speaker pause?\n189. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCould you tell me more about...? request detail about the role\nHow would you describe...? ask about team or culture\nWhat would success look like in...? understand expectations\nWhat are the next steps in the process? ask about the recruitment timeline\nThank you for the opportunity to speak with you. close the interview\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. Could you _______ me more about...?\n2. _______ would you describe...?\n3. _______ would success look like in...?\n4. _______ are the next steps in the process?\n5. _______ you for the opportunity to speak with you.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect and open questions; would/could for polite enquiry.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -\n We would expect the person to understand the team and manage one small assignment.\n We have a weekly team meeting and daily online updates.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 10 | LESSON 30 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nAsk intelligent interview questions\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use falling intonation for open questions and clear closing statements.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Answer as an interviewer using details from a fictional organisation. B: Ask three prepared questions and \none natural follow-up.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete the final five minutes of a mock interview, including a professional closing.\nSUCCESS CHECK My questions are relevant, open and connected to the role.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nPrepare five questions for a real target role. Rank them from most to least useful.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nMODULE 4\nPRESENTATION & MASTERY\nPresent ideas, handle questions and demonstrate measurable progress.\n31 EXPLORE Open a presentation confidently\n32 BUILD Organise and connect ideas\n33 PERFORM Explain information and visuals\n34 EXPLORE Handle audience questions\n35 BUILD Final interview rehearsal\n36 PERFORM Final showcase and next plan\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":31,"title":"Open a presentation confidently","content":"WEEK 11 | LESSON 31 | EXPLORE\nOpen a presentation confidently\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Gain attention, state purpose and preview a short professional presentation.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n190. What does an audience need in the first 30 seconds?\n191. How many main points suit a five-minute talk?\n192. What makes an opening sound confident?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nPresenter: Good morning, and thank you for joining.\nPresenter\nPresenter:\nPresenter: I’ll cover three main points: the current issue, two options and my recommendation.\nPresenter: The talk will take about five minutes.\nPresenter: Let’s begin with the current issue.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n193. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n194. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n195. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nGood morning, and thank you for joining. open formally and warmly\nToday I’d like to talk about... state the topic\nThe purpose of this presentation is... state the objective\nI’ll cover three main points. preview the structure\nLet’s begin with... move into the first point\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ morning, and thank you for joining.\n2. _______ I’d like to talk about...\n3. The _______ of this presentation is...\n4. I’ll _______ three main points.\n5. _______’s begin with...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple for purpose and overview; will for preview.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nToday I’d like to talk about improving communication during patient handovers.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 11 | LESSON 31 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nOpen a presentation confidently\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause after the greeting, title and roadmap.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Listen with your notes closed. After the opening, state the topic, purpose and roadmap you heard. B: Deliver\na 30-second opening and adjust it after feedback.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Open a five-minute professional presentation without reading full sentences.\nSUCCESS CHECK My audience knows the topic, purpose, structure and approximate length.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord three versions of the same 30-second opening. Choose the clearest one.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":32,"title":"Organise and connect ideas","content":"WEEK 11 | LESSON 32 | BUILD\nOrganise and connect ideas\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Build a clear beginning, middle and end using signposting language.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n196. What is the difference between a point and an example?\n197. Which signpost marks contrast?\n198. How should a conclusion connect to the purpose?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nPresenter:\nPresenter:\nPresenter:\nPresenter:\nPresenter: The key message here is that format affects how quickly people can act.\nPresenter:\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n199. How does the speaker begin?\n200. What detail develops the message?\n201. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nFirst, let’s look at... open the first section\nThis brings me to my second point. signal a transition\nIn contrast... show an important difference\nThe key message here is... highlight meaning\nTo sum up... begin the conclusion\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______, let’s look at...\n2. This _______ me to my second point.\n3. In ________...\n4. The _______ message here is...\n5. To _______ up...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Sequence and contrast linkers; topic sentences.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\nFirst, let’s look at the current handover problem.\nHandover notes are detailed, but key information can be hard to find quickly.\nThis brings me to my second point: a shorter handover summary.\nIn contrast, a handover summary shows the key information on one page.\nTo sum up, I recommend short handover summaries with detailed notes when needed.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 11 | LESSON 32 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nOrganise and connect ideas\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use pauses to mark transitions between sections.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Use five cards: opening, point 1, point 2, point 3 and conclusion. Mix them before your partner speaks. B: \nOrganise the cards and deliver a two-minute mini-presentation.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Present three connected points and a conclusion in three minutes.\nSUCCESS CHECK My transitions are audible and my conclusion returns to the main purpose.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a one-page presentation map with keywords only. Practise without full sentences.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":33,"title":"Explain information and visuals","content":"WEEK 11 | LESSON 33 | PERFORM\nExplain information and visuals\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe a simple chart, plan or process and state what the audience should notice.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n202. What should you say before reading numbers?\n203. How is description different from interpretation?\n204. Which one trend matters most?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nPresenter\nPresenter: The most noticeable change is the increase in Week 3.\nPresenter\nAudience: What was the exact number?\nPresenter\nPresenter\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n205. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n206. Where does the speaker pause?\n207. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThis visual shows... identify what the audience is seeing\nThe most noticeable change is... highlight the main pattern\nCompared with..., ... make a comparison\nApproximately... give a rounded figure\nWhat this means is... interpret the information\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. This _______ shows...\n2. The _______ noticeable change is...\n3. ________ with..., ...\n4. _____________...\n5. _______ this means is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Comparatives, quantities and approximation language.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -\nThis visual shows the number of patients seen over four weeks.\nCompared with Week 1, the clinic saw almost twice as many patients.\nApproximately forty patients were seen in Week 3.\nWhat this means is that the new appointment system may be improving efficiency.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 11 | LESSON 33 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nExplain information and visuals\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Say numbers in groups and pause before the key interpretation.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Sketch a simple four-week chart with invented, safe data and ask for a description. B: Describe the visual, \ncompare two points and interpret the main message.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Explain one chart, diagram or process for two minutes, then answer a question.\nSUCCESS CHECK I identify the visual, select the main pattern and explain why it matters.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nFind or draw a simple professional visual. Record a 90-second explanation using four phrases.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":34,"title":"Handle audience questions","content":"WEEK 12 | LESSON 34 | EXPLORE\nHandle audience questions\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Listen fully, acknowledge the question and give a clear, bounded answer.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n208. What should you do before answering?\n209. How can you respond when you do not know?\n210. When should you promise a follow-up?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nAudience:\nPresenter: Thank you for that question. If I understand you correctly, you’re asking about weekly time savings.\nAudience: Yes, exactly.\nPresenter: The short answer is about two hours per team. I don’t have the final figure with me, but I can confirm it after the \nmeeting.\nAudience: That would be useful.\nPresenter: Of course. Does that answer your question for now?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n211. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n212. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n213. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThank you for that question. acknowledge the audience\nIf I understand you correctly, ... confirm a complex question\nThe short answer is... begin concisely\nI don’t have that figure with me, but... respond honestly when information is missing\nDoes that answer your question? check satisfaction\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ you for that question.\n2. If I __________ you correctly, ...\n3. The _______ answer is...\n4. I _______’t have that figure with me, but...\n5. Does that _______ your question?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect questions, conditionals and repair language.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition -\nHow much time would the new handover process save each week?\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 12 | LESSON 34 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nHandle audience questions\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Repeat the key word from the question before answering.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask one clear, one unclear and one unanswerable question about the presentation. B: Acknowledge, clarify \nif needed, answer honestly and check understanding.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Handle five audience questions without becoming defensive or inventing information.\nSUCCESS CHECK I answer the real question, stay concise and clearly mark any follow-up needed.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nPrepare five likely questions for your final presentation and outline a two-sentence answer for each.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":35,"title":"Final interview rehearsal","content":"WEEK 12 | LESSON 35 | BUILD\nFinal interview rehearsal\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Integrate structure, evidence, interaction and fluency in a complete mock interview.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n214. Which interview answer still needs the most practice?\n215. Which filler helps you organise without delay?\n216. What evidence do you want the interviewer to remember?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: What makes you a strong candidate for this role?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: Can you give an example?\nCandidate\nCandidate: The result of that action was an earlier, more accurate submission.\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n217. How does the speaker begin?\n218. What detail develops the message?\n219. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThat’s an important question. gain a brief moment to organise\nA relevant example would be... move from claim to evidence\nThe result of that action was... complete an evidence story\nTo connect that to this role... make relevance explicit\nIs there any part you’d like me to expand on? invite a follow-up\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. That’s an _________ question.\n2. A ________ example would be...\n3. The _______ of that action was...\n4. To _______ that to this role...\n5. Is _______ any part you’d like me to expand on?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Mixed review based on personal error patterns.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition -\n To connect that to this role, I can bring the same structured approach to your team.\nThat’s an important question. I believe my clinical experience and communication skills are especially relevant.\nA relevant example would be a difficult consultation where I organised information and explained the plan clearly.\nMuna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 12 | LESSON 35 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nFinal interview rehearsal\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use clear sentence stress, natural pace and decisive endings.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Conduct an eight-question interview. Note one strength and two corrections; do not interrupt answers. B: \nAnswer naturally, ask for clarification when needed and ask two questions at the end.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete a 15-minute mock interview with delayed feedback and a second attempt at two answers.\nSUCCESS CHECK I answer relevantly, support claims, interact naturally and improve after feedback.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nReview the recording. Write two exact sentences to keep and two to improve before the final assessment.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n"},{"number":36,"title":"Final showcase and next plan","content":"WEEK 12 | LESSON 36 | PERFORM\nFinal showcase and next plan\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Demonstrate progress and create a practical 30-day continuation plan.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n220. Which recording best shows your progress?\n221. Which strategy will you keep using?\n222. What is one realistic 30-day goal?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: How has your speaking changed?\nStudent: At the beginning of this course, I often stopped when I made a mistake.\nTutor: What is different now?\nStudent: The biggest improvement I’ve noticed is that I can organise my ideas and keep speaking.\nTutor: What will you work on next?\nStudent: My next target is presentation fluency. I’ll continue by recording one short talk every week.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n223. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n224. Where does the speaker pause?\n225. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nAt the beginning of this course... describe the starting point\nThe biggest improvement I’ve noticed is... identify progress\nI can now... state a new ability\nMy next target is... choose a focused goal\nI’ll continue by... commit to a practical action\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. At the _________ of this course...\n2. The _______ improvement I’ve noticed is...\n3. I _______ now...\n4. My _______ target is...\n5. I’ll ________ by...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future intentions and review of personal high-frequency errors.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nWEEK 12 | LESSON 36 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nFinal showcase and next plan\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Compare the baseline and final recordings for pace, pausing and clarity.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Listen to the final showcase and ask questions about progress, evidence and next steps. B: Deliver a five￾minute presentation, answer questions and reflect honestly.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete the final portfolio: conversation, interview, presentation and 30-day learning plan.\nSUCCESS CHECK I demonstrate progress with evidence and leave with one specific, scheduled next action.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nComplete the 30-day plan at the back of this book and schedule the first three practice sessions.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nFinal performance portfolio\nComplete these tasks during Week 12. Use keywords, not full scripts.\nCONVERSATION Maintain a six-minute conversation. Open naturally, ask follow-ups, clarify once and close professionally.\nINTERVIEW Complete a 15-minute interview. Include a structured introduction, two STAR answers and two questions for \nthe interviewer.\nPRESENTATION Deliver a five-minute professional presentation with three points, one simple visual and audience \nquestions.\nWRITING Write a 120-150-word professional follow-up email with a clear purpose, action and deadline.\nSpeaking performance rubric\nAREA 3 - INDEPENDENT 2 - DEVELOPING 1 - NEEDS SUPPORT\nTask Completes the purpose clearly Completes most of the purpose Needs prompts to complete it\nFluency Keeps speaking with manageable \npauses\nSome long pauses, but the message \ncontinues\nFrequent pauses stop the message\nInteraction Responds, follows up and clarifies Responds but rarely extends Short answers; needs repeated \nsupport\nLanguage Useful range; errors rarely block \nmeaning\nSimple range; some errors affect \nclarity\nLimited range; errors often block \nmeaning\nPronunciation Generally clear with useful stress Usually understandable with some \nstrain\nOften difficult to understand\nPortfolio reflection\nThe clearest evidence of my progress is...\nThe communication habit I will continue is...\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nRetrieval challenge answer key 1/2\nCheck only after you have attempted the activity from memory.\nL. LESSON MISSING WORDS 1-5\n1 Your starting point Let | What | repeat | experience | One\n2 A strong professional introduction currently | background | responsible | particularly | current\n3 Keep speaking without long pauses There | first | Another | example | Let\n4 Start conversations naturally How | What | met | How | sounds\n5 Show interest and ask follow-ups Really | How | What | mean | can\n6 End conversations professionally was | should | Let | send | look\n7 Describe your work clearly main | work | typical | purpose | simple\n8 Routines and current priorities usually | moment | Most | week | top\n9 Give a useful work update far | currently | main | expect | next\n10 Tell a past experience few | had | role | most | experience\n11 Sequence a professional story first | While | After | result | end\n12 Describe success and challenge main | Although | decided | What | did\n13 Meet international contacts Where | What | How | seem | useful\n14 Clarify and confirm meaning clarify | understand | mean | Let | give\n15 Phone and video calls Can | breaking | say | Let | summarise\n16 Describe a workplace problem noticed | problem | affecting | most | What\n17 Suggest and compare solutions One | Another | advantage | main | recommend\n18 Explain a process step by step process | Once | next | Before | purpose\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nRetrieval challenge answer key 2/2\nCheck only after you have attempted the activity from memory.\nL. LESSON MISSING WORDS 1-5\n19 Share opinions in meetings From | main | instance | how | What\n20 Agree and disagree politely agree | good | see | not | consider\n21 Messages and follow-up emails writing | confirm | discussed | let | Thank\n22 Answer: Tell me about yourself Currently | Before | Through | What | believe\n23 Qualifications and experience experience | gained | used | prepared | relevant\n24 Strengths and development areas One | example | Colleagues | area | improving\n25 Build a STAR answer situation | responsibility | action | result | What\n26 Present achievements with evidence particularly | specific | improved | outcome | demonstrates\n27 Discuss challenges and lessons One | realised | took | address | Since\n28 Career goals and motivation motivated | role | next | like | opportunity\n29 Why should we hire you? role | experience | can | strong | these\n30 Ask intelligent interview questions tell | How | What | What | Thank\n31 Open a presentation confidently Good | Today | purpose | cover | Let\n32 Organise and connect ideas First | brings | contrast | key | sum\n33 Explain information and visuals visual | most | Compared | Approximately | What\n34 Handle audience questions Thank | understand | short | don | answer\n35 Final interview rehearsal important | relevant | result | connect | there\n36 Final showcase and next plan beginning | biggest | can | next | continue\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nCareer expression record\nRecord complete expressions you can use, not isolated words. Revisit each expression after 2 days, 7 days and 21 days.\nEXPRESSION MY TRUE EXAMPLE D2 D7 D21\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\nMy five rescue phrases\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif\n\n\n\nMy 30-day continuation plan\nONE CLEAR TARGET In 30 days, I will be able to ________________________________________________.\nWeekly rhythm\nWEEK SPEAKING TASK CAREER SITUATION EVIDENCE TO SAVE DATE\n1\n2\n3\n4\nPractice menu\nOne two-minute voice note using three course expressions.\nOne six-minute conversation with follow-up questions.\nOne work update, interview answer or presentation section.\nOne listen-and-shadow practice using a clear professional model.\nOne feedback-and-retry session focused on a single correction.\nProgress review\nThe evidence I will compare with my Week 1 baseline is...\nIf I miss a practice day, I will restart by...\nPersonal edition -Muna Mohamoud Khalif"}]},"career-fluency-abdishakur":{"title":"Career Fluency — Abdishakur Edition","lessons":[{"number":1,"title":"Your starting point","content":"WEEK 1 | LESSON 1 | EXPLORE\nYour starting point\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Complete a friendly speaking diagnostic and identify one clear fluency goal.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n10.Where did you last use English?\n11.What can you already do well?\n12.What speaking situation feels difficult?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nTutor: Tell me a little about yourself and how you use English.\nStudent: In my experience, I use English at work, online and when I travel.\nTutor: What would you most like to improve?\nStudent: Let me think for a moment. One goal I have is to speak with fewer pauses.\nTutor: When do you feel most confident?\nStudent: I feel confident when I read or listen, but spontaneous speaking is harder.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n13.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n14.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n15.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nLet me think for a moment. need time without becoming silent\nWhat I mean is... restate an idea more clearly\nCould you repeat the question? need to hear a question again\nIn my experience... connect an answer to real life\nOne goal I have is... state a learning or career goal\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ me think for a moment.\n2. _______ I mean is...\n3. Could you _______ the question?\n4. In my __________...\n5. _______ goal I have is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Use complete simple sentences; accuracy is observed but not taught today.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 1 | LESSON 1 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nYour starting point\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Speak at a natural volume and finish each thought clearly.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask three diagnostic questions about work, daily life and future goals. Take brief notes. B: Answer each \nquestion for 30-60 seconds. Ask for repetition when needed.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes about who you are, how you use English and what you want to achieve in twelve \nweeks.\nSUCCESS CHECK I complete the task, use at least two rescue phrases and choose one measurable goal.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord a two-minute baseline voice note. Do not restart it. Write three things you want to improve.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":2,"title":"A strong professional introduction","content":"WEEK 1 | LESSON 2 | BUILD\nA strong professional introduction\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Introduce yourself with a clear beginning, useful details and a forward-looking ending.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n16.Which three facts belong in a professional introduction?\n17.What detail should you leave out?\n18.How can you end confidently?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nAmina: It’s good to meet you. What do you do?\nHassan\nAmina: What are you responsible for?\nHassan\nAmina: What would you like to do next?\nHassan\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n19.How does the speaker begin?\n20.What detail develops the message?\n21.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI currently work as... state your present role\nMy background is in... describe education or professional field\nI’m responsible for... explain a main duty\nI’m particularly interested in... name a professional interest\nMy current goal is to... finish with a future direction\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I _________ work as...\n2. My __________ is in...\n3. I’m ___________ for...\n4. I’m ____________ interested in...\n5. My _______ goal is to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple with be, have and work; because for reasons.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n I currently work as a management consultant. My background is in public-sector management.\n I’m responsible for reviewing information and preparing management reports.\n My current goal is to take on greater responsibility and communicate confidently in professional settings.\n\n\n\nWEEK 1 | LESSON 2 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nA strong professional introduction\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the key words: name, role, field, skills and goal.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: You are meeting a new colleague at a regional conference. Ask about role, background and goals. B: Give a \n60-second professional introduction, then ask your partner one question.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a 90-second introduction without reading full sentences.\nSUCCESS CHECK My introduction includes role, background, responsibility, interest and goal.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord the introduction twice. Compare the recordings and note one improvement in fluency.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":3,"title":"Keep speaking without long pauses","content":"WEEK 1 | LESSON 3 | PERFORM\nKeep speaking without long pauses\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Use thinking phrases, connectors and repair strategies to maintain your turn.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n22.What topic can you discuss easily for one minute?\n23.Which connector do you use most?\n24.What do you say when a sentence goes wrong?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: Why is English important for your career?\nStudent: There are two main reasons. The first point is communication at work.\nTutor: Can you give me an example?\nStudent: For example, I sometimes need to speak with people from different countries.\nTutor: And the second reason?\nStudent: Another thing is career growth. Let me say that another way: English can open more professional opportunities.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n25.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n26.Where does the speaker pause?\n27.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThere are two main reasons. organise an answer before giving details\nThe first point is... start your first idea\nAnother thing is... add a second idea\nFor example... support an idea with evidence\nLet me say that another way. repair an unclear sentence\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ are two main reasons.\n2. The _______ point is...\n3. _______ thing is...\n4. For _______...\n5. _______ me say that another way.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Join ideas with and, but, because and so.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 1 | LESSON 3 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nKeep speaking without long pauses\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause between idea groups, not after every word.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask ‘why’ and ‘how’ questions. Wait silently while your partner uses a thinking phrase. B: Answer each \nquestion with two points and one example.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes: ‘Why I am learning English now.’ Repeat the same talk with fewer pauses.\nSUCCESS CHECK I use two organising phrases, one example and one repair phrase instead of stopping.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nComplete three one-minute speaking sprints: work, technology and travel. Save the final recording.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":4,"title":"Start conversations naturally","content":"WEEK 2 | LESSON 4 | EXPLORE\nStart conversations naturally\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Open a conversation in social and professional situations.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n28.Where do you usually meet new people?\n29.Which opening question feels natural to you?\n30.What makes an opening question too personal?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nSalma: Hi, have we met before?\nOmar: I don’t think so. I’m Omar from the planning team.\nSalma: Nice to meet you. What brings you here?\nOmar: I’m attending the digital skills workshop.\nSalma: That sounds interesting. How do you know the organiser?\nOmar: We worked together on a community project last year.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n31.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n32.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n33.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nHow has your day been so far? open a relaxed conversation\nWhat brings you here? ask about purpose at an event\nHave we met before? check whether you know someone\nHow do you know the team? find a shared connection\nThat sounds interesting. respond positively before another question\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ has your day been so far?\n2. _______ brings you here?\n3. Have we _______ before?\n4. _______ do you know the team?\n5. That _______ interesting.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Wh- questions and present simple question order.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 2 | LESSON 4 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nStart conversations naturally\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use rising intonation for friendly opening questions.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Choose a situation: workshop, airport, online meeting or office reception. Start the conversation. B: \nRespond, add one detail and ask a question back.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Start three different two-minute conversations without using the same opening twice.\nSUCCESS CHECK I greet, open, respond to the answer and move to a relevant follow-up question.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite five safe opening questions. Use two of them in a real or simulated conversation.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":5,"title":"Show interest and ask follow-ups","content":"WEEK 2 | LESSON 5 | BUILD\nShow interest and ask follow-ups\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Keep a conversation going by responding, echoing and asking connected questions.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n34.How do you show interest in Somali?\n35.Which English reactions sound natural?\n36.Which question can follow almost any story?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nAli: I recently started learning mapping software.\nNimo: Really? How did you get interested in that?\nAli: A colleague showed me how it can improve planning.\nNimo: What was that like at the beginning?\nAli: It was challenging, but I enjoyed it.\nNimo: I can relate to that. New software often feels difficult before it becomes useful.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n37.How does the speaker begin?\n38.What detail develops the message?\n39.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nReally? What happened next? invite someone to continue a story\nHow did you get interested in that? ask about the origin of an interest\nWhat was that like? ask about an experience\nSo, you mean that...? check your understanding\nI can relate to that. connect the speaker’s experience to yours\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______? What happened next?\n2. _______ did you get interested in that?\n3. _______ was that like?\n4. So, you _______ that...?\n5. I _______ relate to that.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Follow-up questions with how, why, when and what happened next.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 2 | LESSON 5 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nShow interest and ask follow-ups\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Let your voice rise slightly to show real interest.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Tell a short story about learning a skill. Pause after each important detail. B: React first, then ask a \nconnected follow-up. Do not change the topic suddenly.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Keep one conversation going for six exchanges using at least three follow-up expressions.\nSUCCESS CHECK Every question connects to my partner’s previous answer.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nListen to a short interview or conversation. Write down three follow-up questions you hear or could ask.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":6,"title":"End conversations professionally","content":"WEEK 2 | LESSON 6 | PERFORM\nEnd conversations professionally\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Close conversations warmly, confirm next steps and leave a positive final impression.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n40.Why can ending a conversation feel difficult?\n41.What next step might you confirm?\n42.Which closing is formal enough for work?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nMaryan: I should let you get back to the workshop.\nDavid: Of course. It was great speaking with you.\nMaryan: Let’s stay in touch. I’ll send you the project link this afternoon.\nDavid: Thank you. I’ll review it tomorrow.\nMaryan: Excellent. I look forward to speaking again.\nDavid: Likewise. Enjoy the rest of the event.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n43.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n44.Where does the speaker pause?\n45.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nIt was great speaking with you. signal a warm ending\nI should let you get back to... respect the other person’s time\nLet’s stay in touch. suggest future contact\nI’ll send you the details. promise a next action\nI look forward to speaking again. close a professional interaction\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. It _______ great speaking with you.\n2. I _______ let you get back to...\n3. _______’s stay in touch.\n4. I’ll _______ you the details.\n5. I _______ forward to speaking again.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future arrangements with will and present continuous.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 2 | LESSON 6 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nEnd conversations professionally\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use a calm falling tone for a clear, polite ending.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Continue a conversation for two minutes. Then give a time signal so your partner can close it. B: Notice the \nsignal, close politely and confirm one next action.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Open, maintain and close a complete four-minute conversation.\nSUCCESS CHECK My closing is polite, natural and includes a clear next step when appropriate.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nSend a 45-second voice message that ends a professional conversation and confirms a follow-up.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":7,"title":"Describe your work clearly","content":"WEEK 3 | LESSON 7 | EXPLORE\nDescribe your work clearly\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain your role, team and responsibilities to someone outside your field.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n46.How would you explain your job to a 12-year-old?\n47.Who depends on your work?\n48.What result does your role produce?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nGuest: What exactly do you do?\nGuest: What does a typical task involve?\nGuest: Who do you work with?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n49.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n50.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n51.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nMy main responsibility is... name your most important duty\nI work closely with... describe collaboration\nA typical task involves... explain a regular activity\nThe purpose of my role is to... explain why your job exists\nIn simple terms... make technical information accessible\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. My _______ responsibility is...\n2. I _______ closely with...\n3. A _______ task involves...\n4. The _______ of my role is to...\n5. In _______ terms...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple for regular duties; noun phrases for roles and departments.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\nAdvisor:\nAdvisor:\nAdvisor:\n In simple terms, I help managers analyse information and improve decisions.\n A typical task involves reviewing reports, identifying issues and preparing recommendations.\n I work closely with managers, finance staff and other departments.\n\n\n\nWEEK 3 | LESSON 7 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDescribe your work clearly\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR \nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: You know nothing about your partner’s job. Ask for simple explanations and examples. B: Explain your role\nwithout unexplained technical words.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute ‘My work in simple terms’ explanation.\nSUCCESS CHECK A listener outside my field can understand my role, main duty, collaborators and result.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a personal job vocabulary map with ten words. Record five sentences using the most useful words.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\nMark word stress in responsibility, department, financial and coordinate.\n\n"},{"number":8,"title":"Routines and current priorities","content":"WEEK 3 | LESSON 8 | BUILD\nRoutines and current priorities\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Compare regular responsibilities with what you are working on now.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n52.Which duties happen every week?\n53.What is different this week?\n54.How do you decide your top priority?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nTutor: What does a normal week look like for you?\nStudent: Most weeks, I review plans and coordinate with colleagues.\nTutor: What are you working on at the moment?\nStudent\nTutor: Is this week typical?\nStudent: This week is different because we have an important deadline on Thursday.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n55.How does the speaker begin?\n56.What detail develops the message?\n57.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI usually spend my mornings... describe a normal routine\nAt the moment, I’m working on...\nMost weeks, I... describe a frequent pattern\nThis week is different because... contrast routine and present\nMy top priority today is... identify the most urgent task\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I _______ spend my mornings...\n2. At the _______, I’m working on...\n3. _______ weeks, I...\n4. This _______ is different because...\n5. My _______ priority today is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple versus present continuous; adverbs of frequency.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n At the moment, I’m preparing a management report for my department.\nname a current assignment\n\n\n\nWEEK 3 | LESSON 8 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nRoutines and current priorities\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pronounce final -s clearly in works, checks, plans and manages.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask about normal routines, current work and today’s priority. B: Contrast at least two routine duties with \nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes about ‘My normal work and my current priority.’\nSUCCESS CHECK I use present simple for routine and present continuous for current action.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nKeep a one-day English work log. Write five routine statements and three current- action statements.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\none current assignment.\n\n"},{"number":9,"title":"Give a useful work update","content":"WEEK 3 | LESSON 9 | PERFORM\nGive a useful work update\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Report progress, completed actions, current work and next steps.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n58.What makes an update useful?\n59.Which detail should come first?\n60.How can you report a problem without sounding negative?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nManager: Can you give me a quick update?\nEmployee: So far, I’ve completed the first review. I’m currently checking the final details.\nManager: Is anything blocking the work?\nEmployee: The main issue is one missing document, but I expect to receive it today.\nManager: What happens next?\nEmployee: My next step is to update the plan and send it by 3 p.m.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n61.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n62.Where does the speaker pause?\n63.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nSo far, I’ve completed... report completed progress\nI’m currently checking... describe work in progress\nThe main issue is... identify a blocker\nI expect to finish by... give a realistic deadline\nMy next step is to... state the immediate action\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. So _______, I’ve completed...\n2. I’m _________ checking...\n3. The _______ issue is...\n4. I _______ to finish by...\n5. My _______ step is to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present perfect for progress; present continuous for current work; will for next action.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 3 | LESSON 9 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nGive a useful work update\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Link contractions smoothly: I’ve completed, we’re checking, I’ll send.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: You are a manager. Ask about progress, current work, blockers, deadline and next step. B: Give a concise \n60-second update using the five-part structure.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a work update in one minute, then answer two follow-up questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My listener knows what is done, happening now, blocked, due and next.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord two updates: a 30-second version and a 60-second version. Keep the clearest one.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nMODULE 2\nWORKPLACE & INTERNATIONAL ENGLISH\nUse English for experiences, international communication, problems, meetings and professional\nmessages.\n10 EXPLORE Tell a past experience\n11 BUILD Sequence a professional story\n12 PERFORM Describe success and challenge\n13 EXPLORE Meet international contacts\n14 BUILD Clarify and confirm meaning\n15 PERFORM Phone and video calls\n16 EXPLORE Describe a workplace problem\n17 BUILD Suggest and compare solutions\n18 PERFORM Explain a process step by step\n19 EXPLORE Share opinions in meetings\n20 BUILD Agree and disagree politely\n21 PERFORM Messages and follow-up emails\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":10,"title":"Tell a past experience","content":"WEEK 4 | LESSON 10 | EXPLORE\nTell a past experience\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe a past professional or personal experience with clear time references.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n64.Which recent experience helped you grow?\n65.What exactly did you do?\n66.What did the experience teach you?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a useful learning experience.\nCandidate\nInterviewer: What was your role?\nCandidate: My role was to complete the practical tasks and support another participant.\nInterviewer: What did you learn?\nCandidate: The most useful part was solving a real problem. That experience taught me to ask clearer questions.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n67.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n68.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n69.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nA few months ago... place an experience in time\nI had the opportunity to... introduce a valuable experience\nMy role was to... state your responsibility then\nThe most useful part was... highlight value\nThat experience taught me... state a lesson\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. A _______ months ago...\n2. I _______ the opportunity to...\n3. My _______ was to...\n4. The _______ useful part was...\n5. That __________ taught me...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple: regular and common irregular verbs.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n A few months ago, I had the opportunity to join a short management workshop.\n\n\n\nWEEK 4 | LESSON 10 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nTell a past experience\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Practise -ed endings in worked, planned, needed and completed.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask for the time, situation, role, action and lesson. B: Tell one past experience in chronological order.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Tell a two-minute story about learning, work, travel or teamwork.\nSUCCESS CHECK My listener can identify when it happened, what I did and what I learned.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite six past-tense verbs from your story. Record the story again with clear endings.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":11,"title":"Sequence a professional story","content":"WEEK 4 | LESSON 11 | BUILD\nSequence a professional story\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Organise events so a listener can follow what happened from beginning to end.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n70.What happens when a story has no sequence markers?\n71.Which action was happening in the background?\n72.What result ended your story?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nColleague: How did you solve the scheduling problem?\nEmployee: At first, we did not know which information was correct.\nColleague: What did you do?\nEmployee\nColleague: Did that work?\nEmployee\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n73.How does the speaker begin?\n74.What detail develops the message?\n75.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nAt first... describe the starting situation\nWhile I was..., ... show two actions happening together\nAfter that... move to the next event\nAs a result... connect an action to its effect\nIn the end... give the final outcome\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. At _______...\n2. _______ I was..., ...\n3. _______ that...\n4. As a _______...\n5. In the _______...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple and past continuous; sequence markers.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n While I was checking the figures, my colleague contacted the finance team. After that, we compared both records.\n Yes. As a result, we corrected the report. In the end, the team submitted it on time.\n\n\n\nWEEK 4 | LESSON 11 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nSequence a professional story\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause briefly after first, while, after that and finally.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Listen and draw a four-step timeline. Ask questions if an event is unclear. B: Tell a story with a clear \nbeginning, middle, result and ending.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Retell one professional story in three minutes using all five sequence expressions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My story is easy to follow and each event leads naturally to the next.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a five-box timeline for your story. Practise speaking from the timeline, not a full script.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":12,"title":"Describe success and challenge","content":"WEEK 4 | LESSON 12 | PERFORM\nDescribe success and challenge\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain what went well, what was difficult and how you responded.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n76.What challenge can you discuss professionally?\n77.What action did you take?\n78.What would you improve next time?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: Tell me about a challenging task.\nStudent: The main challenge was a very short deadline.\nTutor: How did you respond?\nStudent: Although it was difficult, we divided the work clearly. We decided to check progress every afternoon.\nTutor: What worked well?\nStudent: What worked well was the communication. If I did it again, I would confirm responsibilities earlier.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n79.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n80.Where does the speaker pause?\n81.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThe main challenge was... identify the difficult part\nAlthough it was difficult, ... show a positive contrast\nWe decided to... describe a chosen response\nWhat worked well was... identify a success factor\nIf I did it again, I would... reflect on improvement\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. The _______ challenge was...\n2. ________ it was difficult, ...\n3. We _______ to...\n4. _______ worked well was...\n5. If I _______ it again, I would...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION because, so, although and however for cause and contrast.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 4 | LESSON 12 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDescribe success and challenge\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress contrast words to make the message clear.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask neutral questions about challenge, response, result and reflection. B: Describe a challenge without \nblaming another person.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a balanced two-minute account of a challenge and successful response.\nSUCCESS CHECK I explain the problem, action, positive result and lesson in a professional tone.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a six-sentence reflection using because, so, although and however.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\n"},{"number":13,"title":"Meet international contacts","content":"WEEK 5 | LESSON 13 | EXPLORE\nMeet international contacts\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Introduce yourself, find common ground and adapt your language for a new international contact.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n82.What information is safe to share in a first meeting?\n83.How can you find common ground?\n84.How do you show respect for a different perspective?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nLayla: Hello, where are you joining from?\nDaniel: I’m joining from Nairobi. What kind of work do you do?\nLayla\nDaniel\nLayla: We seem to have that in common. It’s useful to hear your perspective.\nDaniel\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n85.What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n86.Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n87.Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nWhere are you joining from? open an online international meeting\nWhat kind of work do you do? ask about a professional field\nHow long have you worked in that area? ask about experience\nWe seem to have that in common. identify shared experience\nIt’s useful to hear your perspective. value another viewpoint\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ are you joining from?\n2. _______ kind of work do you do?\n3. _______ long have you worked in that area?\n4. We _______ to have that in common.\n5. It’s _______ to hear your perspective.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple and present perfect for background and experience.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n I work in management consulting. I’ve worked in that area for three years.\n Interesting. I also support public-sector programmes.\n Likewise. I’d like to learn more about your current assignment.\n\n\n\nWEEK 5 | LESSON 13 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nMeet international contacts\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Say names, countries and organisations slowly and clearly.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Choose a fictional country and professional role. Ask about location, work and experience. B: Introduce \nyour real background and identify one shared interest.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete a five-minute international networking conversation.\nSUCCESS CHECK I speak clearly, avoid local-only references and ask respectful follow-up questions.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nPrepare a 60-second introduction for an international online meeting. Record it with clear names and places.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":14,"title":"Clarify and confirm meaning","content":"WEEK 5 | LESSON 14 | BUILD\nClarify and confirm meaning\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Prevent misunderstandings by checking, paraphrasing and confirming important information.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n88.Which work details must always be confirmed?\n89.What is the difference between clarifying and confirming?\n90.How can you ask without sounding rude?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nClient\nEmployee: Could you clarify what you mean by early next week?\nClient: Tuesday morning, before ten.\nEmployee: If I understand correctly, you need the final version by 10 a.m. on Tuesday.\nClient: That’s right.\nEmployee: Let me confirm the details in an email after this call.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n91.How does the speaker begin?\n92.What detail develops the message?\n93.How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCould you clarify what you mean by...? ask for a clearer explanation\nIf I understand correctly, ... paraphrase the message\nDo you mean...? check one interpretation\nLet me confirm the details. review important information\nCould you give me an example? request concrete evidence\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. Could you _______ what you mean by...?\n2. If I __________ correctly, ...\n3. Do you _______...?\n4. _______ me confirm the details.\n5. Could you _______ me an example?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect questions and checking structures.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n We need the revised report early next week.\n\n\n\nWEEK 5 | LESSON 14 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nClarify and confirm meaning\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the word that needs correction or confirmation.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Give instructions containing one vague time, term or quantity. B: Ask two clarification questions, \nparaphrase and confirm the final details.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Resolve three misunderstandings in a role-play without switching languages.\nSUCCESS CHECK I clarify politely and repeat the final meaning accurately.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite five clarification questions connected to your work. Use two in a recorded role-play.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":15,"title":"Phone and video calls","content":"WEEK 5 | LESSON 15 | PERFORM\nPhone and video calls\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Open, manage and close remote conversations when sound or connection is imperfect.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n94.What call problems happen most often?\n95.How do you check a number accurately?\n96.What should happen before a call ends?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nHost: Good morning. Can you hear me clearly?\nGuest: Mostly, but you’re breaking up a little.\nHost: Thanks for telling me. Is this better?\nGuest\nHost: Certainly: B-4-7-2. Let me share my screen as well.\nGuest: Perfect. At the end, I’ll summarise the next steps.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n97.Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n98.Where does the speaker pause?\n99.What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCan you hear me clearly? check the audio connection\nYou’re breaking up a little. report an unstable connection\nCould you say that again more slowly? request a clearer repetition\nLet me share my screen. move to visual information\nI’ll summarise the next steps. close with an action review\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ you hear me clearly?\n2. You’re ________ up a little.\n3. Could you _______ that again more slowly?\n4. _______ me share my screen.\n5. I’ll _________ the next steps.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Can, could and would for requests; present continuous for connection problems.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n Yes. Could you say the report ID again more slowly?\n\n\n\nWEEK 5 | LESSON 15 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nPhone and video calls\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Practise letters, numbers, dates and spelling in short groups.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF \nconfirm the details and summarise the next action.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete a four-minute audio-only work call.\nSUCCESS CHECK I manage the opening, repair one communication problem and close with confirmed steps.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord a simulated phone call. Include a spelling check, a number and a clear closing summary.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\nA: Read a short report ID, date and email address. Create one connection problem. B: Manage the problem,\n\n"},{"number":16,"title":"Describe a workplace problem","content":"WEEK 6 | LESSON 16 | EXPLORE\nDescribe a workplace problem\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain a problem objectively, including its impact and urgency.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n100. What facts belong in a problem report?\n101. How is impact different from cause?\n102. Why should you avoid guessing?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nManager: What seems to be the problem?\nEmployee\nManager: When did it start?\nEmployee\nManager: What is the most urgent concern?\nEmployee\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n103. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n104. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n105. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nWe’ve noticed an issue with... introduce a problem neutrally\nThe problem started when... give the origin\nThis is affecting... explain impact\nThe most urgent concern is... identify priority\nWhat we know so far is... separate facts from guesses\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. We’ve _______ an issue with...\n2. The _______ started when...\n3. This is _________...\n4. The _______ urgent concern is...\n5. _______ we know so far is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION There is/are, present perfect and passive forms for current problems.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n We’ve noticed an issue with the latest report.\n The problem started when one figure was changed. This is affecting two report sections.\n The most urgent concern is tomorrow’s submission. What we know so far is that the team has not received the update.\n\n\n\nWEEK 6 | LESSON 16 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDescribe a workplace problem\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use clear final consonants in stopped, missed, changed and delayed.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Give a scenario with a missing document, delayed task or incorrect detail. Ask for facts. B: Report the \nproblem, cause, impact, urgency and known facts.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a 90-second problem report followed by three questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK I am factual, specific and clear about urgency without blaming people.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a short problem report with five headings: issue, start, impact, urgency and known facts.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":17,"title":"Suggest and compare solutions","content":"WEEK 6 | LESSON 17 | BUILD\nSuggest and compare solutions\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Offer options, explain advantages and recommend a practical response.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n106. What makes a recommendation convincing?\n107. How many options should you compare?\n108. Which matters more: speed, cost or quality?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nTeam lead: How can we handle the delay?\nEmployee\nTeam lead: Is there another possibility?\nEmployee\nlimited preparation.\nTeam lead: Which do you prefer?\nEmployee\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n109. How does the speaker begin?\n110. What detail develops the message?\n111. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nOne option would be to... introduce a possible solution\nAnother possibility is... add an alternative\nThe advantage of this option is... explain a benefit\nThe main risk is... identify a disadvantage\nI’d recommend... because... make a reasoned choice\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ option would be to...\n2. _______ possibility is...\n3. The _________ of this option is...\n4. The _______ risk is...\n5. I’d _________... because...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Should, could and might; comparatives for options.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n One option would be to move the submission to Thursday.\n Another possibility is to ask another analyst to help. The advantage is that we keep the original date, but the main risk is\n I’d recommend Thursday because the current team already understands the data.\n\n\n\nWEEK 6 | LESSON 17 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nSuggest and compare solutions\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Reduce modals naturally: we could, we should, it might.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Present a simple workplace problem and ask for two options. B: Offer two solutions, compare benefits and \nrisks, then recommend one.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Lead a three-minute solution discussion and reach a decision.\nSUCCESS CHECK My recommendation follows a fair comparison and includes a clear reason.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nChoose one everyday problem. Record two options, one risk and your recommendation.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":18,"title":"Explain a process step by step","content":"WEEK 6 | LESSON 18 | PERFORM\nExplain a process step by step\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Make a work or daily process easy to follow for a new person.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n112. Which process do you know well?\n113. Where do beginners usually get confused?\n114. Why does each main step matter?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nNew colleague: How do we prepare the weekly update?\nEmployee: The process begins with collecting information from each team.\nNew colleague: What happens next?\nEmployee: Once that is complete, we check the dates and progress. The next step is to write a short summary.\nNew colleague: Is there a final check?\nEmployee: Yes. Before we finish, we need to confirm the numbers. The purpose of this step is to prevent reporting errors.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n115. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n116. Where does the speaker pause?\n117. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThe process begins with... introduce the first stage\nOnce that is complete, ... show a required sequence\nThe next step is to... move forward\nBefore we finish, we need to... name a final check\nThe purpose of this step is... explain why a step matters\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. The _______ begins with...\n2. _______ that is complete, ...\n3. The _______ step is to...\n4. _______ we finish, we need to...\n5. The _______ of this step is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Sequencing; imperatives and present simple passive where useful.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 6 | LESSON 18 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nExplain a process step by step\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause after each step and stress action verbs.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Act as a new colleague. Interrupt only when a step, order or purpose is unclear. B: Explain a four- to six￾step process without reading full sentences.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Teach your partner one process and answer clarification questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My explanation has a clear sequence, action verbs and at least one reason.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nDraw a six-step process map. Record the explanation using only the map as notes.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":19,"title":"Share opinions in meetings","content":"WEEK 7 | LESSON 19 | EXPLORE\nShare opinions in meetings\nTODAY'S OUTCOME State an opinion, support it and invite another person to respond.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n118. Which meeting topics require opinions?\n119. What evidence can support an opinion?\n120. How do you invite quieter participants?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nChair: Should we change the weekly meeting time?\nMember: From my point of view, we should move it to the morning.\nChair: Why?\nMember\nChair: Anything else?\nMember: Morning would improve attendance. That’s how I see it. What’s your view?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n121. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n122. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n123. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nFrom my point of view... state a considered opinion\nThe main reason is... support the opinion\nFor instance... add evidence\nThat’s how I see it. close your explanation\nWhat’s your view? invite another speaker\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ my point of view...\n2. The _______ reason is...\n3. For ________...\n4. That’s _______ I see it.\n5. _______’s your view?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Opinion clauses and because for reasons.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n The main reason is that reporting deadlines often fall later. For instance, two people missed last week’s meeting.\n\n\n\nWEEK 7 | LESSON 19 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nShare opinions in meetings\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the main opinion word and the strongest reason.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Give a meeting topic and listen without interrupting. Ask for a reason and example. B: State an opinion, \nreason and example, then invite your partner’s view.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Contribute twice to a five-minute mini-meeting.\nSUCCESS CHECK Each contribution has one clear point, supporting reason and invitation to respond.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord a 90-second opinion about remote work, professional training or technology.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":20,"title":"Agree and disagree politely","content":"WEEK 7 | LESSON 20 | BUILD\nAgree and disagree politely\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Respond to ideas with respect while adding, qualifying or challenging a point.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n124. Why do people sometimes avoid disagreement?\n125. Which phrase softens a challenge?\n126. How can disagreement improve a decision?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nColleague A: I think every update should be a long written report.\nColleague B: I see your point; however, long reports take time to read.\nColleague A: What would you suggest?\nColleague B: Could we consider a short dashboard instead?\nColleague A: That’s a good point. I’d add a monthly detailed report.\nColleague B: I agree with that because it gives us both speed and detail.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n127. How does the speaker begin?\n128. What detail develops the message?\n129. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI agree with that because... give supported agreement\nThat’s a good point. I’d add... build on an idea\nI see your point; however... disagree respectfully\nI’m not completely convinced that... express careful doubt\nCould we consider... instead? offer an alternative\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I _______ with that because...\n2. That’s a _______ point. I’d add...\n3. I _______ your point; however...\n4. I’m _______ completely convinced that...\n5. Could we ________... instead?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Contrast with but, however and while; comparatives.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 7 | LESSON 20 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nAgree and disagree politely\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use a warm tone when disagreeing; stress the idea, not the person.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Defend one option: written report, dashboard or verbal update. B: Agree with one part, disagree with \nanother and offer an alternative.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Reach agreement after a four-minute respectful disagreement.\nSUCCESS CHECK I respond to the idea, give a reason and avoid personal language.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite three polite disagreements. Read them aloud twice: neutral tone, then warmer tone.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":21,"title":"Messages and follow-up emails","content":"WEEK 7 | LESSON 21 | PERFORM\nMessages and follow-up emails\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Write and speak concise professional requests, updates and follow-ups.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n130. When should you send a follow-up?\n131. What belongs in the subject line?\n132. How can you make the requested action clear?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: What should your follow-up message include?\nStudent: I’m writing to follow up on yesterday’s meeting.\nTutor: What information do you need?\nStudent: Could you please confirm the final deadline? As discussed, I’ll prepare the first draft.\nTutor: How will you close?\nStudent: Please let me know if you need anything else. Thank you for your assistance.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n133. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n134. Where does the speaker pause?\n135. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI’m writing to follow up on... reopen an earlier topic\nCould you please confirm...? request important information\nAs discussed, ... refer to an agreed point\nPlease let me know if... invite a response or question\nThank you for your assistance. close a professional request\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I’m _______ to follow up on...\n2. Could you please _______...?\n3. As _________, ...\n4. Please _______ me know if...\n5. _______ you for your assistance.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Polite requests with could/would; present perfect for follow-up.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 7 | LESSON 21 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nMessages and follow-up emails\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Read messages aloud in meaningful chunks, not word by word.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Explain a meeting decision and one missing detail. B: Give an oral summary, then draft a 70-word follow-up\nmessage.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Read your message aloud; your partner identifies purpose, action and deadline.\nSUCCESS CHECK My message is polite, concise and makes the next action unmistakable.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a 100-120-word professional email requesting confirmation of a deadline or meeting.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nMODULE 3\nINTERVIEW & CAREER COMMUNICATION\nTurn qualifications and experience into clear, evidence-based interview answers.\n22 EXPLORE Answer: Tell me about yourself\n23 BUILD Qualifications and experience\n24 PERFORM Strengths and development areas\n25 EXPLORE Build a STAR answer\n26 BUILD Present achievements with evidence\n27 PERFORM Discuss challenges and lessons\n28 EXPLORE Career goals and motivation\n29 BUILD Why should we hire you?\n30 PERFORM Ask intelligent interview questions\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":22,"title":"Answer: Tell me about yourself","content":"WEEK 8 | LESSON 22 | EXPLORE\nAnswer: Tell me about yourself\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Give a focused interview introduction connecting present work, relevant background and future \nvalue.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n136. What information is relevant to the job?\n137. How is this answer different from a personal biography?\n138. What value can you offer?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Tell me about yourself.\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n139. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n140. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n141. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCurrently, I... begin with present professional activity\nBefore that, I... add relevant background\nThrough that experience, I developed... name transferable skills\nWhat interests me about this opportunity is... connect to the role\nI believe I can contribute by... state potential value\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _________, I...\n2. _______ that, I...\n3. _______ that experience, I developed...\n4. _______ interests me about this opportunity is...\n5. I _______ I can contribute by...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present-past-future structure; present simple and past simple.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n Currently, I work as a management consultant, where I support analysis, reports and recommendations.\n Before that, I completed postgraduate study and developed experience in management-related work.\n Through that experience, I developed management, communication and problem-solving skills.\n What interests me about this opportunity is the chance to take on greater responsibility.\n I believe I can contribute by combining analytical thinking with careful coordination.\n\n\n\nWEEK 8 | LESSON 22 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nAnswer: Tell me about yourself\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Begin strongly and avoid a rising, uncertain ending.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Act as interviewer. Ask the main question and two follow-ups about experience and value. B: Answer in 60-\n90 seconds using present, past and future.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Deliver the answer twice: first with notes, then with five keywords only.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer is relevant, structured and connected to the opportunity.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite five keywords for your answer. Record a 90-second version without a full script.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":23,"title":"Qualifications and experience","content":"WEEK 8 | LESSON 23 | BUILD\nQualifications and experience\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Connect education, training and experience directly to a job requirement.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n142. Which qualification best matches your target role?\n143. What evidence proves you can use a skill?\n144. When should you use past simple?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: What relevant experience do you have?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: Where did you gain it?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: How does it prepare you for this role?\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n145. How does the speaker begin?\n146. What detail develops the message?\n147. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI have experience in... summarise relevant experience\nI gained this experience while... locate where learning happened\nI have used... for... show duration or purpose\nThis prepared me to... connect experience to readiness\nA relevant example is... provide evidence\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I have __________ in...\n2. I _______ this experience while...\n3. I have _______... for...\n4. This ________ me to...\n5. A ________ example is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present perfect versus past simple; for and since.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n I have experience in management consulting, report writing and coordinating work.\n I gained this experience through public-sector work and management assignments.\n This prepared me to organise information carefully. A relevant example is an assignment where I coordinated inputs for a\nmanagement report.\n\n\n\nWEEK 8 | LESSON 23 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nQualifications and experience\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR \nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Read three fictional job requirements and ask for evidence for each. B: Match two real qualifications or \nexperiences to the requirements.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Answer: ‘What relevant experience do you have?’ with two pieces of evidence.\nSUCCESS CHECK I do more than list qualifications; I explain relevance and evidence.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nChoose a real job advertisement. Highlight three requirements and prepare one evidence sentence for each.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\nPronounce qualifications, experience, consultant and management clearly.\n\n"},{"number":24,"title":"Strengths and development areas","content":"WEEK 8 | LESSON 24 | PERFORM\nStrengths and development areas\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe strengths with evidence and discuss improvement honestly and constructively.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n148. Which strengths matter for your target career?\n149. What evidence can you give?\n150. Which development area is honest but manageable?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nInterviewer: What is one of your strengths?\nCandidate: One of my strengths is careful planning. For example, I break large tasks into clear stages.\nInterviewer: How do others see that strength?\nCandidate: Colleagues often rely on me to check details before a deadline.\nInterviewer: What are you currently improving?\nCandidate: An area I’m developing is public speaking. I’m improving it by practising short presentations every week.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n151. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n152. Where does the speaker pause?\n153. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nOne of my strengths is... name a relevant strength\nFor example, I... prove the strength\nColleagues often rely on me to... show external evidence\nAn area I’m developing is... name a manageable improvement area\nI’m improving it by... show active development\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ of my strengths is...\n2. For _______, I...\n3. __________ often rely on me to...\n4. An _______ I’m developing is...\n5. I’m _________ it by...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Adjective-noun collocations; can and have improved.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 8 | LESSON 24 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nStrengths and development areas\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the strength and the evidence, not every word.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask for one strength, proof, one development area and current action. B: Give a balanced answer without \nusing clichés such as ‘I work too hard.’\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Answer two interview questions: one strength and one development area.\nSUCCESS CHECK Every claim includes evidence or a concrete improvement action.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a strength-evidence-action chart for three strengths and one development area.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":25,"title":"Build a STAR answer","content":"WEEK 9 | LESSON 25 | EXPLORE\nBuild a STAR answer\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Use Situation, Task, Action and Result to answer behavioural interview questions.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n154. What does each STAR letter mean?\n155. Which stage should usually be longest?\n156. Why should you say ‘I’ when describing your action?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a time you solved a problem.\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate\nCandidate: What I learned was to confirm critical information early.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n157. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n158. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n159. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThe situation was... set the context briefly\nMy responsibility was to... state the task\nThe action I took was... focus on your contribution\nAs a result, ... show the outcome\nWhat I learned was... add reflection when useful\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. The _________ was...\n2. My ______________ was to...\n3. The _______ I took was...\n4. As a _______, ...\n5. _______ I learned was...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple and past continuous; sequence and result connectors.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n The situation was a report with inconsistent financial information.\n My responsibility was to identify the correct figures before the deadline.\n The action I took was to compare the records and contact the relevant department.\n As a result, we corrected the report on time.\n\n\n\nWEEK 9 | LESSON 25 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nBuild a STAR answer\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause clearly between the four STAR stages.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask one behavioural question and signal if any STAR stage is missing. B: Answer using four short sections; \nfocus most on Action.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute STAR answer about problem-solving or teamwork.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer contains all four stages and makes my personal action clear.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nComplete a STAR planner for a real experience. Record the answer using only four headings.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":26,"title":"Present achievements with evidence","content":"WEEK 9 | LESSON 26 | BUILD\nPresent achievements with evidence\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe an achievement using specific actions, measurable results and relevant learning.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n160. Which achievement is relevant to your target role?\n161. What was your personal contribution?\n162. Can you express the result with a number or comparison?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: Which achievement are you most proud of?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: What was your contribution?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: What was the outcome?\nCandidate: The outcome was an early submission with fewer errors. This demonstrates my ability to coordinate work carefully.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n163. How does the speaker begin?\n164. What detail develops the message?\n165. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI’m particularly proud of... introduce a meaningful achievement\nMy specific contribution was... separate your role from the team’s\nWe improved... by... quantify positive change\nThe outcome was... state the final result\nThis demonstrates my ability to... connect achievement to a competency\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I’m ____________ proud of...\n2. My ________ contribution was...\n3. We ________... by...\n4. The _______ was...\n\n5. This ____________ my ability to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Result language, comparatives and quantifiers.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n I’m particularly proud of completing a management report before its deadline.\n My specific contribution was organising the information and checking progress.\n\n\n\nWEEK 9 | LESSON 26 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nPresent achievements with evidence\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Say numbers, dates and percentages slowly enough to be understood.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask for achievement, contribution, evidence and relevance. B: Present one achievement and answer follow￾up questions with specific details.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a 90-second achievement answer containing one measurable or observable result.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer proves impact instead of only saying the work was successful.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite three result statements using a number, time saved, quality improvement or completed output.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":27,"title":"Discuss challenges and lessons","content":"WEEK 9 | LESSON 27 | PERFORM\nDiscuss challenges and lessons\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Take responsibility, explain a constructive response and show learning.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n166. What type of challenge is safe and relevant to discuss?\n167. How do you show responsibility?\n168. What lasting change proves learning?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a mistake or difficult situation.\nCandidate: One difficulty I encountered was underestimating how long a task would take.\nCandidate: I realised that I had not allowed enough time for checking.\nCandidate: I took responsibility for informing the team early.\nCandidate\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n169. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n170. Where does the speaker pause?\n171. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nOne difficulty I encountered was... introduce a professional challenge\nI realised that... show awareness\nI took responsibility for... explain ownership\nTo address it, I... describe corrective action\nSince then, I have... show lasting improvement\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ difficulty I encountered was...\n2. I ________ that...\n3. I _______ responsibility for...\n4. To _______ it, I...\n5. _______ then, I have...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past forms; if + present for future response.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n To address it, I revised the work plan and asked for feedback.\n Since then, I have added review time to every important report.\n\n\n\nWEEK 9 | LESSON 27 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nDiscuss challenges and lessons\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use a calm, responsible tone and avoid defensive stress.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask about challenge, awareness, responsibility, action and lasting improvement. B: Discuss a genuine but \nmanageable challenge without blaming other people.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute lesson-learned answer using all five stages.\nSUCCESS CHECK I show honesty, action and improvement; the answer ends positively.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nWrite a before-and-after reflection: what I did before, what happened and what I do now.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":28,"title":"Career goals and motivation","content":"WEEK 10 | LESSON 28 | EXPLORE\nCareer goals and motivation\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Explain why a role fits your interests, skills and realistic future direction.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n172. What genuinely motivates you?\n173. Which part of the role fits your background?\n174. What future goal sounds realistic?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nInterviewer: Why are you interested in this role?\nCandidate\nCandidate\nInterviewer: Where do you see yourself in a few years?\nCandidate\nCandidate: This opportunity would allow me to learn from an experienced international team.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n175. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n176. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n177. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nI’m motivated by... explain a genuine source of energy\nThis role appeals to me because... connect motivation to the opportunity\nIn the next few years, I hope to... state a realistic direction\nI’d like to develop... identify a growth goal\nThis opportunity would allow me to... connect the role to progress\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. I’m _________ by...\n2. This _______ appeals to me because...\n3. In the _______ few years, I hope to...\n4. I’d _______ to develop...\n5. This ___________ would allow me to...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future forms: want to, hope to, plan to and would like to.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n I’m motivated by roles that improve how organisations and public services work.\n This role appeals to me because it combines management, analysis and teamwork.\n I hope to take greater responsibility and develop stronger management and leadership skills.\n\n\n\nWEEK 10 | LESSON 28 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nCareer goals and motivation\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use contractions naturally but make key career words clear.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask why this role, why this organisation and what next. B: Connect personal motivation to actual job \nresponsibilities.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute motivation and career-goal answer.\nSUCCESS CHECK My answer is specific to the role and does not sound memorised or exaggerated.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nResearch one organisation. Prepare three honest reasons you could be interested in its work.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":29,"title":"Why should we hire you?","content":"WEEK 10 | LESSON 29 | BUILD\nWhy should we hire you?\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Make a concise value argument using requirement, evidence and contribution.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n178. What are the employer’s top three needs?\n179. Which evidence supports each need?\n180. What value can you contribute immediately?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: Why should we hire you?\nCandidate: This role requires careful planning, communication and reliable follow-up.\nCandidate\nCandidate: I can bring a calm approach and strong attention to detail.\nCandidate\nCandidate: For these reasons, I believe I can contribute effectively from the beginning.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n181. How does the speaker begin?\n182. What detail develops the message?\n183. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThis role requires... identify the employer’s need\nMy experience has prepared me to... connect background to performance\nI can bring... state a useful contribution\nA strong example is... support the claim\nFor these reasons, I believe... close the argument\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. This _______ requires...\n2. My __________ has prepared me to...\n3. I _______ bring...\n4. A _______ example is...\n5. For _______ reasons, I believe...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Because, therefore and can to connect evidence to value.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n My experience has prepared me to analyse information and coordinate tasks.\n A strong example is a recent report that I helped complete ahead of deadline.\n\n\n\nWEEK 10 | LESSON 29 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nWhy should we hire you?\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the three key value words in your answer.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Read a fictional job description and challenge any unsupported claim. B: Give a 60-second value argument \nwith one strong example.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Answer ‘Why should we hire you?’ and respond to two evidence questions.\nSUCCESS CHECK My claims match the role and are supported by believable evidence.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a three-column note: requirement, my evidence, my contribution. Record the final answer.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":30,"title":"Ask intelligent interview questions","content":"WEEK 10 | LESSON 30 | PERFORM\nAsk intelligent interview questions\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Ask relevant questions, respond to answers and close an interview professionally.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n184. Which questions show preparation?\n185. Which questions can be answered from the website?\n186. How many questions should you prepare?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nInterviewer: Do you have any questions for us?\nCandidate: Yes. What would success look like in the first three months?\nInterviewer\nCandidate: That’s helpful. Could you tell me more about how the team works together?\nInterviewer\nCandidate: Thank you. What are the next steps in the process?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n187. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n188. Where does the speaker pause?\n189. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nCould you tell me more about...? request detail about the role\nHow would you describe...? ask about team or culture\nWhat would success look like in...? understand expectations\nWhat are the next steps in the process? ask about the recruitment timeline\nThank you for the opportunity to speak with you. close the interview\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. Could you _______ me more about...?\n2. _______ would you describe...?\n3. _______ would success look like in...?\n4. _______ are the next steps in the process?\n5. _______ you for the opportunity to speak with you.\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect and open questions; would/could for polite enquiry.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n We would expect the person to understand the team and manage one small assignment.\n We have a weekly team meeting and daily online updates.\n\n\n\nWEEK 10 | LESSON 30 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nAsk intelligent interview questions\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use falling intonation for open questions and clear closing statements.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Answer as an interviewer using details from a fictional organisation. B: Ask three prepared questions and \none natural follow-up.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete the final five minutes of a mock interview, including a professional closing.\nSUCCESS CHECK My questions are relevant, open and connected to the role.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nPrepare five questions for a real target role. Rank them from most to least useful.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nMODULE 4\nPRESENTATION & MASTERY\nPresent ideas, handle questions and demonstrate measurable progress.\n31 EXPLORE Open a presentation confidently\n32 BUILD Organise and connect ideas\n33 PERFORM Explain information and visuals\n34 EXPLORE Handle audience questions\n35 BUILD Final interview rehearsal\n36 PERFORM Final showcase and next plan\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":31,"title":"Open a presentation confidently","content":"WEEK 11 | LESSON 31 | EXPLORE\nOpen a presentation confidently\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Gain attention, state purpose and preview a short professional presentation.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n190. What does an audience need in the first 30 seconds?\n191. How many main points suit a five-minute talk?\n192. What makes an opening sound confident?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nPresenter: Good morning, and thank you for joining.\nPresenter\nPresenter: The purpose of this presentation is to recommend a clearer reporting process.\nPresenter: I’ll cover three main points: the current issue, two options and my recommendation.\nPresenter: The talk will take about five minutes.\nPresenter: Let’s begin with the current issue.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n193. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n194. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n195. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nGood morning, and thank you for joining. open formally and warmly\nToday I’d like to talk about... state the topic\nThe purpose of this presentation is... state the objective\nI’ll cover three main points. preview the structure\nLet’s begin with... move into the first point\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ morning, and thank you for joining.\n2. _______ I’d like to talk about...\n3. The _______ of this presentation is...\n4. I’ll _______ three main points.\n5. _______’s begin with...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple for purpose and overview; will for preview.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n Today I’d like to talk about improving our monthly management reports.\n\n\n\nWEEK 11 | LESSON 31 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nOpen a presentation confidently\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Pause after the greeting, title and roadmap.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Listen with your notes closed. After the opening, state the topic, purpose and roadmap you heard. B: Deliver\na 30-second opening and adjust it after feedback.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Open a five-minute professional presentation without reading full sentences.\nSUCCESS CHECK My audience knows the topic, purpose, structure and approximate length.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nRecord three versions of the same 30-second opening. Choose the clearest one.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":32,"title":"Organise and connect ideas","content":"WEEK 11 | LESSON 32 | BUILD\nOrganise and connect ideas\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Build a clear beginning, middle and end using signposting language.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n196. What is the difference between a point and an example?\n197. Which signpost marks contrast?\n198. How should a conclusion connect to the purpose?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nPresenter: First, let’s look at the current reporting problem.\nPresenter: Updates are detailed, but they are difficult to scan quickly.\nPresenter: This brings me to my second point: a shorter dashboard.\nPresenter: In contrast, a dashboard shows the main information on one page.\nPresenter: The key message here is that format affects how quickly people can act.\nPresenter: To sum up, I recommend short weekly dashboards and one detailed monthly report.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n199. How does the speaker begin?\n200. What detail develops the message?\n201. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nFirst, let’s look at... open the first section\nThis brings me to my second point. signal a transition\nIn contrast... show an important difference\nThe key message here is... highlight meaning\nTo sum up... begin the conclusion\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______, let’s look at...\n2. This _______ me to my second point.\n3. In ________...\n4. The _______ message here is...\n5. To _______ up...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Sequence and contrast linkers; topic sentences.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 11 | LESSON 32 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nOrganise and connect ideas\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use pauses to mark transitions between sections.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Use five cards: opening, point 1, point 2, point 3 and conclusion. Mix them before your partner speaks. B: \nOrganise the cards and deliver a two-minute mini-presentation.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Present three connected points and a conclusion in three minutes.\nSUCCESS CHECK My transitions are audible and my conclusion returns to the main purpose.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nCreate a one-page presentation map with keywords only. Practise without full sentences.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":33,"title":"Explain information and visuals","content":"WEEK 11 | LESSON 33 | PERFORM\nExplain information and visuals\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Describe a simple chart, plan or process and state what the audience should notice.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n202. What should you say before reading numbers?\n203. How is description different from interpretation?\n204. Which one trend matters most?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nPresenter\nPresenter: The most noticeable change is the increase in Week 3.\nPresenter\nAudience: What was the exact number?\nPresenter\nPresenter\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n205. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n206. Where does the speaker pause?\n207. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThis visual shows... identify what the audience is seeing\nThe most noticeable change is... highlight the main pattern\nCompared with..., ... make a comparison\nApproximately... give a rounded figure\nWhat this means is... interpret the information\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. This _______ shows...\n2. The _______ noticeable change is...\n3. ________ with..., ...\n4. _____________...\n5. _______ this means is...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Comparatives, quantities and approximation language.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n This visual shows the number of completed reports over four weeks.\n Compared with Week 1, the team completed almost twice as many reports.\n Approximately forty reports were completed in Week 3.\n What this means is that the new reporting method may be improving consistency.\n\n\n\nWEEK 11 | LESSON 33 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nExplain information and visuals\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Say numbers in groups and pause before the key interpretation.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Sketch a simple four-week chart with invented, safe data and ask for a description. B: Describe the visual, \ncompare two points and interpret the main message.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Explain one chart, diagram or process for two minutes, then answer a question.\nSUCCESS CHECK I identify the visual, select the main pattern and explain why it matters.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nFind or draw a simple professional visual. Record a 90-second explanation using four phrases.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":34,"title":"Handle audience questions","content":"WEEK 12 | LESSON 34 | EXPLORE\nHandle audience questions\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Listen fully, acknowledge the question and give a clear, bounded answer.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer from experience. Add a reason or example after each answer.\n208. What should you do before answering?\n209. How can you respond when you do not know?\n210. When should you promise a follow-up?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once with the text covered. State the situation and the speaker's goal. Listen again and number the main ideas in the order \nyou hear them.\nAudience: How much time would the new process save each week?\nPresenter: Thank you for that question. If I understand you correctly, you’re asking about weekly time savings.\nAudience: Yes, exactly.\nPresenter: The short answer is about two hours per team. I don’t have the final figure with me, but I can confirm it after the \nmeeting.\nAudience: That would be useful.\nPresenter: Of course. Does that answer your question for now?\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n211. What is the speaker trying to achieve?\n212. Which line makes the message easy to follow?\n213. Which expression could you reuse today?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThank you for that question. acknowledge the audience\nIf I understand you correctly, ... confirm a complex question\nThe short answer is... begin concisely\nI don’t have that figure with me, but... respond honestly when information is missing\nDoes that answer your question? check satisfaction\nCover the expression column. Use the communication jobs to recall each expression. Then uncover it and correct yourself.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. _______ you for that question.\n2. If I __________ you correctly, ...\n3. The _______ answer is...\n4. I _______’t have that figure with me, but...\n5. Does that _______ your question?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect questions, conditionals and repair language.\nCreate two true examples and one question. Check the verb form before you speak.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 12 | LESSON 34 | EXPLORE | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nHandle audience questions\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Repeat the key word from the question before answering.\nMark thought groups with / in one model line. Listen, shadow, then say it with your own detail.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Ask one clear, one unclear and one unanswerable question about the presentation. B: Acknowledge, clarify \nif needed, answer honestly and check understanding.\nRound 1: use the support phrases. Round 2: change one detail and speak from keywords only.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Handle five audience questions without becoming defensive or inventing information.\nSUCCESS CHECK I answer the real question, stay concise and clearly mark any follow-up needed.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nPrepare five likely questions for your final presentation and outline a two-sentence answer for each.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":35,"title":"Final interview rehearsal","content":"WEEK 12 | LESSON 35 | BUILD\nFinal interview rehearsal\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Integrate structure, evidence, interaction and fluency in a complete mock interview.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer quickly, then connect one answer to language from the previous lesson.\n214. Which interview answer still needs the most practice?\n215. Which filler helps you organise without delay?\n216. What evidence do you want the interviewer to remember?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and identify the beginning, development and ending. Read in roles, then close the book and rebuild the conversation \nfrom six keywords.\nInterviewer: What makes you a strong candidate for this role?\nCandidate\nInterviewer: Can you give an example?\nCandidate\nCandidate: The result of that action was an earlier, more accurate submission.\nCandidate\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n217. How does the speaker begin?\n218. What detail develops the message?\n219. How does the speaker finish or invite a response?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nThat’s an important question. gain a brief moment to organise\nA relevant example would be... move from claim to evidence\nThe result of that action was... complete an evidence story\nTo connect that to this role... make relevance explicit\nIs there any part you’d like me to expand on? invite a follow-up\nChoose three expressions. Make one true sentence with each, then extend every sentence with a reason, example or result.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. That’s an _________ question.\n2. A ________ example would be...\n3. The _______ of that action was...\n4. To _______ that to this role...\n5. Is _______ any part you’d like me to expand on?\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Mixed review based on personal error patterns.\nSay one accurate sentence, change its time or subject, then ask your partner to check it.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n That’s an important question. I believe my management experience and communication skills are especially relevant.\n A relevant example would be a report process where I improved how information was organised.\n To connect that to this role, I can bring the same structured approach to your team.\n\n\n\nWEEK 12 | LESSON 35 | BUILD | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nFinal interview rehearsal\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Use clear sentence stress, natural pace and decisive endings.\nRead one line slowly, then naturally. Keep the stressed words clear while reducing the small words.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Conduct an eight-question interview. Note one strength and two corrections; do not interrupt answers. B: \nAnswer naturally, ask for clarification when needed and ask two questions at the end.\nRound 1: complete the roles slowly. Round 2: switch roles, add one follow-up question and remove full-sentence notes.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete a 15-minute mock interview with delayed feedback and a second attempt at two answers.\nSUCCESS CHECK I answer relevantly, support claims, interact naturally and improve after feedback.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Apply the repair, use two toolkit expressions and improve one delivery feature.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nReview the recording. Write two exact sentences to keep and two to improve before the final assessment.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n"},{"number":36,"title":"Final showcase and next plan","content":"WEEK 12 | LESSON 36 | PERFORM\nFinal showcase and next plan\nTODAY'S OUTCOME Demonstrate progress and create a practical 30-day continuation plan.\n1 CONNECT | 5 min\nAnswer without notes. Your partner asks one follow-up after each response.\n220. Which recording best shows your progress?\n221. Which strategy will you keep using?\n222. What is one realistic 30-day goal?\n2 MODEL IN ACTION | 10 min\nListen once and take only five keywords. Retell the message in 30 seconds, then compare your version with the model.\nTutor: How has your speaking changed?\nStudent: At the beginning of this course, I often stopped when I made a mistake.\nTutor: What is different now?\nStudent: The biggest improvement I’ve noticed is that I can organise my ideas and keep speaking.\nTutor: What will you work on next?\nStudent: My next target is presentation fluency. I’ll continue by recording one short talk every week.\n3 DECODE THE MODEL | 6 min\n223. Which five keywords carry the meaning?\n224. Where does the speaker pause?\n225. What would make the answer stronger?\n4 EXPRESSION TOOLKIT | 10 min\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION COMMUNICATION JOB\nAt the beginning of this course... describe the starting point\nThe biggest improvement I’ve noticed is... identify progress\nI can now... state a new ability\nMy next target is... choose a focused goal\nI’ll continue by... commit to a practical action\nComplete the retrieval lines without looking. Use all five expressions in a 60-second response; repeat in 45 seconds without losing \nmeaning.\n5 RETRIEVAL CHALLENGE | 7 min\n1. At the _________ of this course...\n2. The _______ improvement I’ve noticed is...\n3. I _______ now...\n4. My _______ target is...\n5. I’ll ________ by...\n6 ACCURACY FOCUS | 7 min\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future intentions and review of personal high-frequency errors.\nCorrect one sentence from your earlier speaking. Repeat it three times with new details.\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nWEEK 12 | LESSON 36 | PERFORM | PERFORMANCE PAGE\nFinal showcase and next plan\n7 CLEAR SPEECH COACH | 6 min\nSOUND CLEAR Compare the baseline and final recordings for pace, pausing and clarity.\nSay the same message in 30 seconds and 20 seconds. Keep complete thought groups; do not race word by word.\n8 GUIDED REHEARSAL | 12 min\nROLE BRIEF A: Listen to the final showcase and ask questions about progress, evidence and next steps. B: Deliver a five￾minute presentation, answer questions and reflect honestly.\nRound 1: rehearse with a planning minute. Round 2: perform with a new detail, no script and one unexpected follow-up.\nSUPPORT CHALLENGE\nUse the toolkit and three written keywords. Close the toolkit; add a reason, example and follow-up.\n9 CAREER PERFORMANCE MISSION | 12 min\nYOUR MISSION Complete the final portfolio: conversation, interview, presentation and 30-day learning plan.\nSUCCESS CHECK I demonstrate progress with evidence and leave with one specific, scheduled next action.\nPlanning keywords: 1) __________________ 2) __________________ 3) __________________\n10 FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY | 10 min\nKEEP One sentence or strategy that worked: __________________________________\nREPAIR One correction that improves communication: __________________________________\nUPGRADE One stronger expression to add: __________________________________\nRETRY RULE Repeat the most difficult 60 seconds now. Apply the repair, keep the message complete and save the stronger \nattempt.\n11 INDEPENDENT TRANSFER | 15-30 min\nComplete the 30-day plan at the back of this book and schedule the first three practice sessions.\nSpaced review: revisit today's five expressions after 2 days and again after 7 days.\nWEEKLY EVIDENCE Save one recording or written product. Name it with the week, lesson and date. Compare it with last \nweek's evidence.\nAfter this lesson, I can: [ ] Not yet [ ] With support [ ] Independently\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nFinal performance portfolio\nComplete these tasks during Week 12. Use keywords, not full scripts.\nCONVERSATION Maintain a six-minute conversation. Open naturally, ask follow-ups, clarify once and close professionally.\nINTERVIEW Complete a 15-minute interview. Include a structured introduction, two STAR answers and two questions for \nthe interviewer.\nPRESENTATION Deliver a five-minute professional presentation with three points, one simple visual and audience \nquestions.\nWRITING Write a 120-150-word professional follow-up email with a clear purpose, action and deadline.\nSpeaking performance rubric\nAREA 3 - INDEPENDENT 2 - DEVELOPING 1 - NEEDS SUPPORT\nTask Completes the purpose clearly Completes most of the purpose Needs prompts to complete it\nFluency Keeps speaking with manageable \npauses\nSome long pauses, but the message \ncontinues\nFrequent pauses stop the message\nInteraction Responds, follows up and clarifies Responds but rarely extends Short answers; needs repeated \nsupport\nLanguage Useful range; errors rarely block \nmeaning\nSimple range; some errors affect \nclarity\nLimited range; errors often block \nmeaning\nPronunciation Generally clear with useful stress Usually understandable with some \nstrain\nOften difficult to understand\nPortfolio reflection\nThe clearest evidence of my progress is...\nThe communication habit I will continue is...\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nRetrieval challenge answer key 1/2\nCheck only after you have attempted the activity from memory.\nL. LESSON MISSING WORDS 1-5\n1 Your starting point Let | What | repeat | experience | One\n2 A strong professional introduction currently | background | responsible | particularly | current\n3 Keep speaking without long pauses There | first | Another | example | Let\n4 Start conversations naturally How | What | met | How | sounds\n5 Show interest and ask follow-ups Really | How | What | mean | can\n6 End conversations professionally was | should | Let | send | look\n7 Describe your work clearly main | work | typical | purpose | simple\n8 Routines and current priorities usually | moment | Most | week | top\n9 Give a useful work update far | currently | main | expect | next\n10 Tell a past experience few | had | role | most | experience\n11 Sequence a professional story first | While | After | result | end\n12 Describe success and challenge main | Although | decided | What | did\n13 Meet international contacts Where | What | How | seem | useful\n14 Clarify and confirm meaning clarify | understand | mean | Let | give\n15 Phone and video calls Can | breaking | say | Let | summarise\n16 Describe a workplace problem noticed | problem | affecting | most | What\n17 Suggest and compare solutions One | Another | advantage | main | recommend\n18 Explain a process step by step process | Once | next | Before | purpose\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nRetrieval challenge answer key 2/2\nCheck only after you have attempted the activity from memory.\nL. LESSON MISSING WORDS 1-5\n19 Share opinions in meetings From | main | instance | how | What\n20 Agree and disagree politely agree | good | see | not | consider\n21 Messages and follow-up emails writing | confirm | discussed | let | Thank\n22 Answer: Tell me about yourself Currently | Before | Through | What | believe\n23 Qualifications and experience experience | gained | used | prepared | relevant\n24 Strengths and development areas One | example | Colleagues | area | improving\n25 Build a STAR answer situation | responsibility | action | result | What\n26 Present achievements with evidence particularly | specific | improved | outcome | demonstrates\n27 Discuss challenges and lessons One | realised | took | address | Since\n28 Career goals and motivation motivated | role | next | like | opportunity\n29 Why should we hire you? role | experience | can | strong | these\n30 Ask intelligent interview questions tell | How | What | What | Thank\n31 Open a presentation confidently Good | Today | purpose | cover | Let\n32 Organise and connect ideas First | brings | contrast | key | sum\n33 Explain information and visuals visual | most | Compared | Approximately | What\n34 Handle audience questions Thank | understand | short | don | answer\n35 Final interview rehearsal important | relevant | result | connect | there\n36 Final showcase and next plan beginning | biggest | can | next | continue\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nCareer expression record\nRecord complete expressions you can use, not isolated words. Revisit each expression after 2 days, 7 days and 21 days.\nEXPRESSION MY TRUE EXAMPLE D2 D7 D21\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\n[ ] [ ] [ ]\nMy five rescue phrases\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi\n\n\n\nMy 30-day continuation plan\nONE CLEAR TARGET In 30 days, I will be able to ________________________________________________.\nWeekly rhythm\nWEEK SPEAKING TASK CAREER SITUATION EVIDENCE TO SAVE DATE\n1\n2\n3\n4\nPractice menu\nOne two-minute voice note using three course expressions.\nOne six-minute conversation with follow-up questions.\nOne work update, interview answer or presentation section.\nOne listen-and-shadow practice using a clear professional model.\nOne feedback-and-retry session focused on a single correction.\nProgress review\nThe evidence I will compare with my Week 1 baseline is...\nIf I miss a practice day, I will restart by...\nPersonal edition - Abdishakur Isse Hashi"}]},"career-fluency-abdisalan":{"title":"Career Fluency — Abdisalan Edition","lessons":[{"number":1,"title":"Your starting point","content":"WEEK 1 • LESSON 1\nYour starting point\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Complete a friendly speaking diagnostic and identify one clear fluency goal.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Where did you last use English?\n2. What can you already do well?\n3. What speaking situation feels difficult?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nLet me think for a moment. need time without becoming silent\nWhat I mean is… restate an idea more clearly\nCould you repeat the question? need to hear a question again\nIn my experience… connect an answer to real life\nOne goal I have is… state a learning or career goal\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTutor: Tell me a little about yourself and how you use English.\nStudent: In my experience, I use English at work, online and when I travel.\nTutor: What would you most like to improve?\nStudent: Let me think for a moment. One goal I have is to speak with fewer pauses.\nTutor: When do you feel most confident?\nStudent: I feel confident when I read or listen, but spontaneous speaking is harder.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Use complete simple sentences; accuracy is observed but not \ntaught today.\nSOUND CLEAR Speak at a natural volume and finish each thought clearly.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Let me think for a __________.\n2. What I __________ is…\n3. Could you __________ the question?\n4. In my __________…\n\n\n\n5. One __________ I have is…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask three diagnostic questions about work, daily life and future goals. Take brief notes.\nPARTNER B Answer each question for 30–60 seconds. Ask for repetition when needed.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes about who you are, how you use English and what you want \nto achieve in twelve weeks.\nSuccess means: I complete the task, use at least two rescue phrases and choose one measurable \ngoal.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Record a two-minute baseline voice note. Do not restart it. Write three things you \nwant to improve.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":2,"title":"A strong professional introduction","content":"WEEK 1 • LESSON 2\nA strong professional introduction\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Introduce yourself with a clear beginning, useful details and a forward-looking \nending.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which three facts belong in a professional introduction?\n2. What detail should you leave out?\n3. How can you end confidently?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI currently work as… state your present role\nMy background is in… describe education or professional field\nI’m responsible for… explain a main duty\nI’m particularly interested in… name a professional interest\nMy current goal is to… finish with a future direction\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nAmina: It’s good to meet you. What do you do?\nHassan: I currently work as a project assistant. My background is in construction planning.\nAmina: What are you responsible for?\nHassan: I’m responsible for checking schedules and preparing weekly updates.\nAmina: What would you like to do next?\nHassan: My current goal is to lead larger projects and communicate confidently with international teams.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple with be, have and work; because for reasons.\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the key words: name, role, field, skills and goal.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I __________ work as…\n2. My __________ is in…\n3. I’m __________ for…\n4. I’m __________ interested in…\n\n\n\n5. My __________ goal is to…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A You are meeting a new colleague at a regional conference. Ask about role, background \nand goals.\nPARTNER B Give a 60-second professional introduction, then ask your partner one question.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a 90-second introduction without reading full sentences.\nSuccess means: My introduction includes role, background, responsibility, interest and goal.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Record the introduction twice. Compare the recordings and note one improvement \nin fluency.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":3,"title":"Keep speaking without long pauses","content":"WEEK 1 • LESSON 3\nKeep speaking without long pauses\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Use thinking phrases, connectors and repair strategies to maintain your turn.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What topic can you discuss easily for one minute?\n2. Which connector do you use most?\n3. What do you say when a sentence goes wrong?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThere are two main reasons. organise an answer before giving details\nThe first point is… start your first idea\nAnother thing is… add a second idea\nFor example… support an idea with evidence\nLet me say that another way. repair an unclear sentence\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTutor: Why is English important for your career?\nStudent: There are two main reasons. The first point is communication at work.\nTutor: Can you give me an example?\nStudent: For example, I sometimes need to speak with people from different countries.\nTutor: And the second reason?\nStudent: Another thing is career growth. Let me say that another way: English can open more \nprofessional opportunities.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Join ideas with and, but, because and so.\nSOUND CLEAR Pause between idea groups, not after every word.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. There are two main __________.\n2. The __________ point is…\n3. __________ thing is…\n4. For __________…\n\n\n\n5. Let me say that __________ way.\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask ‘why’ and ‘how’ questions. Wait silently while your partner uses a thinking phrase.\nPARTNER B Answer each question with two points and one example.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes: ‘Why I am learning English now.’ Repeat the same talk with \nfewer pauses.\nSuccess means: I use two organising phrases, one example and one repair phrase instead of \nstopping.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Complete three one-minute speaking sprints: work, technology and travel. Save the \nfinal recording.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":4,"title":"Start conversations naturally","content":"WEEK 2 • LESSON 4\nStart conversations naturally\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Open a conversation in social and professional situations.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Where do you usually meet new people?\n2. Which opening question feels natural to you?\n3. What makes an opening question too personal?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nHow has your day been so far? open a relaxed conversation\nWhat brings you here? ask about purpose at an event\nHave we met before? check whether you know someone\nHow do you know the team? find a shared connection\nThat sounds interesting. respond positively before another question\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nSalma: Hi, have we met before?\nOmar: I don’t think so. I’m Omar from the planning team.\nSalma: Nice to meet you. What brings you here?\nOmar: I’m attending the digital skills workshop.\nSalma: That sounds interesting. How do you know the organiser?\nOmar: We worked together on a community project last year.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Wh- questions and present simple question order.\nSOUND CLEAR Use rising intonation for friendly opening questions.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. How has your day been so __________?\n2. What __________ you here?\n3. Have we met __________?\n4. How do you know the __________?\n5. That __________ interesting.\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Choose a situation: workshop, airport, online meeting or office reception. Start the \nconversation.\nPARTNER B Respond, add one detail and ask a question back.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Start three different two-minute conversations without using the same opening \ntwice.\nSuccess means: I greet, open, respond to the answer and move to a relevant follow-up question.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write five safe opening questions. Use two of them in a real or simulated \nconversation.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":5,"title":"Show interest and ask follow-ups","content":"WEEK 2 • LESSON 5\nShow interest and ask follow-ups\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Keep a conversation going by responding, echoing and asking connected \nquestions.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. How do you show interest in Somali?\n2. Which English reactions sound natural?\n3. Which question can follow almost any story?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nReally? What happened next? invite someone to continue a story\nHow did you get interested in that? ask about the origin of an interest\nWhat was that like? ask about an experience\nSo, you mean that…? check your understanding\nI can relate to that. connect the speaker’s experience to yours\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nAli: I recently started learning mapping software.\nNimo: Really? How did you get interested in that?\nAli: A colleague showed me how it can improve planning.\nNimo: What was that like at the beginning?\nAli: It was challenging, but I enjoyed it.\nNimo: I can relate to that. New software often feels difficult before it becomes useful.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Follow-up questions with how, why, when and what happened next.\nSOUND CLEAR Let your voice rise slightly to show real interest.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Really? What __________ next?\n2. How did you get __________ in that?\n3. What was that __________?\n4. So, you __________ that…?\n\n\n\n5. I can __________ to that.\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Tell a short story about learning a skill. Pause after each important detail.\nPARTNER B React first, then ask a connected follow-up. Do not change the topic suddenly.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Keep one conversation going for six exchanges using at least three follow-up \nexpressions.\nSuccess means: Every question connects to my partner’s previous answer.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Listen to a short interview or conversation. Write down three follow-up questions \nyou hear or could ask.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":6,"title":"End conversations professionally","content":"WEEK 2 • LESSON 6\nEnd conversations professionally\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Close conversations warmly, confirm next steps and leave a positive final \nimpression.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Why can ending a conversation feel difficult?\n2. What next step might you confirm?\n3. Which closing is formal enough for work?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nIt was great speaking with you. signal a warm ending\nI should let you get back to… respect the other person’s time\nLet’s stay in touch. suggest future contact\nI’ll send you the details. promise a next action\nI look forward to speaking again. close a professional interaction\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nMaryan: I should let you get back to the workshop.\nDavid: Of course. It was great speaking with you.\nMaryan: Let’s stay in touch. I’ll send you the project link this afternoon.\nDavid: Thank you. I’ll review it tomorrow.\nMaryan: Excellent. I look forward to speaking again.\nDavid: Likewise. Enjoy the rest of the event.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future arrangements with will and present continuous.\nSOUND CLEAR Use a calm falling tone for a clear, polite ending.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. It was great __________ with you.\n2. I should let you get __________ to…\n3. Let’s stay in __________.\n4. I’ll send you the __________.\n\n\n\n5. I look __________ to speaking again.\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Continue a conversation for two minutes. Then give a time signal so your partner can \nclose it.\nPARTNER B Notice the signal, close politely and confirm one next action.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Open, maintain and close a complete four-minute conversation.\nSuccess means: My closing is polite, natural and includes a clear next step when appropriate.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Send a 45-second voice message that ends a professional conversation and \nconfirms a follow-up.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":7,"title":"Describe your work clearly","content":"WEEK 3 • LESSON 7\nDescribe your work clearly\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Explain your role, team and responsibilities to someone outside your field.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. How would you explain your job to a 12-year-old?\n2. Who depends on your work?\n3. What result does your role produce?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nMy main responsibility is… name your most important duty\nI work closely with… describe collaboration\nA typical task involves… explain a regular activity\nThe purpose of my role is to… explain why your job exists\nIn simple terms… make technical information accessible\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nGuest: What exactly do you do?\nPlanner: In simple terms, I help the team prepare accurate project plans.\nGuest: What does a typical task involve?\nPlanner: A typical task involves reviewing information, checking locations and updating schedules.\nGuest: Who do you work with?\nPlanner: I work closely with technical staff, managers and field teams.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple for regular duties; noun phrases for roles and \ndepartments.\nSOUND CLEAR Mark word stress in responsibility, department, technical and coordinate.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. My main __________ is…\n2. I work __________ with…\n3. A typical task __________…\n4. The __________ of my role is to…\n\n\n\n5. In __________ terms…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A You know nothing about your partner’s job. Ask for simple explanations and examples.\nPARTNER B Explain your role without unexplained technical words.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute ‘My work in simple terms’ explanation.\nSuccess means: A listener outside my field can understand my role, main duty, collaborators and \nresult.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Create a personal job vocabulary map with ten words. Record five sentences using \nthe most useful words.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":8,"title":"Routines and current priorities","content":"WEEK 3 • LESSON 8\nRoutines and current priorities\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Compare regular responsibilities with what you are working on now.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which duties happen every week?\n2. What is different this week?\n3. How do you decide your top priority?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI usually spend my mornings… describe a normal routine\nAt the moment, I’m working on… name a current project\nMost weeks, I… describe a frequent pattern\nThis week is different because… contrast routine and present\nMy top priority today is… identify the most urgent task\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTutor: What does a normal week look like for you?\nStudent: Most weeks, I review plans and coordinate with colleagues.\nTutor: What are you working on at the moment?\nStudent: At the moment, I’m preparing an update for a new project.\nTutor: Is this week typical?\nStudent: This week is different because we have an important deadline on Thursday.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple versus present continuous; adverbs of frequency.\nSOUND CLEAR Pronounce final -s clearly in works, checks, plans and manages.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I __________ spend my mornings…\n2. At the __________, I’m working on…\n3. Most __________, I…\n4. This week is __________ because…\n5. My top __________ today is…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask about normal routines, current work and today’s priority.\nPARTNER B Contrast at least two routine duties with one current project.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Speak for two minutes about ‘My normal work and my current priority.’\nSuccess means: I use present simple for routine and present continuous for current action.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Keep a one-day English work log. Write five routine statements and three current￾action statements.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":9,"title":"Give a useful work update","content":"WEEK 3 • LESSON 9\nGive a useful work update\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Report progress, completed actions, current work and next steps.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What makes an update useful?\n2. Which detail should come first?\n3. How can you report a problem without sounding negative?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nSo far, I’ve completed… report completed progress\nI’m currently checking… describe work in progress\nThe main issue is… identify a blocker\nI expect to finish by… give a realistic deadline\nMy next step is to… state the immediate action\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nManager: Can you give me a quick update?\nEmployee: So far, I’ve completed the first review. I’m currently checking the final details.\nManager: Is anything blocking the work?\nEmployee: The main issue is one missing document, but I expect to receive it today.\nManager: What happens next?\nEmployee: My next step is to update the plan and send it by 3 p.m.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present perfect for progress; present continuous for current work; \nwill for next action.\nSOUND CLEAR Link contractions smoothly: I’ve completed, we’re checking, I’ll send.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. So far, I’ve __________…\n2. I’m __________ checking…\n3. The main __________ is…\n4. I __________ to finish by…\n\n\n\n5. My next __________ is to…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A You are a manager. Ask about progress, current work, blockers, deadline and next \nstep.\nPARTNER B Give a concise 60-second update using the five-part structure.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a work update in one minute, then answer two follow-up questions.\nSuccess means: My listener knows what is done, happening now, blocked, due and next.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Record two updates: a 30-second version and a 60-second version. Keep the \nclearest one.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n\n\nMODULE 2\nWORKPLACE & INTERNATIONAL\nENGLISH\nUse English for experiences, international communication, problems,\nmeetings and professional messages.\n10. Tell a past experience\n11. Sequence a professional story\n12. Describe success and challenge\n13. Meet international contacts\n14. Clarify and confirm meaning\n15. Phone and video calls\n16. Describe a workplace problem\n17. Suggest and compare solutions\n18. Explain a process step by step\n19. Share opinions in meetings\n20. Agree and disagree politely\n21. Messages and follow-up emails\n\n"},{"number":10,"title":"Tell a past experience","content":"WEEK 4 • LESSON 10\nTell a past experience\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Describe a past professional or personal experience with clear time \nreferences.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which recent experience helped you grow?\n2. What exactly did you do?\n3. What did the experience teach you?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nA few months ago… place an experience in time\nI had the opportunity to… introduce a valuable experience\nMy role was to… state your responsibility then\nThe most useful part was… highlight value\nThat experience taught me… state a lesson\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a useful learning experience.\nCandidate: A few months ago, I had the opportunity to join a short technical workshop.\nInterviewer: What was your role?\nCandidate: My role was to complete the practical tasks and support another participant.\nInterviewer: What did you learn?\nCandidate: The most useful part was solving a real problem. That experience taught me to ask clearer \nquestions.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple: regular and common irregular verbs.\nSOUND CLEAR Practise -ed endings in worked, planned, needed and completed.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. A few __________ ago…\n2. I had the __________ to…\n3. My __________ was to…\n\n\n\n4. The most __________ part was…\n5. That experience __________ me…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask for the time, situation, role, action and lesson.\nPARTNER B Tell one past experience in chronological order.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Tell a two-minute story about learning, work, travel or teamwork.\nSuccess means: My listener can identify when it happened, what I did and what I learned.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write six past-tense verbs from your story. Record the story again with clear \nendings.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":11,"title":"Sequence a professional story","content":"WEEK 4 • LESSON 11\nSequence a professional story\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Organise events so a listener can follow what happened from beginning to \nend.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What happens when a story has no sequence markers?\n2. Which action was happening in the background?\n3. What result ended your story?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nAt first… describe the starting situation\nWhile I was…, … show two actions happening together\nAfter that… move to the next event\nAs a result… connect an action to its effect\nIn the end… give the final outcome\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nColleague: How did you solve the scheduling problem?\nEmployee: At first, we did not know which information was correct.\nColleague: What did you do?\nEmployee: While I was checking the records, my colleague contacted the field team. After that, we \ncompared both sources.\nColleague: Did that work?\nEmployee: Yes. As a result, we corrected the schedule. In the end, the team finished on time.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple and past continuous; sequence markers.\nSOUND CLEAR Pause briefly after first, while, after that and finally.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. At __________…\n2. __________ I was…, …\n3. __________ that…\n\n\n\n4. As a __________…\n5. In the __________…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Listen and draw a four-step timeline. Ask questions if an event is unclear.\nPARTNER B Tell a story with a clear beginning, middle, result and ending.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Retell one professional story in three minutes using all five sequence expressions.\nSuccess means: My story is easy to follow and each event leads naturally to the next.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Create a five-box timeline for your story. Practise speaking from the timeline, not a \nfull script.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":12,"title":"Describe success and challenge","content":"WEEK 4 • LESSON 12\nDescribe success and challenge\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Explain what went well, what was difficult and how you responded.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What challenge can you discuss professionally?\n2. What action did you take?\n3. What would you improve next time?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThe main challenge was… identify the difficult part\nAlthough it was difficult, … show a positive contrast\nWe decided to… describe a chosen response\nWhat worked well was… identify a success factor\nIf I did it again, I would… reflect on improvement\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTutor: Tell me about a challenging task.\nStudent: The main challenge was a very short deadline.\nTutor: How did you respond?\nStudent: Although it was difficult, we divided the work clearly. We decided to check progress every \nafternoon.\nTutor: What worked well?\nStudent: What worked well was the communication. If I did it again, I would confirm responsibilities \nearlier.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION because, so, although and however for cause and contrast.\nSOUND CLEAR Stress contrast words to make the message clear.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. The main __________ was…\n2. __________ it was difficult, …\n3. We __________ to…\n\n\n\n4. What __________ well was…\n5. If I did it __________, I would…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask neutral questions about challenge, response, result and reflection.\nPARTNER B Describe a challenge without blaming another person.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Give a balanced two-minute account of a challenge and successful response.\nSuccess means: I explain the problem, action, positive result and lesson in a professional tone.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write a six-sentence reflection using because, so, although and however.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":13,"title":"Meet international contacts","content":"WEEK 5 • LESSON 13\nMeet international contacts\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Introduce yourself, find common ground and adapt your language for a new \ninternational contact.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What information is safe to share in a first meeting?\n2. How can you find common ground?\n3. How do you show respect for a different perspective?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nWhere are you joining from? open an online international meeting\nWhat kind of work do you do? ask about a professional field\nHow long have you worked in that area? ask about experience\nWe seem to have that in common. identify shared experience\nIt’s useful to hear your perspective. value another viewpoint\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nLayla: Hello, where are you joining from?\nDaniel: I’m joining from Nairobi. What kind of work do you do?\nLayla: I work in project planning. I’ve worked in that area for three years.\nDaniel: Interesting. I also support infrastructure projects.\nLayla: We seem to have that in common. It’s useful to hear your perspective.\nDaniel: Likewise. I’d like to learn more about your current project.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple and present perfect for background and experience.\nSOUND CLEAR Say names, countries and organisations slowly and clearly.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Where are you __________ from?\n2. What __________ of work do you do?\n3. How __________ have you worked in that area?\n4. We seem to have that in __________.\n\n\n\n5. It’s useful to hear your __________.\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Choose a fictional country and professional role. Ask about location, work and \nexperience.\nPARTNER B Introduce your real background and identify one shared interest.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Complete a five-minute international networking conversation.\nSuccess means: I speak clearly, avoid local-only references and ask respectful follow-up questions.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Prepare a 60-second introduction for an international online meeting. Record it with \nclear names and places.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":14,"title":"Clarify and confirm meaning","content":"WEEK 5 • LESSON 14\nClarify and confirm meaning\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Prevent misunderstandings by checking, paraphrasing and confirming \nimportant information.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which work details must always be confirmed?\n2. What is the difference between clarifying and confirming?\n3. How can you ask without sounding rude?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nCould you clarify what you mean by…? ask for a clearer explanation\nIf I understand correctly, … paraphrase the message\nDo you mean…? check one interpretation\nLet me confirm the details. review important information\nCould you give me an example? request concrete evidence\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nClient: We need the revised plan early next week.\nEmployee: Could you clarify what you mean by early next week?\nClient: Tuesday morning, before ten.\nEmployee: If I understand correctly, you need the final version by 10 a.m. on Tuesday.\nClient: That’s right.\nEmployee: Let me confirm the details in an email after this call.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect questions and checking structures.\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the word that needs correction or confirmation.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Could you __________ what you mean by…?\n2. If I understand __________, …\n3. Do you __________…?\n4. Let me __________ the details.\n\n\n\n5. Could you give me an __________?\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Give instructions containing one vague time, term or quantity.\nPARTNER B Ask two clarification questions, paraphrase and confirm the final details.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Resolve three misunderstandings in a role-play without switching languages.\nSuccess means: I clarify politely and repeat the final meaning accurately.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write five clarification questions connected to your work. Use two in a recorded \nrole-play.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":15,"title":"Phone and video calls","content":"WEEK 5 • LESSON 15\nPhone and video calls\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Open, manage and close remote conversations when sound or connection is \nimperfect.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What call problems happen most often?\n2. How do you check a number accurately?\n3. What should happen before a call ends?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nCan you hear me clearly? check the audio connection\nYou’re breaking up a little. report an unstable connection\nCould you say that again more slowly? request a clearer repetition\nLet me share my screen. move to visual information\nI’ll summarise the next steps. close with an action review\n\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nHost: Good morning. Can you hear me clearly?\nGuest: Mostly, but you’re breaking up a little.\nHost: Thanks for telling me. Is this better?\nGuest: Yes. Could you say the project code again more slowly?\nHost: Certainly: B–4–7–2. Let me share my screen as well.\nGuest: Perfect. At the end, I’ll summarise the next steps.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Can, could and would for requests; present continuous for \nconnection problems.\nSOUND CLEAR Practise letters, numbers, dates and spelling in short groups.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Can you hear me __________?\n2. You’re __________ up a little.\n3. Could you say that again more __________?\n\n\n\n4. Let me __________ my screen.\n5. I’ll __________ the next steps.\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Read a short project code, date and email address. Create one connection problem.\nPARTNER B Manage the problem, confirm the details and summarise the next action.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Complete a four-minute audio-only work call.\nSuccess means: I manage the opening, repair one communication problem and close with \nconfirmed steps.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Record a simulated phone call. Include a spelling check, a number and a clear \nclosing summary.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":16,"title":"Describe a workplace problem","content":"WEEK 6 • LESSON 16\nDescribe a workplace problem\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Explain a problem objectively, including its impact and urgency.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What facts belong in a problem report?\n2. How is impact different from cause?\n3. Why should you avoid guessing?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nWe’ve noticed an issue with… introduce a problem neutrally\nThe problem started when… give the origin\nThis is affecting… explain impact\nThe most urgent concern is… identify priority\nWhat we know so far is… separate facts from guesses\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nManager: What seems to be the problem?\nEmployee: We’ve noticed an issue with the latest schedule.\nManager: When did it start?\nEmployee: The problem started when one location was changed. This is affecting two field visits.\nManager: What is the most urgent concern?\nEmployee: The most urgent concern is tomorrow’s visit. What we know so far is that the team has not \nreceived the update.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION There is/are, present perfect and passive forms for current \nproblems.\nSOUND CLEAR Use clear final consonants in stopped, missed, changed and delayed.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. We’ve __________ an issue with…\n2. The problem __________ when…\n3. This is __________…\n\n\n\n4. The most __________ concern is…\n5. What we __________ so far is…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Give a scenario with a missing document, delayed task or incorrect detail. Ask for facts.\nPARTNER B Report the problem, cause, impact, urgency and known facts.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Deliver a 90-second problem report followed by three questions.\nSuccess means: I am factual, specific and clear about urgency without blaming people.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write a short problem report with five headings: issue, start, impact, urgency and \nknown facts.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":17,"title":"Suggest and compare solutions","content":"WEEK 6 • LESSON 17\nSuggest and compare solutions\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Offer options, explain advantages and recommend a practical response.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What makes a recommendation convincing?\n2. How many options should you compare?\n3. Which matters more: speed, cost or quality?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nOne option would be to… introduce a possible solution\nAnother possibility is… add an alternative\nThe advantage of this option is… explain a benefit\nThe main risk is… identify a disadvantage\nI’d recommend… because… make a reasoned choice\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTeam lead: How can we handle the delay?\nEmployee: One option would be to move the visit to Thursday.\nTeam lead: Is there another possibility?\nEmployee: Another possibility is to send a different team. The advantage is that we keep the original \ndate, but the main risk is limited preparation.\nTeam lead: Which do you prefer?\nEmployee: I’d recommend Thursday because the original team already knows the site.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Should, could and might; comparatives for options.\nSOUND CLEAR Reduce modals naturally: we could, we should, it might.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. One __________ would be to…\n2. Another __________ is…\n3. The __________ of this option is…\n4. The main __________ is…\n\n\n\n5. I’d __________… because…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Present a simple workplace problem and ask for two options.\nPARTNER B Offer two solutions, compare benefits and risks, then recommend one.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Lead a three-minute solution discussion and reach a decision.\nSuccess means: My recommendation follows a fair comparison and includes a clear reason.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Choose one everyday problem. Record two options, one risk and your \nrecommendation.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":18,"title":"Explain a process step by step","content":"WEEK 6 • LESSON 18\nExplain a process step by step\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Make a work or daily process easy to follow for a new person.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which process do you know well?\n2. Where do beginners usually get confused?\n3. Why does each main step matter?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThe process begins with… introduce the first stage\nOnce that is complete, … show a required sequence\nThe next step is to… move forward\nBefore we finish, we need to… name a final check\nThe purpose of this step is… explain why a step matters\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nNew colleague: How do we prepare the weekly update?\nEmployee: The process begins with collecting information from each team.\nNew colleague: What happens next?\nEmployee: Once that is complete, we check the dates and progress. The next step is to write a short \nsummary.\nNew colleague: Is there a final check?\nEmployee: Yes. Before we finish, we need to confirm the numbers. The purpose of this step is to prevent\nreporting errors.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Sequencing; imperatives and present simple passive where useful.\nSOUND CLEAR Pause after each step and stress action verbs.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. The process __________ with…\n2. __________ that is complete, …\n3. The __________ step is to…\n\n\n\n4. __________ we finish, we need to…\n5. The __________ of this step is…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Act as a new colleague. Interrupt only when a step, order or purpose is unclear.\nPARTNER B Explain a four- to six-step process without reading full sentences.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Teach your partner one process and answer clarification questions.\nSuccess means: My explanation has a clear sequence, action verbs and at least one reason.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Draw a six-step process map. Record the explanation using only the map as notes.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":19,"title":"Share opinions in meetings","content":"WEEK 7 • LESSON 19\nShare opinions in meetings\nTODAY’S OUTCOME State an opinion, support it and invite another person to respond.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which meeting topics require opinions?\n2. What evidence can support an opinion?\n3. How do you invite quieter participants?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nFrom my point of view… state a considered opinion\nThe main reason is… support the opinion\nFor instance… add evidence\nThat’s how I see it. close your explanation\nWhat’s your view? invite another speaker\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nChair: Should we change the weekly meeting time?\nMember: From my point of view, we should move it to the morning.\nChair: Why?\nMember: The main reason is that field visits often happen later. For instance, two people missed last \nweek’s meeting.\nChair: Anything else?\nMember: Morning would improve attendance. That’s how I see it. What’s your view?\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Opinion clauses and because for reasons.\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the main opinion word and the strongest reason.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. From my point of __________…\n2. The main __________ is…\n3. For __________…\n4. That’s how I __________ it.\n\n\n\n5. What’s __________ view?\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Give a meeting topic and listen without interrupting. Ask for a reason and example.\nPARTNER B State an opinion, reason and example, then invite your partner’s view.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Contribute twice to a five-minute mini-meeting.\nSuccess means: Each contribution has one clear point, supporting reason and invitation to \nrespond.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Record a 90-second opinion about remote work, professional training or technology.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":20,"title":"Agree and disagree politely","content":"WEEK 7 • LESSON 20\nAgree and disagree politely\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Respond to ideas with respect while adding, qualifying or challenging a point.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Why do people sometimes avoid disagreement?\n2. Which phrase softens a challenge?\n3. How can disagreement improve a decision?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI agree with that because… give supported agreement\nThat’s a good point. I’d add… build on an idea\nI see your point; however… disagree respectfully\nI’m not completely convinced that… express careful doubt\nCould we consider… instead? offer an alternative\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nColleague A: I think every update should be a long written report.\nColleague B: I see your point; however, long reports take time to read.\nColleague A: What would you suggest?\nColleague B: Could we consider a short dashboard instead?\nColleague A: That’s a good point. I’d add a monthly detailed report.\nColleague B: I agree with that because it gives us both speed and detail.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Contrast with but, however and while; comparatives.\nSOUND CLEAR Use a warm tone when disagreeing; stress the idea, not the person.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I __________ with that because…\n2. That’s a good point. I’d __________…\n3. I see your point; __________…\n4. I’m not completely __________ that…\n5. Could we consider… __________?\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Defend one option: written report, dashboard or verbal update.\nPARTNER B Agree with one part, disagree with another and offer an alternative.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Reach agreement after a four-minute respectful disagreement.\nSuccess means: I respond to the idea, give a reason and avoid personal language.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write three polite disagreements. Read them aloud twice: neutral tone, then warmer\ntone.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":21,"title":"Messages and follow-up emails","content":"WEEK 7 • LESSON 21\nMessages and follow-up emails\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Write and speak concise professional requests, updates and follow-ups.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. When should you send a follow-up?\n2. What belongs in the subject line?\n3. How can you make the requested action clear?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI’m writing to follow up on… reopen an earlier topic\nCould you please confirm…? request important information\nAs discussed, … refer to an agreed point\nPlease let me know if… invite a response or question\nThank you for your assistance. close a professional request\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTutor: What should your follow-up message include?\nStudent: I’m writing to follow up on yesterday’s meeting.\nTutor: What information do you need?\nStudent: Could you please confirm the final deadline? As discussed, I’ll prepare the first draft.\nTutor: How will you close?\nStudent: Please let me know if you need anything else. Thank you for your assistance.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Polite requests with could/would; present perfect for follow-up.\nSOUND CLEAR Read messages aloud in meaningful chunks, not word by word.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I’m writing to __________ up on…\n2. Could you please __________…?\n3. As __________, …\n4. Please let me __________ if…\n5. Thank you for your __________.\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Explain a meeting decision and one missing detail.\nPARTNER B Give an oral summary, then draft a 70-word follow-up message.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Read your message aloud; your partner identifies purpose, action and deadline.\nSuccess means: My message is polite, concise and makes the next action unmistakable.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write a 100–120-word professional email requesting confirmation of a deadline or \nmeeting.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n\n\nMODULE 3\nINTERVIEW & CAREER\nCOMMUNICATION\nTurn qualifications and experience into clear, evidence-based\ninterview answers.\n22. Answer: Tell me about yourself\n23. Qualifications and experience\n24. Strengths and development areas\n25. Build a STAR answer\n26. Present achievements with evidence\n27. Discuss challenges and lessons\n28. Career goals and motivation\n29. Why should we hire you?\n30. Ask intelligent interview questions\n\n"},{"number":22,"title":"Answer: Tell me about yourself","content":"WEEK 8 • LESSON 22\nAnswer: Tell me about yourself\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Give a focused interview introduction connecting present work, relevant \nbackground and future value.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What information is relevant to the job?\n2. How is this answer different from a personal biography?\n3. What value can you offer?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nCurrently, I… begin with present professional activity\nBefore that, I… add relevant background\nThrough that experience, I developed… name transferable skills\nWhat interests me about this opportunity is… connect to the role\nI believe I can contribute by… state potential value\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Tell me about yourself.\nCandidate: Currently, I work in project support, where I help coordinate plans and updates.\nCandidate: Before that, I studied architecture and completed several team projects.\nCandidate: Through that experience, I developed planning, communication and problem-solving skills.\nCandidate: What interests me about this opportunity is the international project work.\nCandidate: I believe I can contribute by combining technical understanding with careful coordination.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present–past–future structure; present simple and past simple.\nSOUND CLEAR Begin strongly and avoid a rising, uncertain ending.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. __________, I…\n2. __________ that, I…\n3. Through that experience, I __________…\n4. What __________ me about this opportunity is…\n\n\n\n5. I believe I can __________ by…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Act as interviewer. Ask the main question and two follow-ups about experience and \nvalue.\nPARTNER B Answer in 60–90 seconds using present, past and future.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Deliver the answer twice: first with notes, then with five keywords only.\nSuccess means: My answer is relevant, structured and connected to the opportunity.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write five keywords for your answer. Record a 90-second version without a full \nscript.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":23,"title":"Qualifications and experience","content":"WEEK 8 • LESSON 23\nQualifications and experience\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Connect education, training and experience directly to a job requirement.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which qualification best matches your target role?\n2. What evidence proves you can use a skill?\n3. When should you use past simple?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI have experience in… summarise relevant experience\nI gained this experience while… locate where learning happened\nI have used… for… show duration or purpose\nThis prepared me to… connect experience to readiness\nA relevant example is… provide evidence\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: What relevant experience do you have?\nCandidate: I have experience in planning, digital tools and team projects.\nInterviewer: Where did you gain it?\nCandidate: I gained this experience while studying and supporting practical projects.\nInterviewer: How does it prepare you for this role?\nCandidate: This prepared me to organise information carefully. A relevant example is a project where I \ncoordinated tasks for a small team.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present perfect versus past simple; for and since.\nSOUND CLEAR Pronounce qualifications, experience, certificate and architecture clearly.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I have __________ in…\n2. I __________ this experience while…\n3. I have __________… for…\n4. This __________ me to…\n\n\n\n5. A __________ example is…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Read three fictional job requirements and ask for evidence for each.\nPARTNER B Match two real qualifications or experiences to the requirements.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Answer: ‘What relevant experience do you have?’ with two pieces of evidence.\nSuccess means: I do more than list qualifications; I explain relevance and evidence.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Choose a real job advertisement. Highlight three requirements and prepare one \nevidence sentence for each.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":24,"title":"Strengths and development areas","content":"WEEK 8 • LESSON 24\nStrengths and development areas\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Describe strengths with evidence and discuss improvement honestly and \nconstructively.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which strengths matter for your target career?\n2. What evidence can you give?\n3. Which development area is honest but manageable?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nOne of my strengths is… name a relevant strength\nFor example, I… prove the strength\nColleagues often rely on me to… show external evidence\nAn area I’m developing is… name a manageable improvement area\nI’m improving it by… show active development\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: What is one of your strengths?\nCandidate: One of my strengths is careful planning. For example, I break large tasks into clear stages.\nInterviewer: How do others see that strength?\nCandidate: Colleagues often rely on me to check details before a deadline.\nInterviewer: What are you currently improving?\nCandidate: An area I’m developing is public speaking. I’m improving it by practising short presentations \nevery week.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Adjective–noun collocations; can and have improved.\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the strength and the evidence, not every word.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. One of my __________ is…\n2. For __________, I…\n3. Colleagues often __________ on me to…\n\n\n\n4. An area I’m __________ is…\n5. I’m __________ it by…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask for one strength, proof, one development area and current action.\nPARTNER B Give a balanced answer without using clichés such as ‘I work too hard.’\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Answer two interview questions: one strength and one development area.\nSuccess means: Every claim includes evidence or a concrete improvement action.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Create a strength-evidence-action chart for three strengths and one development \narea.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":25,"title":"Build a STAR answer","content":"WEEK 9 • LESSON 25\nBuild a STAR answer\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Use Situation, Task, Action and Result to answer behavioural interview \nquestions.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What does each STAR letter mean?\n2. Which stage should usually be longest?\n3. Why should you say ‘I’ when describing your action?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThe situation was… set the context briefly\nMy responsibility was to… state the task\nThe action I took was… focus on your contribution\nAs a result, … show the outcome\nWhat I learned was… add reflection when useful\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a time you solved a problem.\nCandidate: The situation was a project with inconsistent information.\nCandidate: My responsibility was to identify the correct details before the deadline.\nCandidate: The action I took was to compare the records and contact the field team.\nCandidate: As a result, we corrected the plan on time.\nCandidate: What I learned was to confirm critical information early.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past simple and past continuous; sequence and result connectors.\nSOUND CLEAR Pause clearly between the four STAR stages.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. The __________ was…\n2. My __________ was to…\n3. The __________ I took was…\n4. As a __________, …\n\n\n\n5. What I __________ was…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask one behavioural question and signal if any STAR stage is missing.\nPARTNER B Answer using four short sections; focus most on Action.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute STAR answer about problem-solving or teamwork.\nSuccess means: My answer contains all four stages and makes my personal action clear.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Complete a STAR planner for a real experience. Record the answer using only four \nheadings.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":26,"title":"Present achievements with evidence","content":"WEEK 9 • LESSON 26\nPresent achievements with evidence\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Describe an achievement using specific actions, measurable results and \nrelevant learning.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which achievement is relevant to your target role?\n2. What was your personal contribution?\n3. Can you express the result with a number or comparison?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI’m particularly proud of… introduce a meaningful achievement\nMy specific contribution was… separate your role from the team’s\nWe improved… by… quantify positive change\nThe outcome was… state the final result\nThis demonstrates my ability to… connect achievement to a competency\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Which achievement are you most proud of?\nCandidate: I’m particularly proud of completing a team project before its deadline.\nInterviewer: What was your contribution?\nCandidate: My specific contribution was organising the task schedule and checking progress.\nInterviewer: What was the outcome?\nCandidate: The outcome was an early submission with fewer errors. This demonstrates my ability to \ncoordinate work carefully.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Result language, comparatives and quantifiers.\nSOUND CLEAR Say numbers, dates and percentages slowly enough to be understood.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I’m particularly __________ of…\n2. My __________ contribution was…\n3. We __________… by…\n\n\n\n4. The __________ was…\n5. This __________ my ability to…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask for achievement, contribution, evidence and relevance.\nPARTNER B Present one achievement and answer follow-up questions with specific details.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Give a 90-second achievement answer containing one measurable or observable \nresult.\nSuccess means: My answer proves impact instead of only saying the work was successful.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write three result statements using a number, time saved, quality improvement or \ncompleted output.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":27,"title":"Discuss challenges and lessons","content":"WEEK 9 • LESSON 27\nDiscuss challenges and lessons\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Take responsibility, explain a constructive response and show learning.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What type of challenge is safe and relevant to discuss?\n2. How do you show responsibility?\n3. What lasting change proves learning?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nOne difficulty I encountered was… introduce a professional challenge\nI realised that… show awareness\nI took responsibility for… explain ownership\nTo address it, I… describe corrective action\nSince then, I have… show lasting improvement\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Tell me about a mistake or difficult situation.\nCandidate: One difficulty I encountered was underestimating how long a task would take.\nCandidate: I realised that I had not allowed enough time for checking.\nCandidate: I took responsibility for informing the team early.\nCandidate: To address it, I revised the schedule and asked for feedback.\nCandidate: Since then, I have added review time to every project plan.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Past forms; if + present for future response.\nSOUND CLEAR Use a calm, responsible tone and avoid defensive stress.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. One difficulty I __________ was…\n2. I __________ that…\n3. I took __________ for…\n4. To __________ it, I…\n5. __________ then, I have…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask about challenge, awareness, responsibility, action and lasting improvement.\nPARTNER B Discuss a genuine but manageable challenge without blaming other people.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute lesson-learned answer using all five stages.\nSuccess means: I show honesty, action and improvement; the answer ends positively.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Write a before-and-after reflection: what I did before, what happened and what I do \nnow.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":28,"title":"Career goals and motivation","content":"WEEK 10 • LESSON 28\nCareer goals and motivation\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Explain why a role fits your interests, skills and realistic future direction.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What genuinely motivates you?\n2. Which part of the role fits your background?\n3. What future goal sounds realistic?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nI’m motivated by… explain a genuine source of energy\nThis role appeals to me because… connect motivation to the opportunity\nIn the next few years, I hope to… state a realistic direction\nI’d like to develop… identify a growth goal\nThis opportunity would allow me to… connect the role to progress\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Why are you interested in this role?\nCandidate: I’m motivated by projects that improve how cities and services work.\nCandidate: This role appeals to me because it combines planning, technology and teamwork.\nInterviewer: Where do you see yourself in a few years?\nCandidate: I hope to take greater responsibility and develop stronger project leadership skills.\nCandidate: This opportunity would allow me to learn from an experienced international team.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future forms: want to, hope to, plan to and would like to.\nSOUND CLEAR Use contractions naturally but make key career words clear.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. I’m __________ by…\n2. This role __________ to me because…\n3. In the next few years, I __________ to…\n4. I’d like to __________…\n5. This opportunity would __________ me to…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask why this role, why this organisation and what next.\nPARTNER B Connect personal motivation to actual job responsibilities.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Give a two-minute motivation and career-goal answer.\nSuccess means: My answer is specific to the role and does not sound memorised or exaggerated.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Research one organisation. Prepare three honest reasons you could be interested \nin its work.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":29,"title":"Why should we hire you?","content":"WEEK 10 • LESSON 29\nWhy should we hire you?\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Make a concise value argument using requirement, evidence and contribution.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What are the employer’s top three needs?\n2. Which evidence supports each need?\n3. What value can you contribute immediately?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThis role requires… identify the employer’s need\nMy experience has prepared me to… connect background to performance\nI can bring… state a useful contribution\nA strong example is… support the claim\nFor these reasons, I believe… close the argument\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Why should we hire you?\nCandidate: This role requires careful planning, communication and reliable follow-up.\nCandidate: My experience has prepared me to organise information and coordinate tasks.\nCandidate: I can bring a calm approach and strong attention to detail.\nCandidate: A strong example is a recent project that I helped complete ahead of schedule.\nCandidate: For these reasons, I believe I can contribute effectively from the beginning.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Because, therefore and can to connect evidence to value.\nSOUND CLEAR Stress the three key value words in your answer.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. This role __________…\n2. My experience has __________ me to…\n3. I can __________…\n4. A __________ example is…\n5. For these __________, I believe…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Read a fictional job description and challenge any unsupported claim.\nPARTNER B Give a 60-second value argument with one strong example.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Answer ‘Why should we hire you?’ and respond to two evidence questions.\nSuccess means: My claims match the role and are supported by believable evidence.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Create a three-column note: requirement, my evidence, my contribution. Record the\nfinal answer.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":30,"title":"Ask intelligent interview questions","content":"WEEK 10 • LESSON 30\nAsk intelligent interview questions\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Ask relevant questions, respond to answers and close an interview \nprofessionally.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which questions show preparation?\n2. Which questions can be answered from the website?\n3. How many questions should you prepare?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nCould you tell me more about…? request detail about the role\nHow would you describe…? ask about team or culture\nWhat would success look like in…? understand expectations\nWhat are the next steps in the process? ask about the recruitment timeline\nThank you for the opportunity to speak with you. close the interview\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: Do you have any questions for us?\nCandidate: Yes. What would success look like in the first three months?\nInterviewer: We would expect the person to understand the team and manage one small project.\nCandidate: That’s helpful. Could you tell me more about how the team works together?\nInterviewer: We have a weekly planning meeting and daily online updates.\nCandidate: Thank you. What are the next steps in the process?\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect and open questions; would/could for polite enquiry.\nSOUND CLEAR Use falling intonation for open questions and clear closing statements.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Could you tell me __________ about…?\n2. How would you __________…?\n3. What would __________ look like in…?\n4. What are the next __________ in the process?\n\n\n\n5. Thank you for the __________ to speak with you.\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Answer as an interviewer using details from a fictional organisation.\nPARTNER B Ask three prepared questions and one natural follow-up.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Complete the final five minutes of a mock interview, including a professional closing.\nSuccess means: My questions are relevant, open and connected to the role.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Prepare five questions for a real target role. Rank them from most to least useful.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n\n\nMODULE 4\nPRESENTATION & MASTERY\nPresent ideas, handle questions and demonstrate measurable\nprogress.\n31. Open a presentation confidently\n32. Organise and connect ideas\n33. Explain information and visuals\n34. Handle audience questions\n35. Final interview rehearsal\n36. Final showcase and next plan\n\n"},{"number":31,"title":"Open a presentation confidently","content":"WEEK 11 • LESSON 31\nOpen a presentation confidently\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Gain attention, state purpose and preview a short professional presentation.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What does an audience need in the first 30 seconds?\n2. How many main points suit a five-minute talk?\n3. What makes an opening sound confident?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\n\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nGood morning, and thank you for joining. open formally and warmly\nToday I’d like to talk about… state the topic\nThe purpose of this presentation is… state the objective\nI’ll cover three main points. preview the structure\nLet’s begin with… move into the first point\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nPresenter: Good morning, and thank you for joining.\nPresenter: Today I’d like to talk about improving our weekly project updates.\nPresenter: The purpose of this presentation is to recommend a clearer reporting process.\nPresenter: I’ll cover three main points: the current issue, two options and my recommendation.\nPresenter: The talk will take about five minutes.\nPresenter: Let’s begin with the current issue.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Present simple for purpose and overview; will for preview.\nSOUND CLEAR Pause after the greeting, title and roadmap.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Good morning, and thank you for __________.\n2. __________ I’d like to talk about…\n3. The __________ of this presentation is…\n4. I’ll __________ three main points.\n5. Let’s __________ with…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Listen with your notes closed. After the opening, state the topic, purpose and roadmap \nyou heard.\nPARTNER B Deliver a 30-second opening and adjust it after feedback.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Open a five-minute professional presentation without reading full sentences.\nSuccess means: My audience knows the topic, purpose, structure and approximate length.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Record three versions of the same 30-second opening. Choose the clearest one.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":32,"title":"Organise and connect ideas","content":"WEEK 11 • LESSON 32\nOrganise and connect ideas\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Build a clear beginning, middle and end using signposting language.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What is the difference between a point and an example?\n2. Which signpost marks contrast?\n3. How should a conclusion connect to the purpose?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nFirst, let’s look at… open the first section\nThis brings me to my second point. signal a transition\nIn contrast… show an important difference\nThe key message here is… highlight meaning\nTo sum up… begin the conclusion\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nPresenter: First, let’s look at the current reporting problem.\nPresenter: Updates are detailed, but they are difficult to scan quickly.\nPresenter: This brings me to my second point: a shorter dashboard.\nPresenter: In contrast, a dashboard shows the main information on one page.\nPresenter: The key message here is that format affects how quickly people can act.\nPresenter: To sum up, I recommend short weekly dashboards and one detailed monthly report.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Sequence and contrast linkers; topic sentences.\nSOUND CLEAR Use pauses to mark transitions between sections.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. __________, let’s look at…\n2. This __________ me to my second point.\n3. In __________…\n4. The key __________ here is…\n5. To __________ up…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Use five cards: opening, point 1, point 2, point 3 and conclusion. Mix them before your \npartner speaks.\nPARTNER B Organise the cards and deliver a two-minute mini-presentation.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Present three connected points and a conclusion in three minutes.\nSuccess means: My transitions are audible and my conclusion returns to the main purpose.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Create a one-page presentation map with keywords only. Practise without full \nsentences.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":33,"title":"Explain information and visuals","content":"WEEK 11 • LESSON 33\nExplain information and visuals\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Describe a simple chart, plan or process and state what the audience should \nnotice.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What should you say before reading numbers?\n2. How is description different from interpretation?\n3. Which one trend matters most?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThis visual shows… identify what the audience is seeing\nThe most noticeable change is… highlight the main pattern\nCompared with…, … make a comparison\nApproximately… give a rounded figure\nWhat this means is… interpret the information\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nPresenter: This visual shows the number of completed tasks over four weeks.\nPresenter: The most noticeable change is the increase in Week 3.\nPresenter: Compared with Week 1, the team completed almost twice as many tasks.\nAudience: What was the exact number?\nPresenter: Approximately forty tasks were completed in Week 3.\nPresenter: What this means is that the new planning method may be improving consistency.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Comparatives, quantities and approximation language.\nSOUND CLEAR Say numbers in groups and pause before the key interpretation.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. This visual __________…\n2. The most __________ change is…\n3. __________ with…, …\n4. __________…\n\n\n\n5. What this __________ is…\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Sketch a simple four-week chart with invented, safe data and ask for a description.\nPARTNER B Describe the visual, compare two points and interpret the main message.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Explain one chart, diagram or process for two minutes, then answer a question.\nSuccess means: I identify the visual, select the main pattern and explain why it matters.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Find or draw a simple professional visual. Record a 90-second explanation using \nfour phrases.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":34,"title":"Handle audience questions","content":"WEEK 12 • LESSON 34\nHandle audience questions\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Listen fully, acknowledge the question and give a clear, bounded answer.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. What should you do before answering?\n2. How can you respond when you do not know?\n3. When should you promise a follow-up?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThank you for that question. acknowledge the audience\nIf I understand you correctly, … confirm a complex question\nThe short answer is… begin concisely\nI don’t have that figure with me, but… respond honestly when information is missing\nDoes that answer your question? check satisfaction\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nAudience: How much time would the new process save each week?\nPresenter: Thank you for that question. If I understand you correctly, you’re asking about weekly time \nsavings.\nAudience: Yes, exactly.\nPresenter: The short answer is about two hours per team. I don’t have the final figure with me, but I can \nconfirm it after the meeting.\nAudience: That would be useful.\nPresenter: Of course. Does that answer your question for now?\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Indirect questions, conditionals and repair language.\nSOUND CLEAR Repeat the key word from the question before answering.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. Thank you for that __________.\n2. If I understand you __________, …\n3. The __________ answer is…\n\n\n\n4. I don’t have that __________ with me, but…\n5. Does that __________ your question?\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Ask one clear, one unclear and one unanswerable question about the presentation.\nPARTNER B Acknowledge, clarify if needed, answer honestly and check understanding.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Handle five audience questions without becoming defensive or inventing \ninformation.\nSuccess means: I answer the real question, stay concise and clearly mark any follow-up needed.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Prepare five likely questions for your final presentation and outline a two-sentence \nanswer for each.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":35,"title":"Final interview rehearsal","content":"WEEK 12 • LESSON 35\nFinal interview rehearsal\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Integrate structure, evidence, interaction and fluency in a complete mock \ninterview.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which interview answer still needs the most practice?\n2. Which filler helps you organise without delay?\n3. What evidence do you want the interviewer to remember?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nThat’s an important question. gain a brief moment to organise\nA relevant example would be… move from claim to evidence\nThe result of that action was… complete an evidence story\nTo connect that to this role… make relevance explicit\nIs there any part you’d like me to expand on? invite a follow-up\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nInterviewer: What makes you a strong candidate for this role?\nCandidate: That’s an important question. I believe my planning experience and communication skills are \nespecially relevant.\nInterviewer: Can you give an example?\nCandidate: A relevant example would be a project where I reorganised the task schedule.\nCandidate: The result of that action was an earlier, more accurate submission.\nCandidate: To connect that to this role, I can bring the same structured approach to your project team.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Mixed review based on personal error patterns.\nSOUND CLEAR Use clear sentence stress, natural pace and decisive endings.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. That’s an __________ question.\n2. A __________ example would be…\n3. The __________ of that action was…\n\n\n\n4. To __________ that to this role…\n5. Is there any part you’d like me to __________ on?\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Conduct an eight-question interview. Note one strength and two corrections; do not \ninterrupt answers.\nPARTNER B Answer naturally, ask for clarification when needed and ask two questions at the end.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Complete a 15-minute mock interview with delayed feedback and a second attempt \nat two answers.\nSuccess means: I answer relevantly, support claims, interact naturally and improve after feedback.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Review the recording. Write two exact sentences to keep and two to improve before\nthe final assessment.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n"},{"number":36,"title":"Final showcase and next plan","content":"WEEK 12 • LESSON 36\nFinal showcase and next plan\nTODAY’S OUTCOME Demonstrate progress and create a practical 30-day continuation plan.\n1 | ACTIVATE • 8 MIN\nDiscuss the questions with your tutor or partner. Give a reason or example after each answer.\n1. Which recording best shows your progress?\n2. Which strategy will you keep using?\n3. What is one realistic 30-day goal?\n2 | LANGUAGE BANK • 12 MIN\nUSEFUL EXPRESSION USE IT WHEN YOU…\nAt the beginning of this course… describe the starting point\nThe biggest improvement I’ve noticed is… identify progress\nI can now… state a new ability\nMy next target is… choose a focused goal\nI’ll continue by… commit to a practical action\nLIVE MOVE Choose two expressions. Say one true sentence about yourself with each expression.\n3 | LISTEN, NOTICE AND RESPOND • 10 MIN\nCover the dialogue. Listen as your tutor reads it once. State the main purpose. Uncover it, read \nagain and switch roles.\nTutor: How has your speaking changed?\nStudent: At the beginning of this course, I often stopped when I made a mistake.\nTutor: What is different now?\nStudent: The biggest improvement I’ve noticed is that I can organise my ideas and keep speaking.\nTutor: What will you work on next?\nStudent: My next target is presentation fluency. I’ll continue by recording one short talk every week.\nNotice: underline the opening; circle one follow-up; box the phrase that keeps the speaker moving.\n4 | GRAMMAR AND PRONUNCIATION COACH • 10 MIN\nGRAMMAR FOR THE MISSION Future intentions and review of personal high-frequency errors.\nSOUND CLEAR Compare the baseline and final recordings for pace, pausing and clarity.\n5 | COMPLETE AND PERSONALISE • 10 MIN\nComplete the expressions from memory. Then change two sentences so they are true for you.\n1. At the __________ of this course…\n2. The biggest __________ I’ve noticed is…\n3. I can __________…\n4. My next __________ is…\n5. I’ll __________ by…\n\n\n\n6 | LIVE PARTNER PRACTICE • 12 MIN\nPARTNER A Listen to the final showcase and ask questions about progress, evidence and next \nsteps.\nPARTNER B Deliver a five-minute presentation, answer questions and reflect honestly.\nRound 1: complete the role-play. Round 2: switch roles or change one important detail.\n7 | PERFORMANCE MISSION • 8 MIN\nYOUR MISSION Complete the final portfolio: conversation, interview, presentation and 30-day \nlearning plan.\nSuccess means: I demonstrate progress with evidence and leave with one specific, scheduled next \naction.\n8 | FEEDBACK, REPAIR AND RETRY • 10 MIN\nFinish speaking first. Your tutor records only the corrections that will most improve communication.\nOne sentence I used well:\nOne correction to make:\nOne stronger expression to add:\nRETRY RULE Repeat the mission now. Use the correction and at least two expressions from today’s \nLanguage Bank.\n9 | INDEPENDENT MISSION • 5 MIN\n15–30 MINUTES Complete the 30-day plan at the back of this book and schedule the first three \npractice sessions.\nAfter today’s lesson, I can: ☐ ☐ ☐ Not yet With support Independently\n\n\n\nFinal performance portfolio\nComplete these tasks during Week 12. Use keywords, not full scripts.\nCONVERSATION Maintain a six-minute conversation. Open naturally, ask follow-ups, clarify once \nand close professionally.\nINTERVIEW Complete a 15-minute interview. Include a structured introduction, two STAR answers \nand two questions for the interviewer.\nPRESENTATION Deliver a five-minute professional presentation with three points, one simple visual \nand audience questions.\nWRITING Write a 120–150-word professional follow-up email with a clear purpose, action and \ndeadline.\nSpeaking performance rubric\nAREA 3 — INDEPENDENT 2 — DEVELOPING 1 — NEEDS SUPPORT\nTask Completes the purpose clearly Completes most of the purpose Needs prompts to complete it\nFluency Keeps speaking with manageable \npauses\nSome long pauses but message\ncontinues\nFrequent pauses stop the \nmessage\nInteraction Responds, follows up and clarifies Responds but rarely extends Short answers; needs repeated \nsupport\nLanguage Useful range; errors rarely block \nmeaning\nSimple range; some errors \naffect clarity\nLimited range; errors often \nblock meaning\nPronunciation Generally clear with useful stress Usually understandable with \nsome strain Often difficult to understand\nMy 30-day continuation plan\nMy next speaking target is:\nThe real situation where I will use it is:\nI will practise on these days and times:\nMy weekly speaking partner or tutor will be:\nThe evidence I will collect is:\nAfter 30 days, success will look like:\n\n\n\nLanguage-practice answer key\nThese answers complete Task 5 in each lesson. Personalised sentences and speaking tasks will \nvary.\nLesson 1: Your starting point\n1. moment • 2. mean • 3. repeat • 4. experience • 5. goal\nLesson 2: A strong professional introduction\n1. currently • 2. background • 3. responsible • 4. particularly • 5. current\nLesson 3: Keep speaking without long pauses\n1. reasons • 2. first • 3. another • 4. example • 5. another\nLesson 4: Start conversations naturally\n1. far • 2. brings • 3. before • 4. team • 5. sounds\nLesson 5: Show interest and ask follow-ups\n1. happened • 2. interested • 3. like • 4. mean • 5. relate\nLesson 6: End conversations professionally\n1. speaking • 2. back • 3. touch • 4. details • 5. forward\nLesson 7: Describe your work clearly\n1. responsibility • 2. closely • 3. involves • 4. purpose • 5. simple\nLesson 8: Routines and current priorities\n1. usually • 2. moment • 3. weeks • 4. different • 5. priority\nLesson 9: Give a useful work update\n1. completed • 2. currently • 3. issue • 4. expect • 5. step\nLesson 10: Tell a past experience\n1. months • 2. opportunity • 3. role • 4. useful • 5. taught\nLesson 11: Sequence a professional story\n1. first • 2. while • 3. after • 4. result • 5. end\nLesson 12: Describe success and challenge\n1. challenge • 2. although • 3. decided • 4. worked • 5. again\nLesson 13: Meet international contacts\n1. joining • 2. kind • 3. long • 4. common • 5. perspective\nLesson 14: Clarify and confirm meaning\n1. clarify • 2. correctly • 3. mean • 4. confirm • 5. example\nLesson 15: Phone and video calls\n1. clearly • 2. breaking • 3. slowly • 4. share • 5. summarise\n\n\n\nLesson 16: Describe a workplace problem\n1. noticed • 2. started • 3. affecting • 4. urgent • 5. know\nLesson 17: Suggest and compare solutions\n1. option • 2. possibility • 3. advantage • 4. risk • 5. recommend\nLesson 18: Explain a process step by step\n1. begins • 2. once • 3. next • 4. before • 5. purpose\nLesson 19: Share opinions in meetings\n1. view • 2. reason • 3. instance • 4. see • 5. your\nLesson 20: Agree and disagree politely\n1. agree • 2. add • 3. however • 4. convinced • 5. instead\nLesson 21: Messages and follow-up emails\n1. follow • 2. confirm • 3. discussed • 4. know • 5. assistance\nLesson 22: Answer: Tell me about yourself\n1. currently • 2. before • 3. developed • 4. interests • 5. contribute\nLesson 23: Qualifications and experience\n1. experience • 2. gained • 3. used • 4. prepared • 5. relevant\nLesson 24: Strengths and development areas\n1. strengths • 2. example • 3. rely • 4. developing • 5. improving\nLesson 25: Build a STAR answer\n1. situation • 2. responsibility • 3. action • 4. result • 5. learned\nLesson 26: Present achievements with evidence\n1. proud • 2. specific • 3. improved • 4. outcome • 5. demonstrates\nLesson 27: Discuss challenges and lessons\n1. encountered • 2. realised • 3. responsibility • 4. address • 5. since\nLesson 28: Career goals and motivation\n1. motivated • 2. appeals • 3. hope • 4. develop • 5. allow\nLesson 29: Why should we hire you?\n1. requires • 2. prepared • 3. bring • 4. strong • 5. reasons\nLesson 30: Ask intelligent interview questions\n1. more • 2. describe • 3. success • 4. steps • 5. opportunity\nLesson 31: Open a presentation confidently\n1. joining • 2. today • 3. purpose • 4. cover • 5. begin\nLesson 32: Organise and connect ideas\n1. first • 2. brings • 3. contrast • 4. message • 5. sum\nLesson 33: Explain information and visuals\n1. shows • 2. noticeable • 3. compared • 4. approximately • 5. means\n\n\n\nLesson 34: Handle audience questions\n1. question • 2. correctly • 3. short • 4. figure • 5. answer\nLesson 35: Final interview rehearsal\n1. important • 2. relevant • 3. result • 4. connect • 5. expand\nLesson 36: Final showcase and next plan\n1. beginning • 2. improvement • 3. now • 4. target • 5. continue\n\n\n\nCourse vocabulary record\nRecord expressions you can use—not isolated words. Revisit each expression after 2 days, 7 days \nand 21 days.\nEXPRESSION MY EXAMPLE DAY 2 DAY 7 DAY 21\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n☐ ☐ ☐\n\n\n\nRecommended free practice resources\nBritish Council A2 speaking: https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/speaking/a2\nBritish Council B1 speaking: https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/speaking/b1\nCambridge English learner activities: https://www.cambridgeenglish.org/learning-english/activities-for￾learners/\nCouncil of Europe CEFR Companion Volume: https://rm.coe.int/common-european-framework-of￾reference-for-languages-learning-teaching/16809ea0d4\nDesign note\nThe course uses CEFR-informed, action-oriented speaking tasks. Progress is judged through task \ncompletion, fluency, interaction, language control and intelligibility. All dialogues, activities and \npractice texts in this student book were written specifically for this course."}]}};
